\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  reserves up,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  speculation growing. \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2002\par 06:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external reserves rose for the seventh month in October even though it has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since June, but the dollar's latest decline has made traders nervous in the light of record \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  The country's reserves amounted to $460.975 billion at the end of October, up $250 million from September, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, adding that, as of August, Japan was the world's largest reserves holder for the 35th consecutive month. \par \par \uc2  It said the Japanese monetary authorities conducted no \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the July-September quarter. \par \par \uc2  In April-June they spent a total of 4.0162 trillion yen ($33.14 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen down, the largest amount spent in any quarter since the MOF started disclosing such data in 1991. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the authorities have kept out of the market since June 28, but tension over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is growing as the dollar slumped on Friday to its lowest level against the yen since mid-September. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dipped to a low of 120.81 yen and as of 0452 GMT it stood at 121.02/05 yen , compared with a late U.S. level of 121.08/13 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar turned weak after the U.S. Federal Reserve's surprisingly big half-point rate cut on Wednesday failed to boost Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  "Tension (over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is clearly increasing as the dollar is approaching the 120-yen level," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland (RBS). \par \par \uc2  "I don't think (the Japanese monetary authorities) will wait until the dollar falls to 115 yen," Hanao said. \par \par \uc2  The market believes the Japanese government wants the yen lower because it is one of the most effective ways to shore up the economy at a time when Japan is unable to go much further with monetary or fiscal steps, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen helps exporters by boosting the value of foreign sales. It also helps increase the value of overseas assets held by Japanese financial institutions, which are trying to write off massive amounts of non-performing loans. \par \par \uc2  A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also allow Japanese institutional investors to make huge gains on their foreign securities holdings, which could help improve their earnings. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108dyb8009qe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
