\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Kuroda to meet with market partipants Tues. \b0\par\par\uc2 201 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2002\par 05:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 8 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, and other \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials will meet market participants on Tuesday to discuss developments in global economies and Japan's tax policies, a MOF spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda will talk about developments after the recent meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors, while tax bureau officials will explain the latest tax reform issues, the spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  The meeting, which will include analysts and economists from the private sector, will be held at 4:00 p.m. (0700 GMT). \par \par \uc2  "The objective is to expand our public relations and disseminate information. We will consider holding another meeting in the future if the response is good," the spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  There have not been many meetings between ministry officials and members of the financial community, he added. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said last week that Japan, as well as other nations, could accept a weaker yen if it was the result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflecting fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But he added that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not artificially drive the yen down through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just for the sake of price stability, or in order to address deflation. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021008dya8005ps\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
