\par\par \b \uc2  Sakakibara: `Difficult' For Takenaka To Carry Out Reform \b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2002\par 23:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- If the dollar should appreciate to levels above about Y130, that would prompt "huge resistance from the U.S." government, said Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's former vice minister of finance for international affairs, speaking Monday. \par \par \uc2  He was addressing an academic audience at Columbia University's business school on the subject of "Japan's Unavoidable Financial Crisis." He was responding to a question from the audience about how much the yen would have to weaken before it could have a significant positive effect on Japan's lagging economy. \par \par \uc2  "A natural weakening of the yen as a result of economic fundamentals could be condoned to something like Y130 to Y135," he said, but added that he would oppose "aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting via the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  In his address, Sakakibara, who is now a professor at Japan's Keio University, launched a fierce attack on Japan's current Financial Services Minister Heizo Takenaka, saying that Takenaka has been speaking out of turn by discussing policy options publicly before firm decisions have been made. \par \par \uc2  Because of the vested interests that encircle Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party, "it is very difficult for Takenaka to carry out this mission" of accelerating reforms in Japan's banking sector and broader economy, Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  A defect of the Koizumi administration's reform plans unveiled since a cabinet reshuffle a week ago are that although Takenaka appears committed to addressing the huge problem of Japanese banks' non-performing loans, "corporate restructuring" measures are an "element which seems to be lacking" in the proposals, he said. \par \par \uc2  One possibility in the coming months is that Takenaka will push through aggressive disposals of the banks' non-performing loans, which have been estimated at around Y150 trillion, but without a vigorous shakeup of management at banks and companies and additional measures to ensure genuine reforms, such a step "will probably result in an acute economic and financial crisis in Japan," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  On monetary policy, Sakakibara expressed the view that  "easing without inflation targeting will not be expected." \par \par \uc2  Much of his prepared remarks were devoted to the dangers of implementing "partial policies," such as the half-baked measures to address Japan's banking system in the late 1990s. Sakakibara advised those who are eagerly watching the administration of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's apparently newfound impetus to tackle Japan's serious economic problems to "wait and be objective." \par \par \uc2  He conceded that the appointment of Takenaka does seem to mark "a dramatic shift in policies," but openly fretted that such a change might worsen the crisis Japan faces, not alleviate it. \par \par \uc2  One scenario that might unfold would be that the recently appointed Takenaka might go the way of the man he replaced, Hakuo Yanagisawa, who ran into formidable resistance from the strong industry lobby groups who hold considerable sway over the LDP government, in sectors such as distribution, construction and real estate. \par \par \uc2  "Those industries" Sakakibara said, "are highly politicized." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara urged a multi-pronged approach in an effort to galvanize Japan's economy. He emphasized that the problems Japan faces are not exclusively related to monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "It is hard to believe that liquidity constraints are binding" and holding back Japanese businesses, when the Bank of Japan's short-term interest rates are effectively zero, he said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also stressed the complexity of the situation in which Japan now finds itself given that the world economy is in a worse situation than it was a year ago. The U.S. economic outlook is "to say the least, very uncertain," Sakakibara said, adding that weakness in Europe's biggest economy, Germany, is also a worrying indication of the global economy's current challenges. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara could not resist offering some advice to Takenaka on the potentially tricky subject of how to talk about the problems in Japan's financial sector when speaking in public, because of the potential impact of global capital markets. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened by more than a full yen against the dollar and hit its lowest level against the euro in more than three years after Takenaka openly questioned whether major Japanese banks are "too big to fail," which jolted equity investors as well. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Markets know which banks and companies' stocks to sell when they hear\par such a statement," Sakakibara advised, and added that he hopes Takenaka\par and others in the Koizumi administration "will become more professional"\par in talking about such issues.\par    -By John Parry, Dow Jones Newswires; (201) 938-2096;\par john.parry@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021007dya70016t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
