\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar mired near one-mth low vs euro,Iraq weighs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2002\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The dollar was mired near a one-month low against the euro on Thursday and made no headway against the yen as worries of further Wall Street losses and the United States' stance on Iraq kept it under pressure. \par \par \uc2  Investors were nervous ahead of U.S. jobless claims and service sector data later in the day, with futures markets pointing to another dismal session for U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush won domestic endorsement of his anti-Iraq campaign on Wednesday, but faces stiff opposition at the United Nations to a draft resolution paving the way for possible war. \par \par \uc2  "Both Germany and France have said they won't support a harsher resolution on Iraq which is making Bush appear more isolated," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at West LB. \par \par \uc2  "Political worries, the sell-off in U.S. equities and caution ahead of U.S. data are all weighing on the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to $0.9896 in the European session but failed to pierce tough chart resistance at $0.99, a level last touched in early September. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  clung to a tight range around 122.80 yen after concern over painful Japanese bank reform pushed the benchmark Nikkei stock index to a new 19-year low. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS EYED \par \par \uc2  Futures markets were pointing to a weak open on Wall Street after major U.S. stock indices shed over two percent on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Although stock markets have been tumbling around the world, the dollar tends to be hit harder than its peers because the United States relies heavily on inflows of foreign capital to fund its huge trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  "We are coming into the earnings season and that is still putting pressure on the dollar, but appetite for European equities has waned," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said uncertainty over the global economy and the geopolitical situation was likely to keep major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  confined to tight ranges for now. \par \par \uc2  The euro pushed to a one-week high above 121.50 yen in early European trade, but had pared gains to 121.28 by 0950 GMT. \par \par \uc2  U.S. weekly jobless claims are due at 1230 GMT, ahead of Friday's September employment report. \par \par \uc2  The Institute of Supply Management's non-manufacturing index is due at 1400 GMT, and is forecast to show a slight improvement to 51.4 in September, from 50.9 in August. \par \par \uc2  FOREIGNERS SELL JAPAN STOCKS \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Thursday that foreign investors sold a net 425.5 billion yen ($3.47 billion) of Japanese stocks last week, the second-largest net selling amount since weekly data was first compiled in April 2001. \par \par \uc2  That raises net Japanese equity selling by foreign investors for the whole of September to around 1.4 trillion yen - a level not seen at least in the past several years. \par \par \uc2  Japan's vice minister for international affairs, Haruhiko Kuroda, repeatedly tried to talk down the yen on Wednesday, but the market hardly reacted. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda told the World Leadership Forum in New York that movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - namely the yen's recent weakening - were natural and reasonable. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Kuroda said on a Japanese television programme from New York that Japan, as well as other nations, could accept a weaker yen if it reflected economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But he said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not artificially drive the yen down through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just for the sake of price stability or in order to address ongoing deflation. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021003dya300k42\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
