\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Threat of war, sliding stocks hold dollar in check. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2002\par 09:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The dollar was trapped within recent ranges against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, unable to rise because of concern about a U.S.-led war on Iraq, but finding support from sliding Japanese and European stocks. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush won domestic endorsement of his anti-Iraq campaign on Wednesday, but faced stiff opposition at the United Nations to a draft resolution paving the way for possible war. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks futures fell by more than two percent on Wednesday, after major U.S. firm Dow Chemical said it would miss profit expectations. Tokyo stocks ended at a 19-year low, and European stocks also fell at the open. \par \par \uc2  "We are coming into the earnings season and that is still putting pressure on the dollar, but appetite for European equities has waned," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was virtually unchanged from the U.S. close at 122.80 yen but weakened by a quarter percent towards one-month highs around $0.99 per euro at 0730 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trading was thin due to the Day of German Unity holiday, and ahead of key U.S. employment data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei average closed at a 19-year low on Thursday, with bank shares extending falls amid fears that the government will take tough measures to force them to sort out their bad-loan problem. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei share index ended trade down 1.25 percent at 8,936.43. \par \par \uc2  "With the market still waiting for (Japan's) anti-deflation package, share prices should stay weak and bearish stocks will undermine the yen," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "But for now, the market is also careful about selling the yen too strongly because there are uncertainties about the dollar due to bearish Wall Street stocks and worries over the tensions between the United States and Iraq." \par \par \uc2  British Prime Minister Tony Blair said on Thursday a tougher U.N. resolution was needed on Iraq before weapons inspectors return, and urged the international community to present a united front. \par \par \uc2  U.S. weekly jobless claims are due at 1230 GMT, ahead of Friday's September employment report. \par \par \uc2  The Institute of Supply Management's non-manufacturing index is due at 1400 GMT, and is forecast to show a slight improvement to 51.4 in September, from 50.9 in August. \par \par \uc2  FOREIGNERS SELL JAPAN STOCKS \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Thursday that foreign investors sold a net 425.5 billion yen ($3.47 billion) of Japanese stocks last week, the second-largest net selling amount since weekly data was first compiled in April 2001. \par \par \uc2  That raises net Japanese equity selling by foreign investors for the whole of September to around 1.4 trillion yen - a level not seen at least in the past several years. \par \par \uc2  Official statistics for September will not be available from the ministry until mid-October. \par \par \uc2  Japan's vice minister for international affairs, Haruhiko Kuroda, repeatedly tried to talk down the yen on Wednesday in New York, but the market hardly reacted. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda told the World Leadership Forum that movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - namely the yen's recent weakening - were natural and reasonable. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Kuroda said on a Japanese television programme from New York that Japan, as well as other nations, could accept a weaker yen if it reflected economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But he said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not artificially drive the yen down through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just for the sake of price stability or in order to address ongoing deflation. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021003dya300fs3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
