\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar fails to benefit from capital flow, stocks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2002\par 08:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The dollar failed to rise during late trading on Thursday despite data showing an exodus of foreign investors from the Japanese equity market and Japan's main share index closing at a 19-year low. \par \par \uc2  In the end, buying sentiment for the greenback remained overshadowed by the possibility of a U.S. strike on Iraq. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush won domestic endorsement of his anti-Iraq campaign on Wednesday, but faced stiff opposition at the United Nations to a draft resolution paving the way for possible war. \par \par \uc2  "With the market still waiting for (Japan's) anti-deflation package, share prices should stay weak and bearish stocks will undermine the yen," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "But for now, the market is also careful about selling the yen too strongly because there are uncertainties about the dollar due to bearish Wall Street stocks and worries over the tensions between the United States and Iraq." \par \par \uc2  At 0535 GMT, the greenback was quoted at 122.77/83 yen little changed from the late U.S. close of 122.89/97. \par \par \uc2  The yen stayed weak against the euro, standing at 121.09/18 yen against 121.22/32. \par \par \uc2  The dollar remained under pressure against the European unit, with the euro at 98.64/72 cents compared with the New York level of 98.67/72. \par \par \uc2  NO HELP FROM CAPITAL FLOW \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed that foreign investors sold a net 425.5 billion yen ($3.47 billion) in Japanese stocks last week, the second-largest net selling amount since weekly data was first compiled in April 2001. \par \par \uc2  That raises net Japanese equity selling by foreign investors for the whole of September to around 1.4 trillion yen - a level not seen at least in the past several years. \par \par \uc2  Official statistics for September will not be available from the ministry until mid-October. \par \par \uc2  "In theory, the massive Japanese stock selling by foreigners should favour the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they convert the yen proceeds into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , usually the dollar," said Shuzo Kakuta, senior vice president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  division at Mizuho Corporate Bank. \par \par \uc2  However, he said that the market was not in the mood to bid the dollar up at present in the face of U.S.-Iraq tensions and sliding Japanese equities that could hurt U.S. and European shares as well. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei average closed at a 19-year low on Thursday, with bank shares extending falls amid fears that the government will take tough measures to force them to sort out their bad-loan problem. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei share index ended trade down 1.25 percent at 8,936.43. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors, who hold about 18 percent of existing Japanese stocks, were thought to be still unloading Japanese equities. \par \par \uc2  Heavy selling of Japanese equities by foreign investors also reflected an aversion to risk-taking by global institutional investors as recent sharp declines in U.S. and European equities have dealt a heavy blow to their portfolios. \par \par \uc2  The steep decline on Wall Street also hurt the dollar. On Wednesday, the Dow Jones industrial average fell 2.31 percent to 7,755.61 and the technology-heavy Nasdaq index dropped 2.18 percent to 1,187.30. \par \par \uc2  KURODA TRIES TO TALK DOWN YEN \par \par \uc2  Japan's vice minister for international affairs, Haruhiko Kuroda, repeatedly tried to talk down the yen on Wednesday in New York, but the market hardly reacted. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda told the World Leadership Forum that movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - namely the yen's recent weakening - were natural and reasonable. \par \par \uc2  "If \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements are in line with fundamentals, we should not manipulate," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Kuroda said on a Japanese television programme from New York that Japan, as well as other nations, could accept a weaker yen if it reflected economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But he said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not artificially drive the yen down through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just for the sake of price stability or in order to address ongoing deflation. \par \par \uc2  "Kuroda's comments came in line with expectations, but drew mixed responses as he appeared to welcome the yen's recent weakness but gave the impression that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. ($1=122.75 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021003dya300civ\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
