\par\par \b \uc2  Yen on defensive, but Wall St fall limits losses. \b0\par\par\uc2 569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2002\par 03:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The yen remained on the defensive in Asia on Thursday on growing pessimism about the Japanese economy, but another fall on Wall Street limited its decline. \par \par \uc2  With Japanese share prices slumping and the market still unclear on the shape of the government's planned reforms, the market was unwilling to hold yen positions aggressively. \par \par \uc2  "With the market still waiting for (Japan's) anti-deflation package, share prices should stay weak and bearish stocks would undermine the yen," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "But for now, the market is also careful about selling the yen too strongly because there are uncertainties about the dollar due to bearish Wall Street stocks and worries over the tensions between the United States and Iraq," Hanao said. \par \par \uc2  The steep decline on Wall Street also hurt the dollar on Wednesday. The Dow Jones industrial average fell 2.31 percent to 7,755.61 and the technology-heavy Nasdaq index dropped 2.18 percent to 1,187.30. Although the dollar retained its footing against the yen, it was under pressure against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 2311 GMT, the euro was quoted at 98.64/72 cents compared with a late U.S. level of 98.67/72. Dealers said the euro rose as high as 98.71 cents in overseas trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased against the yen, but it was well supported at 121.07/13 yen against 121.22/32. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 122.75/78 yen against 122.89/97 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  YEN TALKED DOWN \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda repeatedly tried to talk down the yen on Wednesday, although the market showed little reaction. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda told the World Leadership Forum in New York that movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - namely the yen's recent weakening trend - were natural and reasonable. \par \par \uc2  "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements are in line with fundamentals, we should not manipulate," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  Separately Kuroda said on a Japanese television programme from New York that that Japan, as well as other nations, could accept a weaker yen if it was the result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflecting economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But he said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not artificially drive the yen down through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just for the sake of price stability or in order to address ongoing deflation. \par \par \uc2  "Kuroda's comments came in line with expectations, but drew mixed responses as he appeared to welcome the recent yen's weakness but gave an impression that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "But we all know that the Japanese authorities want a weaker yen," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to be range-bound in Tokyo ahead of U.S. payrolls data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is seen moving in a range of 122.50 to 123.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  The greenback is expected to by supported by firm buying interest from Japanese institutional investors on dips, while plenty of sell orders are rumoured to be lined up around 123 yen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  All \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  news ... Debt..