\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-MOF Kato: Japan Will "Act" If Yen Rises On Bank Plan \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2002\par 14:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if hopes of an aggressive banking sector cleanup cause the yen to surge, a former top Finance Ministry bureaucrat said Friday. \par \par \uc2  If the yen strengthens on signs of a faster, but also more deflationary, approach to ridding banks of their bad loans, "then the market's not reflecting fundamentals," said Takatoshi Kato, a former vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities will act in such a case," Kato, now an advisor to the Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. \par \par \uc2  Kato said Japan and its banking sector problems may not feature too prominently at the meeting of top Group of Seven finance officials later Friday as other issues on the agenda take center stage. But Japan's banking system, entrenched deflation - and remedies Tokyo has in mind - will certainly be an issue when Japan and the U.S. meet bilaterally, Kato said. \par \par \uc2  The G7 meeting in Washington will likely address global economic surveillance, the outlook for the U.S. economy, crude oil and stock prices, and emerging markets, said the former bureaucrat and regular attendant of such meetings. \par \par \uc2  "But it would be best to brace for the U.S. pressing Japan for an explanation on its progress in banking sector reform," when Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa meets U.S. counterpart Paul O'Neill ahead of the G7, Kato said. \par \par \uc2  Kato said it might be best to keep an eye out on how the G7 communique, to be released after the meeting, refers to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That's because Japanese authorities would like the statement to appear that the G7 is tacitly approving a weaker yen, which would help support Japan's export-led recovery and ease deflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  "Japan would like it to appear that other nations don't object to yen's weakening in a correction from previous excessive strength," even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reference is kept unchanged, Kato said. \par \par \uc2  The G7 statement in April said: "We will continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate." \par \par \uc2  Kuroda Meant Deflation-Fighting In Japan Hard \par \par \uc2  Kato also said he believes that Haruhiko Kuroda, current vice finance minister for international affairs, wasn't necessarily advocating the use of "massive" yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an anti-deflation step, when Kuroda mentioned the possibility earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Taken in context, a controversial speech Kuroda gave to academics and businessmen appears to have merely pointed out the various steps Japan could take to fight deflation - such as monetary, government debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , policy - and highlighting the difficulties of each one, Kato said. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda jolted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with the Sept. 13 speech by saying that if the Bank Of Japan was "constrained," as it says it is, in stabilizing prices, the government would have a couple of options. \par \par \uc2  One choice, albeit unpalatable, would be to "use exchange-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for price stability," Kuroda said in the speech. \par \par \uc2  "If the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authority sells the yen and buys the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a massive way, it could depreciate the yen and stop price deflation," said Kuroda, the government's point man in setting Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda added a "serious rethinking" of debt, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policies "may be called for" in the fight against deflation. \par \par \uc2  The words shocked market players, unused to such strong talk for a Finance Ministry bureaucrat, and sent the dollar on rallying more than two yen. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda told reporters later that day his remarks were "theoretical." But they were regarded as unusually blunt for a senior bureaucrat - especially as he openly discussed the government trespassing onto the purview of the independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Kato maintained that what Kuroda was really saying was that "for Japan, it's not easy to take steps to fight deflation." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020927dy9r000t2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
