\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-MOF Sakakibara: Dlr May Fall Below Y120 In Two Weeks \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2002\par 07:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Speculation that Japan's Finance Ministry will try to drive the yen lower to spur an economic recovery will likely prove to be groundless, former top finance official Eisuke Sakakibara said Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said expectations that Japan might combine a weak yen policy with aggressive structural reforms in a potent policy mix aimed at jumpstarting the economy would be dashed, predicting the dollar will likely fall below Y120 in the next two weeks as investors take a reality check and unwind their dollar-long positions. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think that (speculation) will turn out to be true," the former vice finance minister for international affairs told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. \par \par \uc2  But if the dollar plunges below the psychologically important Y120 level to as far as Y115, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  "They will probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around Y115. Maybe above Y115, maybe below Y115, I don't know," he said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, known as "Mr. Yen" for his influence over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during his tenure at the ministry, said that MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of trying to stem a rapid appreciation of the yen hasn't changed. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market seven times between May 22 and June 28, selling Y4.016 trillion of yen, a record-high \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amount for a three-month period, to keep the surging yen from snuffing out the nation's nascent export-led economic recovery. Since the end of June, the dollar has traded in a range of Y115.50-Y124.04. \par \par \uc2  Both the U.S. and Japanese monetary authorities are thought to be comfortable with the dollar's recent trading range in the Y122s and Y123s. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month, U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill said the dollar's level around Y123 was "reasonable," while rhetoric from Japanese finance ministry officials for a weak yen has eased recently. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, Sakakibara projected the dollar will continue to hug a Y115-Y125 range "for a while." \par \par \uc2  "The situation is so volatile, so it's very difficult to put up a medium-term forecast. An attack on Iraq, a possible double-dip in the U.S. and deterioration of the economy in Japan, so we have all kinds of uncertain factors," he said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have "read too much" into the Sept. 13 statement released by Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said then that if the Bank of Japan were "constrained," as it says it is, in stabilizing prices, the government would have a couple of options. One choice, albeit unpalatable, Kuroda told academics and businessmen, would be to "use exchange-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for price stability." \par \par \uc2  "If the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authority sells the yen and buys the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a massive way, it could depreciate the yen and stop price deflation," said Kuroda, the government's point man in setting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said Kuroda's statement was likely meant to put pressure on the BOJ to take more steps to fight deflation, rather than signaling a shift in the finance ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "I talked with Kuroda after the speech and he said he hasn't changed anything," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  This weekend's meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations is unlikely to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  much, Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said Japan might tell the G7 it will take aggressive structural reforms in order to fight deflation and stimulate the nation's shaky economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  But he added that anti-deflation steps now under consideration by the Japanese government, including a possible injection of public funds into banks, are the "same old story." \par \par \uc2  "It's a repetition of the same thing. The government has injected some public funds (into banks) since 1998, but the problem is that that hasn't been accompanied by restructuring of the borrowers," Sakakibara said. "The same situation prevails again." \par \par \uc2  Any infusion of public funds into Japan's ailing banks would only be a temporary measure of providing extra capital to banks, he added. \par \par \uc2  "This is a sort of subsidy to the banks. It temporarily sustains the banks and the balance sheets," Sakakibara said. "Unless it is accompanied by very dramatic restructuring on the part of the borrowers in industries like distribution, construction and real estate, an infusion of public money doesn't have any real sustainable impact." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said he doesn't see any signs the Japanese government is planning on taking any major steps to restructure financially unhealthy corporate borrowers. \par \par \uc2  "It's the same sort of paradigm. They haven't really changed their patterns of thinking. It's the same party," he said. "The likelihood of the government acting dramatically from past (experience) is very low. I don't trust this government." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said that Japan's current administration, headed by Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, doesn't have the political will to change the existing old guard mentality gripping the ruling Liberal Democratic Party. \par \par \uc2  "We really need to change this system of 'vested interests.' The vested interests are so strong, we gotta break that. That's the key," he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par ron.harui@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020927dy9r000a5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
