\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ stock move unlikely to match Hong Kong success. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dominic Lau \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2002\par 09:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Controversial stock purchases by the Hong Kong government in 1998 show \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in equity markets can work, but analysts say the Bank of Japan's proposal to buy shares from banks is likely to meet with less success. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's financial system was solid even as speculators hammered away at its markets at the height of the Asian financial crisis, and the government's US$15 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quick and decisive. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy, on the other hand, has been shaky for over a decade, and countless reform and stimulus packages over the years have failed to revive its markets or rekindle its economic clout. \par \par \uc2  "In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market quite a few times but not with much success. I don't think the chances for success for the Bank of Japan are as high as the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  had," Nilesh Jasani, regional equity strategist at HSBC Securities, said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it would buy shares directly from the nation's banks, putting its credibility on the line in an attempt to shore up the creaking financial system. \par \par \uc2  The plan is the most daring in the region since the Hong Kong government, acting through its de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , snapped up HK$118.1 billion of blue chips in a two-week long \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Hong Kong acted to ward off speculative attacks on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar and on its stock market. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually acts according to government instructions, whereas the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has far greater autonomy. \par \par \uc2  BRIEF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  "In Hong Kong's case, it was quite different because the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to support the market only for a very brief period and to fight off speculators," Jasani said. \par \par \uc2  While some doomsayers at the time decried the government's interference in the markets, there was no arguing it paid off handsomely, stopping speculators in their tracks and creating an eventual windfall for public coffers. \par \par \uc2  The government's Tracker Fund , which was set up in late 1999 to slowly release the shares back onto the market, will hold its final sale next month. \par \par \uc2  Once the final HK$6 billion sale is completed, the government will be in profit to the tune of about HK$89.6 billion. \par \par \uc2  "I think the BOJ will be looking closely at what Hong Kong did in terms of setting up a Tracker Fund," said Robert Subbaraman, regional economist at Lehman Brothers in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Subbaraman stressed the economic circumstances for Hong Kong and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were very different. \par \par \uc2  "At the time Hong Kong did it, its economic fundamentals were generally sound. The attacks put pressure on the exchange rate, but it had a sound banking sector... no external debts. It had ample reserves," he said. \par \par \uc2  "In Japan, on the other hand, the fundamentals are very weak ... large amount of public debts, consumption extremely weak, ageing population. I don't think this in itself will be enough to revive investor sentiment in Japan," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said the Japanese government needs to follow through with economic reforms to revive the ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  MARKET INVOLVEMENT NOT NEW IN ASIA \par \par \uc2  While some central banks routinely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, official support for stock and bond markets is much rarer, albeit less so in Asia since the 1997/98 financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea bought bonds held by banks to increase their liquidity in late 1997 when failures of merchant banks froze the money market. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, four large government pension funds often support the stock market when the government deems it necessary and two years ago, amid sabre-rattling by rival mainland China, the government set up a separate T$500 billion stock stabilisation fund to counter "non-economic" factors. \par \par \uc2  The stock stabilisation fund alone lost up to T$70 billion at one point, igniting fierce political debate on the wisdom of using public funds to support the markets market. \par \par \uc2  It came close to breaking even earlier this year, but recent losses in equity markets probably sent it into red again. \par \par \uc2  (US$=HK$7.8). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020919dy9j00g7e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
