\par\par \b \uc2  Yen little moved despite Nikkei surge, puzzled by BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2002\par 02:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0024 GMT - Yen showing little reaction to Nikkei's surge by over four percent as market ponders what to make of Bank of Japan's plans to buy shares directly from banks. - While such action is likely to help struggling Japanese banks and in turn could benefit the economy, it may lead to yen selling on worries of deterioration in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's assets. - This uncertainty is making traders look for chances to lighten positions. - They said market basically long on dollars and looking to close such positions should the greenback rise above 122 yen. - But underlying sentiment bullish for the greenback after recent remarks by Japanese Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda and U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill. - Kuroda said last Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could end deflation in theory through large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but added that the idea was unlikely to be easily accepted internationally. - O'Neill said on Tuesday the dollar's recent trading ranges against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed reasonable. \par \par \uc2   - Dollar at 121.67 yen against 121.93 in late U.S. Stop-loss orders detected around 121.20 yen. - Euro steady at 97.64 cents . - Single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 118.77 yen against 118.80. - Nikkei share average up 4.13 percent at 9,863.48. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020919dy9j001e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
