\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL YIELD: JGB Dive On BOJ News Is A Salutary Warning \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,011 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2002\par 21:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Proof that Japan can be one of the world's most treacherous sovereign debt markets came earlier Wednesday when a surprise Bank of Japan plan to buy equities from commercial banks gave Japanese government bond yields their biggest daily jump in more than three years. Bond yields and prices move inversely. \par \par \uc2  "It is particularly difficult to get markets right in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," because of the high degree of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Amy Falls, global bond strategist with Morgan Stanley in New York. \par \par \uc2  Making the right bets on Japanese assets is made even trickier now by the pressing need for policy actions, Falls said, as the world's second biggest economy seeks to prevent the shoots of an increasingly delicate economic recovery from withering on the vine. These factors create "a higher degree of volatility" than in other major government bond markets, Falls said. \par \par \uc2  The whipsaw price action serves as a reminder that policy twists in Japan's deflation-dogged economy can spring snares at any time. \par \par \uc2  And the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest, highly unusual proposal has perplexed U.S.-based global strategists, who remain skeptical that such a stop-gap measure for Japanese stocks, currently hovering near 19-year lows, can do much to reflate the moribund economy. \par \par \uc2  "The basic plan is to buy shares directly from the banks, not through the market," BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami told a hastily convened news conference earlier Wednesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will buy the shares at market prices and aims to hold them for 10 years or so. \par \par \uc2  After the announcement, the benchmark 10-year JGB's yield - which moves inversely to its price - had jumped to 1.24% early Wednesday in New York from 1.06% the prior session, a huge leap. \par \par \uc2  While market analysts had been expecting the BOJ to unveil new measures designed to reignite economic growth, they had thought it likely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would undertake quantitative easing by stepping up its purchases of JGBs. When instead, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned its attention unexpectedly to stocks, capital market participants were wrong footed. \par \par \uc2  Long-Term Effectiveness? \par \par \uc2  Analysts based outside Japan expressed doubts the proposal would be effective over the longer term in addressing a central question: the ailing health of the country's banks, an Achilles heel of Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  The main purpose of the BOJ's surprise tactic is indeed to tend to the limping banking sector, which is crippled by some Y150 trillion of bad debts and potentially non-performing loans. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ initiative "is meant to bail out the banking system," said Jan Poser, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Bank Sarasin in Zurich, Switzerland. "But this is really the opposite of what central banks should be doing," he said. "Instead they should leave up to the markets what markets think about stocks," adding that Japan "would be better off to make an outright recapitalization" of the banks. \par \par \uc2  But via its planned measures, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is attempting a "feat" by "moving into fiscal policy, degrading its balance sheet, and calling into question its political independence," wrote Marshall Gittler, a strategist with Bank of America in Tokyo in a special report Wednesday. "It is truly a radical shift in both monetary policy and the nation's treatment of the banking sector  problems." \par \par \uc2  Many U.S.-based fund managers have been warning over the past year that threat of greater government debt issuance to fund a bailout of tottering banks' creditors creates sharp downside risks to JGB prices. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japan's slipping economy - which some economists expect will retreat into contraction by the last quarter - is adding to widespread expectations that the nation's debt-to-gross domestic product ratio, already at a hefty 140%, will continue rising. \par \par \uc2  Yet few could have predicted that such a harsh blow to the JGB market would come not from the country's burgeoning debt levels but would instead be dealt by the maverick BOJ plan. One reason why the proposal shocked so many market participants is that it appears somewhat out of character for Governor Hayami, whose avoidance of an inflation-targeting policy has given him the appearance of a conservative. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Risk Of Side Effects\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Paradoxically, Hayami's key reason for shirking inflation targeting is\par to avoid a crash of JGB prices that would erode the value of major\par Japanese banks' extensive domestic fixed-income holdings, observes\par Poser.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But one undesired side effect of the stock purchase plan could be to\par precipitate that scenario, as domestic investors hungry for returns\par shift into equities and out of the JGB market in the hopes that massive\par BOJ buying will indirectly lift share prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese banks stock holdings are between Y24 and Y25 trillion, while\par the BOJ is capable of purchasing some Y17 trillion of that total, wrote\par Jake Moore, a strategist with Barclays Capital in London in a research\par note Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But if the motivation is to clean out banks' balance sheets to get\par them in a position to start lending again, there is a risk that the plan\par could backfire given that the side-effect of lower JGB prices could more\par than offset the improved cash positions from buying their stock\par holdings. Japanese banks "are believed to hold more Japanese government\par bonds than they do equities" says Marc Chandler, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist with HSBC in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is considering such a tactic shows\par policymakers "are really desperate," Bank Sarasin's Poser said. Because\par economic activity in Japan is still not creating sufficiently strong\par price pressures, pushing up bond yields is one of the few available\par mechanisms to attempt to stoke them.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's government and central bankers "are caught between Scylla and\par Charybdis: they fear a bond market crash, but they also are seeking some\par way out of the deflation crisis", Poser said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires; john.parry@dowjones.com;\par 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Hiroshi Inoue in Tokyo contributed to this report)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4083 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020918dy9i00asy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
