\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: Talk Of Japan Yen Selling Overblown (Probably) \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2002\par 09:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- For more than a year, Japanese monetary officials\par have been very public in their efforts to support the dollar against the\par yen, engaging in unprecedented yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at times to keep\par the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from rising.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  So what does it mean when a top official suddenly raises the issue of using \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to affect prices? \par \par \uc2  It depends how you look at it. From a straight market perspective, there's nothing particularly new about Japan discussing yen sales. Haruhiko Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, was touching on long-standing issues last Friday when he fretted about deflation and pondered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Every Japanese official, including Kuroda, wants to push up the dollar-yen - everybody knows about this already," said Ryuichi Atsuta, head of trading at Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  In a speech to academics and businessmen, Kuroda said, "If the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authority sells the yen and buys the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a massive way, it could depreciate the yen and stop price deflation." \par \par \uc2  The comments stirred the imaginations of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market traders around the world. The dollar surged to a 12-week high of Y122.88 in Tokyo Tuesday, its highest since Y122.79 set on June 24. At 0700 GMT, the dollar was trading around Y122.68. \par \par \uc2  Few observers in Tokyo really believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would dare to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a blatant effort to reflate its sagging economy. At a time when the global economy remains under pressure, the political costs of such a strategy would be too great - especially as Japan's global partners are growing increasingly frustrated over the government's inability to resolve the bad-loan problems in the banking sector. \par \par \uc2  "Drastic banking sector reforms are needed to provide room for the Group of Seven (industrialized nations) to accept a weak yen policy," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, proposing a hypothetical case for using a weaker yen to stop deflation is a new policy twist that seems to show the finance ministry is losing patience with the Bank of Japan. In his comments Friday, Kuroda noted that if the BOJ were "constrained," as it says it is, in stabilizing prices, the government would have an option, albeit an unpalatable one, of using \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for "price stability." \par \par \uc2  Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Tokyo, said, "I was quite shocked. While his comments were academic and theoretical, the fact that he said them was significant and scared people into thinking (the MOF) might sell the yen." \par \par \uc2  Jesper Koll, chief Japan economist at Merril Lynch, said in a research report Tuesday that Kuroda's remarks seem to show the MOF is embarking on a new, aggressive campaign to end deflation. \par \par \uc2  "It is, in my opinion, the equivalent of MOF's declaration of independence: MOF is now ready to take up the fight against deflation through its own monetary policy tools (without waiting for the BOJ)," Koll said. \par \par \uc2  The timing of Kuroda's comments was significant. The remarks came just before the BOJ's policy board was scheduled to gather for a regular monthly meeting. Some insiders say the external pressure on the BOJ could prompt the board to adjust policy this week - even though the central bankers realize that there would be little impact from minor adjustments, since short-term interest rates are already at zero and the banking system is overflowing with cash. \par \par \uc2  A source close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday that the BOJ's thinking is: "not only the Japanese government but also the U.S. government won't be satisfied unless we do something." \par \par \uc2  Koll encouraged asset managers to rethink their Japan positions. \par \par \uc2  "It should mark a turning point in yen asset markets: the yen should depreciate, and once it becomes clear that the fight is for real, both Japanese government bond yields and corporate earnings should rise," Koll said. \par \par \uc2  Other analysts warned that market participants seem to be getting ahead of themselves. \par \par \uc2  "Those remarks were (Kuroda's) personal opinion. They were theoretical speculation on what Japan needs to do; not on what it will do," said Ron Leven, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in Tokyo. "He's trying to ramp the market up with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  "The subtext (of Kuroda's remarks) is an expression of frustration with the BOJ. He's trying to put pressure on the BOJ to do more to stop deflation," Leven said. \par \par \uc2  In his speech, Kuroda said the BOJ could expand its Japanese government bond purchases, set a positive inflation target or both. If the bank can't or won't take such measures, the government could "decisively" shift debt issuance from longer to short maturities, he said. \par \par \uc2  Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has complained about the BOJ's handling of the economy off and on ever since Masaru Hayami became governor, it's very rare for ministry officials to make explicit policy proposals. \par \par \uc2  Koll said, "From my perspective, the chances are rising that a reflationary monetary regime shift is occurring." \par \par \uc2  Koll added that his personal target for the dollar is now Y125-Y130 by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par ron.harui@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020917dy9h000ej\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
