\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits 3-mo high on easing Iraq fears, Kuroda. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 822 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2002\par 08:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The dollar leapt to a three-month high against the yen on Tuesday on news that Iraq agreed to unconditionally re-admit U.N. arms inspectors, easing fears of an imminent U.S. strike on Saddam Hussein's regime. \par \par \uc2  Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, who said the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was moving in the "appropriate" direction, also supported the dollar and kept the yen under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Iraq's acceptance was good for those who wanted to cut short dollar positions since it looks like a war with Iraq will not take place for a while," said Jun Kitazawa, assistant vice president at Brown Brothers Harriman in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But the White House's cool response - calling Iraq's offer an evasive tactic and saying a U.N. Security Council resolution requiring Iraq to disarm was still needed - suggested an eventual strike remained a possibility, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's unclear if the inspection process will go smoothly. The U.S. may attack Iraq eventually, and that could put a cap on the dollar," one dealer at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0530 GMT, the dollar was at 122.74/77 yen , slightly off a three-month high of 122.85 hit earlier. It fetched 122.13 in late New York. U.S. stock futures were also up and Tokyo's Nikkei average leapt 3.27 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's latest rise had forced many dealers to change their views that the dollar is essentially range-bound. Until last week, most traders saw the dollar-yen in 116-121 yen range. \par \par \uc2  "The market's atmosphere has clearly changed," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Itochu Corp, adding that the dollar could test 125 yen soon. \par \par \uc2  Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley, added: "I think the dollar-yen range was moved up to 120-125 yen."But \par \par \uc2  Takashi Toyahara, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Nomura Securities, said he did not think the yen was in for a repeat of its steep slide against the dollar last December. "I don't feel the yen will weaken as much as it did last December," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro slipped to 96.55/60 cents from late U.S. level of 96.90. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 118.49 yen against 118.48 yen. \par \par \uc2  FIGHTING DEFLATION \par \par \uc2  Yen sentiment turned bearish on Friday after Kuroda said large-scale yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could, in theory, help end deflation. But he also said the idea was unlikely to be easily accepted internationally. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers think Japan is unlikely to wade into such a radical policy for now considering the difficulty of gaining an international acceptance and that Kuroda is simply trying to talk down the yen. \par \par \uc2  "This comment was clearly intended to be a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Morgan Stanley's Umemoto. "It showed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants a weaker yen." \par \par \uc2  Some traders speculated that Kuroda made the remarks to weaken the yen ahead of the half-year book closing at the end of the month so Japanese exporters could book overseas profits at a better rate. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, following-up his comments, said on Tuesday: "The market's movement shows it is functioning properly after getting through the anniversary of September 11 without any trouble. The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is moving in the appropriate direction." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan (BOJ) began a two-day monthly board meeting on Tuesday facing pressure to take more aggressive easing steps to arrest three years of deflation. \par \par \uc2  Some Japanese politicians want the BOJ to buy stocks, or exchange traded funds (ETFs), a move that many analysts see as unlikely since it would require a change in legislation. \par \par \uc2  Many market players expect the BOJ to keep its policy unchanged but some players think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may come up with some sort of minor tuning, such as increasing the amount of its purchase of long-term bonds, to soothe politicians. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0602 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 16 Sep.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9651   0.9685   -0.35    +8.39   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen               122.65   122.28   +0.30    -6.82   131.63\par  Euro/yen              118.40   118.47   -0.06    +0.99   117.24\par  Dlr/swiss             1.5239   1.5153   +0.57    -8.17   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr               1.5353   1.5404   -0.33    +5.58   1.4541\par  Can/dlr               1.5822   1.5813   +0.06    -0.60   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr               0.5489   0.5476   +0.24    +7.50   0.5106\par  Euro/swiss            1.4715   1.4685   +0.20    -0.44   1.4780\par  Euro/stg              0.6287   0.6287   +0.00    +2.68   0.6123.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020917dy9h00dw9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
