\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-ANALYSIS-BOJ stock fund buying could backfire. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 887 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2002\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Persuading the Bank of Japan (BOJ) to buy stock funds to prop up an ailing Tokyo stock market, as proposed by some senior government politicians, would risk undermining the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility and further dent confidence in yen assets, senior analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The idea of using purchases of exchange traded funds (ETFs) to support share prices has been raised by coalition parties, who want the government to use public pension money to buy them after the Nikkei share average hit 19-year lows last week. \par \par \uc2  The debate has now turned to whether the BOJ should proceed. \par \par \uc2  Government ministers are cool on the idea of using pension money. Financial Services Minister Hakuo Yanagisawa said on Tuesday it would be appropriate for public pension funds to buy ETFs, but not as part of any government programme to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  ETFs, relatively new in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are index-linked investment funds that are listed on a stock exchange and traded like ordinary shares. They give investors exposure to a stock index, or particular sectors such as technology or banks. \par \par \uc2  Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said on Monday the BOJ should consider new measures like purchasing stock funds to ease financial conditions in an economy smothered by years of asset deflation and a banking system crippled by bad loans. \par \par \uc2  WOULD IT BE LEGAL? \par \par \uc2  It is unclear, however, whether such stock buying by the BOJ would be legal. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami, asked about the proposals for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy ETFs, told reporters on Tuesday: "I am not aware of it. We won't be able to do it." \par \par \uc2  In any case any purchases of ETFs would be a drop in the ocean compared with other regular BOJ liquidity injections into the banking system, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchase of exchange traded funds must not be allowed because it would inevitably degrade market confidence in the BOJ," said Koji Shimamoto, chief strategist at BNP Paribas in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Falling market confidence in the BOJ would eventually deal a heavy blow to the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bonds and stocks," he said, adding that such unconventional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fragile equity market also risked damaging the bank's balance sheet. \par \par \uc2  Whether the BOJ could legally buy stocks through traded funds is unclear, as there is no direct mention of stocks or ETFs in the law governing its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  If it is legal, some analysts believe that either public pension funds or the BOJ may decide to buy the ETFs to prop up the sagging share market in a run-up to the end of September half-year book closing for Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  Some BOJ watchers say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself set a precedent for such unconventional measures by adopting the "quantitative easing" policy early last year to replace traditional monetary tools such as interest rate adjustment. \par \par \uc2  By extending its policy tools, the BOJ has laid itself open to politicians wanting the bank to buy a variety of unconventional instruments that it has never purchased before, including ETFs, foreign bonds and real estate. \par \par \uc2  "My impression is that the BOJ now cares less about maintaining its credibility, but is rather willing to do unconventional things in the name of national interest," said Masuhisa Kobayashi, Japan chief fixed income strategist at Merrill Lynch Japan Securities. \par \par \uc2  However, he added, putting its credibility at risk would in the long run be against the national interest. \par \par \uc2  NOT ENOUGH ETFs AROUND \par \par \uc2  Others also oppose a BOJ shift to buying riskier assets like stock funds. \par \par \uc2  "It would be out of the question," said Kagehide Kaku, deputy chairman of Daiwa Institute of Research. \par \par \uc2  Public pension funds should not take risks with public funds and the BOJ, as a public body, should not take part in a campaign to shore up stock prices, he said. \par \par \uc2  An official at the Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare said public pension funds have never been used for purchasing ETFs and the ministry has not received any such request. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the purpose of the ETF proposal could be either to improve stock price control or to further boost liquidity injection by the BOJ into the banking system. Whatever the aim, the size of the ETF market would limit its effectiveness. \par \par \uc2  "If it were aimed at pumping more liquidity into the banking system to shore up the economy, it would be sheer nonsense," Kaku of Daiwa Research said. \par \par \uc2  Only 1.9 trillion yen ($16.02 billion) worth of ETFs have been issued and only a few financial institutions hold them. \par \par \uc2  "The amount of ETFs in the market is too small to become an effective target of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by either the BOJ or the pension funds," said Atsushi Mizuno, chief fixed income strategist and chief economist for Japan at Deutsche Securities \par \par \uc2  Either as a direct target or as collateral for the BOJ's market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the amount of ETFs is next to nothing compared with other instruments that BOJ uses to pump liquidity into the banking system like Japanese government bonds (JGBs). \par \par \uc2  To put the amount of ETFs in perspective, the BOJ held 53 trillion yen ($446 billion) of JGBs as of the end of July, adding another 1.0 trillion yen worth each month. \par \par \uc2  ($1=118.93 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020910dy9a00l03\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
