\par\par \b \uc2  Surprising Japanese Stock Weakness Sinks U.S. Shares,  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,143 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2002\par 01:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Depressed by sliding U.S. equities and further signs of tepid growth in the U.S. manufacturing sector, the dollar skidded across the board Tuesday, with the euro climbing back within striking distance of parity and the yen jumping more than 1%, despite a slide by Japanese stocks to 19-year lows. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday in New York, the dollar was trading at 117.12 yen, down from 118.16 yen late Monday in London and 118.61 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was changing hands at 99.62 U.S. cents, up sharply from 98.29 cents late Monday in London and 98.11 cents late Friday in New York. Sterling was changing hands at $1.5625, up from $1.5486 on Monday in London and $1.5492 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The onslaught on the dollar, which began during Asian trading, appeared to stem somewhat ironically on the losses in Japan's benchmark 225-issue Nikkei Stock Average, which tumbled 3.2% to end at its weakest point since Sept. 19, 1983. That decline helped drive hefty losses on European stock markets, in turn, driving down global risk appetite and sparking talk once again of a retreat from global equities, which typically tends to weigh on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The plunge by U.S. equity markets served only to exacerbate negative dollar sentiment: The Dow Jones Industrial Average ended down 4.1%, with the Nasdaq Composite losing 3.8%. "U.S. equities tend to lead other markets and the dollar seems to be correlated with equity markets," said Larry Kantor, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at J.P. Morgan in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is also suffering from other factors, several of which are more psychological than fundamental. For one, September is historically the worst month of the year for U.S. equities, which has created the potential for a self-fulfilling prophecy of sorts among global investors. What's more, the dollar is perceived as directly vulnerable in the lead-up to the anniversary of the Sept. 11 attacks. \par \par \uc2  "That's seen as a U.S.-centric risk," Mr. Kantor said. "It's making people very nervous." Those jitters are only accentuated by continued speculation as to when the U.S. might invade Iraq to oust Saddam Hussein. \par \par \uc2  On the more fundamental side, there is also little for dollar bulls to sink their teeth into. After a major selloff during Asian and European trade, the key catalyst for further dollar weakness during the New York day was the closely watched August manufacturing index from the Institute for Supply Management, which showed a reading of 50.5, unchanged from July. \par \par \uc2  Although the ISM reading was only modestly weaker than expected, the prior month's report had already shown a sharp decline and August's data appeared to confirm a continuation of that pattern. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders read the report as "a pretty disappointing number," said Lara Rhame, senior economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. In particular, she cited the decline of the new orders index to 49.7 in August from 50.4 in July as more feeble than expected. \par \par \uc2  Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston, said the 4% loss by the Dow industrials potentially paves the way for the euro to get to parity soon. Yet the key litmus test this week around which dollar sentiment is likely to revolve is Friday's nonfarm payrolls report for August. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's decline was broad-based and steep, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening to as low as 99.75 cents against the euro -- within spitting distance of parity again -- while the pound surged to another one-month high. \par \par \uc2  The yen's gains left some analysts puzzled meanwhile, particularly as they came amid the Nikkei's slide and reports last week that showed Japan's economic recovery in the first half of the year was far weaker than previously thought and the outlook for industrial production, a key prop for growth, is unexpectedly shaky. \par \par \uc2  "There is obviously a weaker dollar story," but even against that backdrop, the yen's strong performance is "intriguing," said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast financial consultancy in New York. "You would have thought that the yen would have dragged its feet more than it has." \par \par \uc2  Seasonal flows into Japanese assets driven by domestic companies are potentially one factor helping the yen, said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with IDEAglobal in New York. Traders' efforts to front-run those shifts tend to prop up the yen particularly in the weeks preceding Japan's fiscal year close on March 31 and half-year end on Sept. 30. \par \par \uc2  Yet the effect seems to be more powerful "in times of stress," such as now, when balance sheets come under more pressure from sagging stock indices, said Mr. Callow. Japanese banks in particular have extensive equity holdings. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists expect that in the next few weeks the yen could firm further, with the dollar weakening to around 115 yen, once again raising the specter of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But in the longer term, doubts about the fragile nature of Japan's economic recovery will return to weigh once more on the yen. \par \par \uc2  Sterling's strong showing against the dollar meanwhile came despite comments by British Prime Minister Tony Blair that the U.K. government hasn't made any decision yet on euro membership or any date for the referendum it has promised on the issue. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Blair's comments may have helped quash press reports that the government had shelved plans for a referendum due to public opposition, reports that helped the pound to rally last week. But sterling also benefited from his comments that the U.N. must be the route to tackle and not avoid the Iraq issue. Mr. Blair also reiterated his view that the U.S. shouldn't have to act alone against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Despite his support for a U.S.-led attack on Iraq, the pound hasn't tended to suffer from the same political risk connotations. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Brazilian real weakened to close at 3.100 reals to the dollar, after Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Arminio Fraga said an adverse global environment was hampering a further reduction in the bank's short-term benchmark rate of 18%. However, the real was supported somewhat later in the session by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars for reals and also by the sale of $50 million of export credits. \par \par \uc2  Write to Grainne McCarthy at grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com and John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Terry Wade and Adriana Arai in Sao Paulo contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020903dy93007k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
