\par\par \b \uc2  Experts Blast BOJ Policy But Can't Agree On Alternatives \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 990 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2002\par 03:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Joseph Rebello \par \par \uc2  JACKSON HOLE, Wyo. -(Dow Jones)- Japan's top economic policymakers aren't alone in their inability to agree on ways to haul the world's second-biggest economy out of a decade-long recession. The world's best economic thinkers can't agree either. \par \par \uc2  Economists attending a two-day conference of leading central bankers from around the world spent at least a third  of their time pondering the best way to revive Japan's stalled economy. They blasted the country's economic policies over the last decade, and contemplated radical remedies - including devaluing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pegging it to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Getting Japan out of recession... remains the world's most urgent monetary-policy task," Lars Svensson, an economics professor at Princeton University, argued in a paper. "Japan has already lost a decade to economic stagnation and deflation. Its monetary policy arguably represents the world's worst monetary-policy mistake since the Great Depression. With continued bad policy, Japan may very well lose another decade." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ex-IMF Official Sees Legal Problems With \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -Peg Idea\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the economists couldn't reach a consensus on their own proposed\par remedies either. Svensson suggested a "foolproof" method - among other\par things, devalue the Japanese yen and peg it at Y140 to Y150 to the\par dollar until the economy recovers. But that only drew a warning from a\par former International Monetary Fund official: the move would be illegal\par under international law.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It would be a clear use of competitive depreciation to boost economic\par activity in Japan, and as such a violation of  international law," said\par Michael Mussa, who was the IMF's chief economist from 1991 to 2001.\par "It's the one thing which  the IMF articles of agreement clearly\par preclude: you may not  engage in competitive depreciation, period."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Martin Feldstein, who heads the National Bureau of Economic Research\par in Cambridge, Mass., advocated cutting Japanese income taxes while\par simultaneously raising Japan's 5% value-added tax by 1 percentage point\par every quarter until  it reaches 20%. That would keep the government's\par revenues intact while giving Japanese households "an incentive to\par spend." But other economists said it wouldn't do the trick on its own.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I see the Feldstein tax proposal as positive, but as a complement to\par monetary policy," said Mickey Levy, an economist with Bank of America.\par Among other things, Levy said in an interview, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should stop\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to control the value of the yen.\par Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently has repeatedly bought dollars to prevent\par the yen from appreciating against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Japanese Policy Inertia Worries Many Economists\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the late 1980s Japan's economy grew so rapidly that the country's\par top officials worried that it might overheat. The Bank of Japan began\par raising interest rates in 1989 to fight the danger of inflation. Stock\par prices plummeted and the economy began to sink. In response, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  began a protracted campaign of interest rate cuts - the overnight\par call-money rate declined from 8% to zero by 1999. But the economy never\par recovered.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the conference here over the weekend, a top Japanese banker said\par the Bank of Japan can't do much more. "We've practically used up all our\par options," said Yutaka Yamaguchi,  the bank's deputy governor, referring\par to what he called "orthodox" instruments of monetary policy. With\par Japanese interest rates at zero, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has resorted to\par pumping cash into the financial system to stimulate the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That comment prompted some economists to fret that the health of the\par global economy in the next few years may depend the health of the\par still-weak U.S. economy. "The Japanese are saying they've done\par everything they can," said John Makin, an economist at the American\par Enterprise Institute in Washington. The Europeans are focusing on\par inflation and suggesting that weaker growth isn't going to prompt an\par interest-rate cut. So everything is basically resting on the U.S."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan Should Seek To Induce Rising Inflation, Expert Says\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Svensson, the Princeton University professor, said in his paper that\par Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is capable of doing much  more. "The\par zero-interest-rate policy implemented from February 1999 to August 2000,\par and again from March 2001, via  the 'quantitative easing' (expanding the\par monetary base) is not sufficiently expansionary to induce a recovery,"\par he said. Because Japan is experiencing deflation rather than inflation,\par the inflation-adjusted interest rate there "remains positive."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There is a foolproof way to jump-start the Japanese economy,"\par Svensson said in his paper. In addition to a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation and a\par temporary pegging of the yen to the dollar, it would involve setting the\par goal of achieving a  specific - and rising - level of inflation in the\par economy. The peg would be dismantled after inflation reaches about 2%,\par Svensson said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "A real depreciation means that Japanese exporters get a  short-term\par competitive edge, but growth in Japan and increased aggregate demand\par will increase Japan's imports from the rest of the world," Svensson\par said. "This is therefore not a beggar-thy-neighbor policy, except\par possibly in the very short run. In the long-run, the region, the U.S.\par and the world will gain from an expansion in Japan."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other economists said the plan was anything but foolproof, even when\par the legal ramifications are set aside. Feldstein, of the NBER, said in\par an interview that the Bank of Japan's deputy governor was "absolutely\par right" to assert that all conventional monetary-policy options have been\par exhausted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They're in a liquidity trap" that monetary policy cannot undo,"\par Feldstein said. The Bank of Japan cannot set an inflation target, as\par Svensson suggested, because "it has no way to increase inflation with\par the instruments available," Feldstein said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Joseph Rebello, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9279;\par joseph.rebello@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020901dy9100001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
