\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Where Did Doom And Gloom Go? \b0\par\par\uc2 2,104 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2002\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Things Not So Bad After All? Look Again\par DJIA          8990.79    gain 212.73    up 2.4%\par NASDAQ        1394.54    gain 33.53     up 2.5%\par S&P 500       950.70     gain 21.93     up 2.4%\par Dow Future    8960.00    loss 7.00      dn 0.1%\par NASDAQ Future 1020.00    loss 0.50      dn 0.1%\par S&P Future    949.40     gain 0.40         0.0%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.28% down 0.02\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Investors held on to their budding optimism Monday, sending stocks sharply higher, but Treasurys also gained in price. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Analysts said the absence of any significant negative news, such as accounting restatements, added to the good mood on Wall Street, which has seen two straight weeks of gains among the three major indexes. Light trading also gave prices a boost. \par \par \uc2  "The path of least resistance is always higher," said Richard E. Cripps, chief market strategist for Legg Mason of Baltimore. \par \par \uc2  Is it really as simple as that? \par \par \uc2  The Conference Board reported that its Index of Leading Economic Indicators, an important gauge of U.S. economic activity, fell 0.4% to 111.7 in July, slightly beating analysts' expectation of a decline of 0.5%. \par \par \uc2  July's figure represents the second straight monthly decline in the index, following a revised 0.2% drop in June. \par \par \uc2  Never mind the report was bad. It still beat expectations, prompting investors to continue their buying from last week. \par \par \uc2  Yet analysts said investors' optimism remains fragile, particularly after the Federal Reserve last week left interest rates unchanged but raised the possibility of future cuts, citing risks of a softening economy. \par \par \uc2  "People are feeling better about the market in general," said Brian G. Belski, fundamental market strategist at US Bancorp Piper Jaffray. "Whether that has staying power remains in question given that one, the markets have been up four weeks in a row and two, it's heavy vacation time on Wall Street." \par \par \uc2  BONDS: U.S. Treasury prices closed Monday higher, after a small bout of late-day buying added some excitement to an eventless day. \par \par \uc2  For the most part, "the market was consolidating" positions after last week's moves, said John Spinello, market strategist with Merrill Lynch in New York. He added that "you've got to be a little surprised" Treasurys held up as well as they did given equities' performance. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, the U.S. reports its trade deficit, expected to have narrowed in June to $37.0 billion from $37.6 billion in May. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japanese Shares In Feeble Recovery\par USD-Yen             119.20       gain  0.62    up 0.5%\par AUD-USD             0.5432       gain  0.0017  up 0.3%\par Nikkei 225          9652.38      gain  53.28   up 0.6%\par Hang Seng           10391.24     gain  144.28  up 1.4%\par JGB Yield           1.2500% down 0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks recovered somewhat feebly Tuesday\par trading, taking a cue from Wall Street's rally. The dollar's gain again\par the yen provided some inspiration.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The modest rise in Japanese equities partially reversed the previous\par day's sharp downturn. Gains, though, were tentative due to the lack of\par fresh domestic news to spur buying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Notable advancers Tuesday morning were major exporters, such as\par technology blue chips NEC, Sony and Kyocera, as well as auto shares\par Honda, Mazda and Toyota, which benefit from the weakening yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the dollar got a lift from improved sentiment\par surrounding U.S. assets and lingering speculation that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may yet\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to brake the yen's recent surge.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said\par Tuesday he sees no reason for the yen to rise, repeating Tokyo's view\par that a weaker yen is in line with fundamentals.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro traded at 116.10, down from 116.38 yen late Monday in Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the benchmark 10-year Japanese government bond was quoted\par at 1.2500%, down from Monday's finish of 1.2600%. Its price rose 0.09\par point to 100.43.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets Need A Fresh Perspective\par Euro-USD            0.9765      gain  0.0001  up 0.01%\par Stlg-USD            1.5282      gain  0.0008  up 0.06%\par USD-Franc           1.5030      loss  0.0002  dn 0.01%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares and government bonds are expected\par to start the session little changed, while the euro searches for\par direction.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares will start the session tentatively in narrow\par ranges until the markets decide whether equities really have formed a\par floor that will withstand damage.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Major European indexes posted impressive gains Monday as investors\par were cheered by pockets of upbeat corporate news and the belief that\par regional markets may have bottomed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Anais Faraj, strategist at Nomura Securities, said that as in 1987,\par investors have discounted a lot of bad news and it could take a severe\par shock to derail sentiment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "But unlike 1987, we are in a bear market and investors have plenty of\par time to join the bandwagon: for now, sell into the rallies," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds are expected to open little changed\par as the market sorts out supplies worries and economic influences.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bonds were lower late Monday as equity markets\par gained momentum and from pressure by supply worries. Reports said the\par German 2003 draft budget foresees a 13% rise in new bond issuance to\par EUR203.1 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The figure of EUR203 billion includes sales of debt instruments\par offered to retail investors, but not the refinancing of "German unity"\par and special funds that mature in 2003.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Technically the future is looking okay as long as it holds above the\par 109.90 area," said a broker at a French bank. It finished at 110.30.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bund futures need to break below the 109.75-80 area before the\par current upward trend is threatened.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors will also keep a watchful eye on the recent upcreep in oil\par price. Analysts are still talking of oil hitting $30 near term,\par especially if OPEC fails to open its taps.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session in a narrow range of between\par $0.9750 to $0.9775 as the market measures the strength of pro-euro\par factors against pro-dollar influences.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar advanced against its major rivals Monday on firming U.S.\par stock markets and on signs of a weaker economic backdrop in Europe and\par Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Global investor risk appetite seems to be gradually increasing while\par "the dollar has been the obvious winner so\par  far," as funds migrate out of traditional safe haven assets including\par gold and the Swiss franc, said Jim McCormick, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist with Lehman Brothers in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar's gains also came against a steady drone of market talk\par about a potential U.S. war against Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Tuesday, August 20: EU Output, US Trade Deficit\par ET/GMT\par 0100/0500 Japan     July Steel Production (last +3.6% YY)\par 0245/0645 France    June Trade Figures\par 0430/0830 U.K.      July Public Borrowing (seen -GBP7.5 B)\par 0430/0830 U.K.      July BBA Lending (last +GBP5.3 Bln)\par 0430/0830 U.K.      July Provisional M4 (seen +6.5% YY)\par 0430/0830 U.K.      July M4 Lending (seen +GBP6.3 B)\par 0515/0915 E.U.      European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  allocates bids\par 0600/1000 E.U.      June Industrial Output (seen +0.5% MM)\par 0745/1145 U.S.      BTM-UBS Warburg Chain Store Sales Index\par 0830/1230 U.S.      June Trade Deficit (seen $37.0 B)\par 0900/1300 U.S.      Redbook Retail Sales for Aug 17 Week\par 1400/1800 U.S.      July Treasury Budget Statement\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WPP (WPPGY): 1H earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 6 analysts): GBP220M (GBP252.4M)\par Consensus sales: GBP1.96B (GBP2.0B)\par Note: New business expected around $1.5B. Worst hit areas likely to be\par specialist communications, PR and US, while market research and\par continental Europe should show more resilience. CEO Martin Sorrell\par isn't expected to be upbeat about advertising outlook. Has already\par said he expects no meaningful recovery before '04.\par KPN (KPN): 2Q earnings\par Consensus net loss (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR26M (EUR48M)\par Note: Analysts provide net result range EUR60M loss to EUR8M profit.\par Debt reduction target eyed. Results due 0600 GMT.\par Vivendi Environnement (VE): Bondholders meeting (1st call)\par Note: Holders of EUR1.4 billion of 2005 convertible bonds will vote on\par whether to accept a 0.75-percentage-point coupon step-up to cut the\par guarantee they carry from parent Vivendi Universal (V). There are\par rumblings a quorum won't be reached as some investors look to squeeze\par out a little more coupon or favor early redemption.\par Svenska Handelsbanken (S.SHB): 2Q Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 4 analysts): SEK2.49B (SEK2.89B)\par Note: Lower earnings likely due to weaker income from fees and\par insurance \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Loan loss provisions seen slightly up from\par current low levels.\par Coloplast (K.COL): 9 Mo earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 5 analysts): DKK534M (DKK289M)\par Consensus sales: DKK4.21B (DKK2.99B)\par Note: Profit is expected to be boosted by one-time income from the sale\par of a unit to Johnson & Johnson (JNJ) in May. Sales rise due to\par integration of UK purchase. Analysts divided about outlook, some say\par Coloplast should show increasing market share, others worry whole\par market for ostomy and wound care is shrinking. Earnings due around\par 1000.\par ISS A/S (K.ISS): 2Q earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 6 analysts): DKK89M (DKK60M)\par Consensus sales: DKK9.8B (DKK8.6B)\par Note: Analysts say profit margins will likely vary significantly\par depending on geography - South America doing badly due to economic\par crisis but recovery in Benelux. Need to keep close eye on organic\par growth rate which may have sunk to 2%-3% vs 7% due to general\par corporate activity slowdown.\par H. Lundbeck A/S (K.LUN): 2Q earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 6 analysts): DKK316M (DKK559.2M)\par Consensus sales: DKK2.26B (DKK1.91B)\par Note: Net profit looking poor compared to sales because of loss on\par Cephalon shares. Some analysts looking for earnings upgrade while\par others say it's more likely in 3Q report.\par Synthes-Stratec (Z.SYS): 1H earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 2 analysts): CHF195.5M (CHF187.7M)\par Consensus sales: CHF806M (CHF741.5M)\par Note: ZKB expects a spurt in sales growth from new products. Sarasin's\par unable "to exclude the risk of potential negative news." Risk of\par slower growth could pressure shares, although "this risk seems to be\par priced in."\par Austrian Airlines (R.AAI): 1H Earnings\par Consensus pretax loss (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR29.73M (EUR70.2M)\par Consensus sales: EUR1.06B (EUR1.04B)\par Note: Analysts cite a slight improvement in sales as reduced capacity\par was compensated by higher yields and improved seat load factor. Focus\par will be on development of restructuring and cost cutting.\par Erste Bank (R.DEO): 1H Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR238.5M (EUR194M)\par Consensus net interest income: EUR784.7M (EUR670M)\par Note: Analysts cite higher net interest margins and strong fee and\par commission development. Most important focus is contribution by Ceska\par Sporitelna.\par William Demant (K.WDH): 1H Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 7 analysts): DKK380M (DKK300M)\par Consensus sales: DKK2B (DKK1.65B)\par Note: Expectations of strong sales for new high-end hearing aid Adapto\par to boost income. Analysts warn that hopes of a FY profit upgrade have\par started to peter out due to worries about slower market growth\par especially in Europe.\par Mersey Docks & Harbour (U.MDK): 1H earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP26.8M (N/A)\par Forecast range: GBP26.5M - GBP27M\par Note:One analyst hopes for but doesn't expect signs of improvement in\par Mersey Docks' Atlantic and Irish Sea \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and sees company as\par fair value at current price.\par Brixton (U.BXT): 1H earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (Credit Lyonnais Securities): GBP21.5M\par (GBP19.9M)\par Note: Credit Lyonnais expects "fairly dull" set of results following\par flat property values during period.\par NEG Micon A/S (K.NEG): 2Q earnings\par Consensus pretax loss (DJ, 5 analysts): DKK140M (DKK70M)\par Consensus sales: DKK1.94B (DKK1.76B)\par Note: Typical 1H seasonality more pronounced than normal after govt\par approval process changed in Germany, a key market.\par Tereg/Demag: E.U. antitrust decision\par Evotec OAI (G.EVT): 2Q Earnings\par Medion (G.MDN): 2Q earnings\par Imtech (N.IMT): 1H earnings\par Mikron (Z.MIK): 1H Earnings\par Morgan Sindall (U.MGN): 1H earnings\par IFX Power (U.IXP): 1H earnings\par London & St Lawrence (U.LSL): FY earnings\par Firth Holdings (U.FTH): AGM\par Ceska Sporitelna (R.CSP): Consolidated six month results under IAS\par Porsche (G.POR): Opening of Leipzig plant\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0500GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1011 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020820dy8k0007y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
