\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Rallies on Stock Gains, But Ends Weaker Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2002\par 01:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar ended higher against many of its key rivals after a roller-coaster session, regaining ground on the back of rallying U.S. equity markets. But the greenback still ended the New York day 1.2% weaker against the yen, once again raising the possibility that the Japanese government might act to brake its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ascent. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the dollar was trading at 117.32 yen, well off its session low of 116.34 yen, but still 1.2% weaker than 118.77 yen late Tuesday in New York. The euro was trading at 97.83 U.S. cents, having lost all its session's gains amid the dollar's late-day recovery against the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and had retreated from its 98.24 U.S. cents level late Tuesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.4954 francs, up from 1.4896 francs. Sterling was at $1.5368, up from $1.5348. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rebound against an array of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "one hundred per cent [attributable to] the stock-market rally," with equity investors regaining some confidence as the deadline for chief executive officers to certify financial accounts expired, said Bruce Giammattei, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh, Penn. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average came back from earlier losses to close up 3.1%, also a result of a major surge in Treasurys earlier, which some analysts said triggered an asset allocation out of debt and into stocks. \par \par \uc2  Part of the ebullience in equities also seemed to be a result of a sense that the Federal Reserve may have achieved what it set out to do in its statement Tuesday: lower longer-dated yields, that in turn can help unleash funds into a credit-starved corporate sector and drive mortgage rates lower, putting more money in homeowners' pockets. \par \par \uc2  In the absence of any new direction-setting economic data, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are willing to follow what ever new information they get," said Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank in New York. "Right now that new information is the equity markets, but there's nothing out there that tells us there's sustainable dollar strength out there." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the dollar had weakened rapidly early in the New York day, in a continuation of a decline that was apparently triggered by the Fed statement. At one point, the dollar had fallen by over two yen, once again raising the specter of imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  One factor that powered the yen higher may have been the coupon payment coming Thursday from the U.S. Treasury, said David Mozina, director of global fixed-income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Banc of America Securities, in New York. Totaling $20.3 billion, this month's coupon payment is "on the larger side," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors own some 11.5% of Treasury debt, and when coupon payments are made, they reinvest a portion of them, but repatriate the rest, Mr. Mozina noted. Still, that may lend the yen just a fleeting boost. \par \par \uc2  The volatility behind the yen's leap may also have stemmed from some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators taking advantage of thin market conditions, with many Japanese participants on vacation for the annual "obon" holidays. \par \par \uc2  As the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied against the dollar, the yen also moved sharply higher against the euro, which at one point in the morning session in New York was buying as little as 114.61 yen, its weakest level since May 22. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, however, the euro had recovered to 115.14 yen, above the 115.00 yen mark. \par \par \uc2  According to one major European bank, the slide in the dollar in intraday trading prompted the Bank of Japan to telephone \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desks to check \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, a phenomenon that often, but not necessarily, preludes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Other major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks said they hadn't received the call, but clearly speculation that the BOJ might re-enter the market was sufficient to put a brake on the dollar's slide. \par \par \uc2  Backing this up, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda was quoted in a report on a Japanese news service, Jiji Press, calling the recent rapid yen rise inappropriate and saying Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to keep a close eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the Fed's statement had underscored a fear that the U.S. economy's sluggish recovery might worsen still, particularly as some recent economic data have shown that manufacturing activity stalled and payroll growth came to a halt in July. \par \par \uc2  Among emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Brazilian real came under some pressure again. In the latest opinion poll of voters' intentions in the country's October presidential elections, government-backed candidate Jose Serra's support remained unchanged at 13.4%, well behind his two main rivals. Late Wednesday, the dollar was buying 3.200 Brazilian reals, versus 3.155 reals late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -- John Parry and Tyler Lifton contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to Grainne McCarthy at grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020815dy8e0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
