\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -U.S. stocks, rate cut talk keep dollar afloat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2002\par 10:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 7 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied near recent six-week highs against the yen and the euro on Wednesday, bolstered by increasing expectations of an asset-boosting U.S. rate cut and by talk of U.S. funds bringing money home. \par \par \uc2  U.S. blue-chip stocks rose by nearly three percent on Tuesday on mounting speculation of a Fed rate cut by the end of the year. An interest rate cut would boost the U.S. economy and U.S. stocks, and therefore the dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said some investors were being forced to buy U.S. shares to maintain the balance of their asset allocations after the value of U.S. stock portfolios declined sharply in recent months. \par \par \uc2  "We've had a such a significant rally on Wall Street that dollar strength is not really surprising," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of speculation that the Fed will cut interest rates - 25 basis points may not do a lot, but it's helping sentiment towards the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 120.64/71 yen and $0.9688/91 per euro by 0740 GMT, little changed from the U.S. close and a little below six-week highs of 121.21 yen and $0.9619 per euro hit on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ASSET SWITCH \par \par \uc2  Dealers said some investors were seen to have sold a huge amount of two-year Schatz debt and shifted funds to European and U.S stocks. On Tuesday, the Schatz yield rose by a hefty 12 basis points. \par \par \uc2  Traders also said hedge funds were dumping short-term government bonds to buy stocks. \par \par \uc2  "Investors have bought European bonds too heavily and sold too many U.S. shares recently," said Hidenao Miyajima, a strategist at Commerz Securities in Tokyo, adding that some investors were now closing such positions. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock index futures were indicating a stronger open on Wall Street, after networking giant Cisco Systems posted higher quarterly earnings in after-hours trade. \par \par \uc2  But market bears remained sceptical about the dollar's outlook, saying the risk of a U.S. double-dip recession was looming despite official talk that the U.S. economy was sound. \par \par \uc2  "It's hard to say U.S. share prices have hit a bottom," said Satoshi Tokuda, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Corp in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund has slashed growth forecasts for the world's leading economies, a German newspaper reported on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Boersen Zeitung said in a report issued ahead of the newspaper's publication on Wednesday that according to internal forecasts it had seen, the IMF now predicts the U.S. economy will grow by 2.2 percent this year, compared to its public forecast of 2.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  SELLING THE YEN \par \par \uc2  Japan spent a record high amount of 4.0162 trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between April and June, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven days between May 22 and June 28, selling the yen and buying dollars and euros. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities were seeking to head off a potentially export-damaging rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, German industrial orders for June, due at 1000 GMT, are forecast to show a fall of 1.5 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020807dy8700g2c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
