\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan interventions \highlight\uc2  at Y4.0162 trln in April-June. \b0\par\par\uc2 158 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2002\par 01:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 7 (Reuters) - Japanese monetary authorities spent a total of 4.0162 trillion yen ($33.20 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the April-June quarter, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts as agent for the MOF, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven days between May 22 and June 28, selling the yen and buying dollars and euros. \par \par \uc2  During the quarter, the BOJ was first seen stepping into the market on May 22 to buy dollars at around 123.80 yen, a level regarded as one of the highest ever at which the BOJ has conducted dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The authorities have kept out of the market since June 28, they said. The dollar fell to a 17-month low of 115.50 yen on July 16. At 2353 GMT, it was quoted at 120.89/94 yen . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020806dy86018ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
