\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Short-Selling Stk Curbs Unlikely To Aid Yen Much \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 957 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2002\par 20:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- New curbs on short selling in Japan's stock markets may stoke some muted interest among foreign investors and help stabilize the yen temporarily. But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts don't expect the rule changes to reverse an accumulating tide of negative sentiment about Japan's economy that is dragging the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Financial Services Agency Tuesday announced a series of steps aimed at boosting the slumping stock market,  including tighter pricing regulations on short-selling using margin trades, planned for implementation in September. \par \par \uc2  The action follows pricing curbs announced in February and imposed in March on short-selling using borrowed shares,  which then helped ignite a foreign bid for Japanese shares,  also lifting the yen. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the Nikkei 225 stock index gained 4.3% to March 8 from Feb 26, while the yen jumped 4.6% against the dollar over the same period as foreign net sales of Japanese  equities soared, notes Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan in New York. \par \par \uc2  Now, the introduction of new curbs in September, although potentially damping trading activity somewhat, "should at least put a bottom under Japanese stock markets," Patterson said. \par \par \uc2  Although such a scenario should be at least mildly positive for the yen, Patterson said she "would be careful about jumping to a strong-yen conclusion", however. \par \par \uc2  The main obstacle likely to prevent the yen repeating its March Spring is the current deceleration of global economic growth, which is taking a toll on Japanese exporters' prospects, not least because activity in the key U.S. market is less robust than anticipated. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Artificial Support\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par More broadly, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders expect the curbs to have only a\par fleeting supportive effect on the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "Moves that smack\par of official manipulation of capital markets, while they may provide some\par short term support for a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or index, in the longer term...reduce\par the global comfort level with whatever index is being artificially\par supported," said Thomas Molloy, a trader at Bank Leumi in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Over the past month, Japan's benchmark Nikkei 225 stock index and the\par yen have retrenched, showing efforts earlier in the year to keep the\par Nikkei above the 10,000 points mark have failed, Molloy said. The effect\par has been to "leave a lot of people out in the cold," including those\par foreign investors who didn't take profits on Japanese equity holdings\par soon enough, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After that finger-burning experience, some of these market\par participants may not be ready to return to Japanese stock markets. In\par the past month, the Nikkei 225 has fallen 12%, while the yen has\par retreated 2% against the dollar. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's slippage is partly a\par reflection of the reversal of net portfolio flows abroad that had poured\par into Japanese stocks in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We are seeing pretty heavy selling by foreigners of Japanese equities\par at the moment," said Mike Rosenberg, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par research with Deutsche Bank in New York, noting net sales of more than\par Y200 billion worth of Japanese equities in the last week of July.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rosenberg continues to have a negative outlook for yen-denominated\par assets. "I feel the fundamental backdrop in  Japan is not very\par attractive," Rosenberg said. "If the U.S. economy is moderating, that\par will (negatively) impact Japanese exports," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While U.S. gross domestic product slowed sharply to a mere 1.1% growth\par in the second quarter from 5.0% in the first, there are also signs of a\par manufacturing slowdown in Japan that may herald the stalling of that\par country's incipient economic revival. Japan's industrial output for the\par month of June fell 0.7% from May, steeper than the 0.3% drop economists\par had forecast.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Should Japan's fledgling recovery fade, that will heighten the\par pressure on the nation's already ailing banks, creaking under the weight\par of some Y150 trillion of bad debts  and potentially non-performing\par loans. Indeed, the curbs on short-selling in the domestic stock market\par may be primarily intended to help banks and companies avoid posting\par dismal financial results at Japan's fiscal mid-year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I do not think it is a coincidence that these curbs were announced a\par month ahead of key book-closing dates," Patterson said. Japan's fiscal\par year ends on March 31, while the half year closes at the end of\par September. In preparation  for announcing financial results at these\par dates, it is in the interests of Japanese companies, and banks, which\par have extensive cross shareholdings, that domestic equity markets do not\par tumble.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Walking A Fine Line\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But in a delicate balancing act, at the same time as lending some\par support to stocks, the Japanese government will  be concerned not to\par inadvertently buoy the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  via  the Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aimed at softening the yen in May and June showed, Japanese\par policymakers see a weaker yen as a priority. The government wants to\par maintain the yen in its current trading range above the key Y120.00 mark\par to the dollar, identified as a `comfort zone' in which Japanese\par exporters have leeway to compete abroad.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rosenberg, of Deutsche Bank, forecasts the dollar trading between\par Y122.00 and Y125.00 over the next three months, before firming further\par to between Y125.00 and Y130.00 within six months, up from the dollar's\par Y121.04 level midday Tuesday in New York. The yen has gained 9% against\par the dollar so far this year.\par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Natsuo Nishio and James Simms in Tokyo contributed to this report).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020806dy86009q1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
