\par\par \b \uc2  Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For July 31 \b0\par\par\uc2 1,902 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2002\par 11:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-415-4020. \par \par \uc2  MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME \par \par \uc2  =JAPAN WATCH: PM Koizumi's plan to cap the Japanese government's guarantees on bank deposits was supposed to signal Japan's banks are back in business and ready to withstand market forces. But it seems the premier is wriggling on reform again, reportedly telling the bank minister to arrange to keep full guarantees on zero-interest-rate deposits meant for settlement purposes. (=Japan Watch column likely to run after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Did Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  know something the markets didn't when it stopped trying to prop up the dollar last month? Despite the damage Finance Minister Shiokawa inflicted with ill-advised comments on exchange rates - offering Y115 as a juicy target for dollar sellers - the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has staged a remarkable comeback from 17-month lows to reclaim Y120 without any help from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF Looking Good After USD/JPY Rebound," ran at 0501 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Global financial markets are experiencing their most turbulent period in recent history, but the public face of the Reserve Bank of Australia remains stoic. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: RBA Quiet In Current Market Turmoil," ran at 0217 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY: Australian financial markets were swamped this week by a flood of new data - including strong retail sales figures - that all pointed to year-on-year economic growth of more than 4% in the second quarter. By rights, the Reserve Bank of Australia should be tightening monetary policy after its board meets Aug. 6, but still no market economists expect that to happen while concerns remain about global share markets. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: RBA To Hold Rates, But For How Long?," ran at 0712 GMT) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA: Indonesia's Bank Restructuring Agency increased pressure on the Gajah Tunggal Group, which owes the state billions in dollars stemming from the Asian financial crisis, but has so far failed to repay a cent. IBRA said it will call Sjamsul Nursalim, the head of the Gajah Tunggal Group, to its offices in Jakarta for questioning Wednesday over his failure to repay any of the 27.4 trillion rupiah the group owes the agency. ("=Indonesia IBRA Increases Pressure On Gajah Tunggal Group," ran at 0332 GMT) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA: U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell's visit to Jakarta Friday is likely to focus on Washington's push for closer ties with the Indonesian military in a bid to forge more effective cooperation between the two countries in the war against terrorism. But Powell's trip, part of a whistle-stop tour of the Asian region, poses a diplomatic conundrum which the U.S. must answer carefully. ("=US Fostering Indonesia Military Ties With Powell Visit," ran at 0614 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK: Thai exports rose further in June, up 4.5% on year, despite the baht's sharp appreciation against the dollar, a sign that the country's economy would continue to recover, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday. June imports grew 5.5% on year, resulting in a trade surplus of $367 million. The Bank of Thailand also revised up its forecast for 2002 GDP growth to 3-4%, from 2.5-3.5% previously, on export and manufacturing production growth in the second quarter. (Wrap expected after 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: The frailty of the U.S. dollar has stoked more demand from international investors for euro-denominated bonds out of Asia, as the region's debt retains its appeal despite turbulence in global markets, senior executives at Deutsche Bank told Dow Jones Newswires. ("=INTERVIEW: Weak Dlr Fuels Demand For Euro Debt -Deutsche," ran at 0842 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY: Australia's Transurban completed the country's first nominal toll road bond deal Wednesday - a bumper A$1.19 billion transaction that may usher in a new breed of infrastructure debt issuance - despite investor nervousness about volatility in world financial markets. ("=Australia Transurban A$1.19B Bond Wins Over Nervous Mkt," ran at 0602 GMT) \par \par \uc2  PERTH: Struggling Australian nickel miner Anaconda Nickel said Wednesday it has gained a further extension until early September, in its long-running debt restructuring talks with U.S. bondholders owed US$402 million. ("=INTERVIEW:Australia's Anaconda/Debt," ran from 0210 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: With the help of a stronger won, South Korea's consumer price index posted a 0.3% decline in July from June, making it less likely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise rates anytime soon. ("=WRAP: S Korea's July CPI Eases Pressure For Rate Hike," ran at 0715 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore's unemployment rate fell slightly in the second quarter - the first drop in six quarters - in what was likely a temporary trough rather than a clear indication of a recovery in the domestic job market. ("=WRAP: Fall In Singapore 2Q Unemployment Likely Temporary," ran at 0642 GMT) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA: The International Monetary Fund praised Indonesia's efforts to rein in its budget deficit on the first day of its mission to the country, to review economic reforms under a $5 billion lending program. ("IMF Official Praises Indonesia's Efforts To Rein Deficit," ran at 0825 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar fell moderately against the yen in Asia Wednesday as Japanese exporters and other players sold the dollar, pushing it below the psychologically important Y120 level. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," first take ran at 0548 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  EQUITIES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Matsushita Electric Industrial Wednesday said it returned to profitability on an operating basis in the April-June quarter, helped by brisk sales of audio-visual equipment and cost-cutting. In the first quarter, Matsushita posted a group operating profit of Y14.56 billion - its first profit in five quarters, and following a year-ago loss of Y38.69 billion. The Japanese consumer electronics giant also revised upward its prior outlook for sales and profits for the first half through Sept. 30, on ongoing strength in sales of audio-visual products and electronics. (Wrap expected to move by 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's power utility Tenaga Nasional to release third-quarter results at 0900 GMT. (Wrap expected around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Nasdaq-listed ST Assembly Test Services said Wednesday it expects current quarter revenue to grow 5-10%, despite warnings of a soft market from some key customers. The Singapore-based chip testing and packaging company also reported a sixth consecutive quarter of loss. But the US$21.57 million, or 22 US cent loss per diluted ADS, was better than analysts' average loss forecast of US$26.7 million. ("=WRAP: ST Assembly Beats 2Q Loss Forecast, Sees 3Q Rev Up," ran at 0456 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK: Thai Airways has decided to dismiss its four financial advisers for a public share offering and appoint six new ones later in the day, a board member said Wednesday. The company has shortlisted 10 local firms, not including Credit Suisse First Boston, which has been dismissed as adviser, and Dresdner Kleinwort, which pulled out. ("Thai Airways/Advisers," first take ran at 0659 GMT; possible further story after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON: Air New Zealand Wednesday unveiled its no-frills domestic airline, saying average fares on its domestic network will fall 20% and by about 28% on the main routes. ("Air NZ/Average Fares Cut," ran from 2317 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: While Japan's banks are struggling to stay afloat, its non-bank finance companies are combating an image as extortionist loan sharks, and Wall Street is shunned by anxious investors, Japanese lender Nissin Co. wants to list on the New York Stock Exchange. But analysts say that if anyone can show how non-bank finance companies are able to make healthy profits despite Japan's financial woes, Nissin may be it. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Nissin Aims To Grow With NYSE Listing," ran at 0648 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY: Insurance Australia Group Wednesday became the latest casualty of slumping equity markets, warning its annual loss could be as high as A$40 million. The insurer joined larger counterpart AMP Ltd. with an earnings warning that spooked retail and institutional investors, even though it has been apparent for some months that equity market returns are under severe pressure. ("=WRAP: Sharp Profit Warnings Pressure Australian Insurers," ran at 0806 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK: Siam Cement, the country's largest industrial conglomerate, Wednesday posted a sharp rise in second-quarter net profit, up 49% to 3.51 billion baht, as sales revenue got a boost from strong domestic demand. Separately, Siam Cement also said it plans to issue on Oct. 1 up to THB7.5 billion of three-year debentures to refinance debt. ("Siam Cement/Earnings," ran from 0654 GMT; and "Thai Siam Cement/Debs," first take ran at 0714 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore-listed Cycle & Carriage and its unit MCL Land likely to announce improved first-half earnings Wednesday largely because of a better performance from Indonesia's Astra, though a weak property sector will still weigh on earnings. (story after 0900 GMT, Wrap on merit) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: SembCorp Marine, soon to be taken private by parent SembCorp Industries, will likely report Wednesday its first-half earnings rose sharply, due to strong demand from the shipbuilding industry. (story afetr 0900 GMT, Wrap on merit) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Jardine Matheson and Jardine Strategic release earnings around 0900 GMT. (stories after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Shares of Chinese rechargeble battery maker BYD Co. rose more than 10% Wednesday on their first session on Hong Kong's main board. (Wrap expected after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI: Major Japanese trading company Sumitomo Corp. and Chinese home appliance maker Hisense Group have agreed to form a 50:50 joint venture to sell Hisense's products in Japan, a Hisense spokeswoman said Wednesday. ("Sumitomo Corp, China's Hisense In Home Appliance Sales JV," ran at 0239 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI: China's Shenzhen Development Bank Co. made an announcement on Wednesday saying it has no information to disclose, following local reports saying foreign companies were competing to take a stake in the bank. ("China's Shenzhen Bk: Has No Activity Requiring Disclosure," ran at 0108 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: Industrial Bank of Korea said Wednesday its preliminary first-half net profit surged 68% on year to 371.6 billion won. The bank will announce its confirmed earnings next month. ("Indus Bk Of Korea/Prelim Earnings," ran at 0205 GMT) \par \par \uc2  ENERGY/COMMODITIES \par \par \uc2  =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: A push to promote the use of coal in power generation in southern China's Guangdong province at the expense of fuel oil will cut fuel oil imports and may push some Asian and European refiners to reconfigure their facilities. ("=China Energy Watch: Coal Policy To Hit Fuel Oil Market," ran at 0901 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY: GrainCorp Ltd., an Australian agribusiness company, said Wednesday it has joined forces with Cargill Australia to formally bid for the Australian milling assets of Goodman Fielder. (Wrap expected to move shortly) \par \par \uc2  MELBOURNE: The prospects for Australia and East Timor coming to an amicable deal over the ownership of billions of dollars' worth of natural gas reserves in the Timor Sea were boosted Wednesday when both sides stepped up their conciliatory rhetoric. ("=WRAP: Australia, E Timor Get Conciliatory On Gas Fields," ran at 0810 GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020731dy7v000lp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
