\par\par \b \uc2  ODJ Japan Bank Woes Reappear As Stocks, Yen Hit \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2002\par 20:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones Commodities Service \par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Maybe it's time to scrap the suspension of disbelief\par that's been behind the recent economic optimism that's driven the yen higher\par this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As Friday's swift, two-and-a-half-cent crumbling of the yen against the dollar\par showed, a fickle surge of confidence surrounding Japan's fledgling, export-\par driven economic revival seems to be unraveling.\par For several months, U.S.-based fund managers have been snapping up short-term\par opportunities in Japanese equities, seeking a quick killing as stocks were\par rising, and the firming yen provided an additional fillip.\par But problems in the U.S. have handed a blow to Japan's capital markets and may\par deal another to its economy.\par The shock waves of turbulence in U.S. stock markets have spread globally.\par Worries that slower-than-expected growth in the U.S. might derail a global\par economic rebound have already reached Japan, the world's second biggest\par economy.\par By midday Friday in New York, the dollar had soared to Y118.75, very sharply\par up from Y116.57 late Thursday in New York. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders who had pushed the\par yen steadily higher, up 11% against the dollar so far this year through\par Thursday, were rethinking that performance Friday.\par In what may mark a turning point for the yen, market participants are once\par again placing emphasis on a fundamental concern that appears to have been\par temporarily overlooked: the Japanese economy remains structurally weak.\par    Deflationary Pressures\par Despite some recent data showing modest upward price movements, "you still\par have a situation with overriding deflationary pressures," says Marcel\par Kasumovich, economist with Goldman Sachs in New York. Any sign of strength in\par Japan's economy "is coming from the external sector, which itself is\par vulnerable," he said, adding that the unexpected fall in U.S. durable goods\par orders in June, reported Thursday, could bode badly for Japanese exporters.\par Against that backdrop, equities investors may be starting to fret that\par Japanese stocks are heading for a fall, after a short-lived period of\par stability.\par "As U.S. fund managers look for profits to offset their losses" at home "Japan\par is bound to catch their attention," says David Mozina, director of global\par fixed-income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research with Banc of America Securities in\par New York.\par Year-to-date, the benchmark Nikkei 225 stock average is down around 9% for the\par year, and more modestly in dollar terms. While still a poor performance, it\par compares well with the Dow Jones Industrial Average's slide of around 18%,\par Mozina notes. But the Nikkei's 3.4% fall earlier Friday could mark the start\par of a relapse on Japan's capital markets that will hurt major domestic\par financial institutions.\par "The slide in stock prices raises questions about the capital adequacy ratios\par at Japanese banks," wrote Marc Chandler, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with HSBC\par in New York. Japan's banks have extensive equity portfolios, which makes them\par highly vulnerable to a slide of Japanese stocks.\par Burdened with a mountain of bad and non-performing loans - estimated by\par Japanese regulator the Financial Services Agency last year at some Y150\par trillion - Japanese banks are already fragile.\par And the Nikkei 225 index's fall to 9591.03 points Friday leaves it below the\par key 9600 mark, getting close to a "danger zone" for Japanese banks because it\par depresses their capital adequacy ratios below 10%, says Mozina.\par    Falling Stocks Hurt Banks\par Japan's major banks would feel yet more pressure if the Nikkei 225 fell below\par 8100 points. That would send their capital ratios below the 8% recommended by\par the Bank for International Settlements, by which time banks would sell part of\par their extensive holdings in Japanese government bonds to cover themselves\par against slipping equities prices, Mozina said.\par Such a scenario would likely send JGB yields - which move inversely to their\par prices - jumping higher.\par The possibility of cratering equities prices occurring at the same time as a\par slide in Japanese sovereign debt prices could recreate a vicious circle.\par Because the banks would be among those institutions most jeopardized by this\par scenario, that would make bank failures more likely and step up pressure on\par the government to bailout creditors, should banks collapse.\par Japan's government debt-to-gross domestic product is currently around 140% and\par was a key consideration behind credit rating agency downgrades for the\par sovereign earlier this year.\par Yet the dark cloud that is again shadowing Japan's government finances may\par soon offer a silver lining in the form of the weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It's what\par Japanese exporters so desperately need.\par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , acting via the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , spent more than $30\par billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  designed to weaken the yen between\par late May and late June. While these actions failed, the market's own\par perception of Japan's economy - returning to a more somber reality - may now\par have the desired effect.\par -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires; john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1851GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djcs000020020726dy7q00ouh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
