\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL YIELD: Japan Bank Woes Reappear As Stocks,Yen Hit \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2002\par 20:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Maybe it's time to scrap the suspension of disbelief that's been behind the recent economic optimism that's driven the yen higher this year. \par \par \uc2  As Friday's swift, two-and-a-half-cent crumbling of the yen against the dollar showed, a fickle surge of confidence surrounding Japan's fledgling, export-driven economic revival seems to be unraveling. \par \par \uc2  For several months, U.S.-based fund managers have been snapping up short-term opportunities in Japanese equities, seeking a quick killing as stocks were rising, and the firming yen provided an additional fillip. \par \par \uc2  But problems in the U.S. have handed a blow to Japan's capital markets and may deal another to its economy. \par \par \uc2  The shock waves of turbulence in U.S. stock markets have spread globally. Worries that slower-than-expected growth in the U.S. might derail a global economic rebound have already reached Japan, the world's second biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  By midday Friday in New York, the dollar had soared to Y118.75, very sharply up from Y116.57 late Thursday in New York. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders who had pushed the yen steadily higher, up 11% against the dollar so far this year through Thursday, were rethinking that performance Friday. \par \par \uc2  In what may mark a turning point for the yen, market participants are once again placing emphasis on a fundamental concern that appears to have been temporarily overlooked: the Japanese economy remains structurally weak. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Deflationary Pressures\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite some recent data showing modest upward price movements, "you\par still have a situation with overriding deflationary pressures," says\par Marcel Kasumovich, economist with Goldman Sachs in New York. Any sign of\par strength in Japan's economy "is coming from the external sector, which\par itself is vulnerable," he said, adding that the unexpected fall in U.S.\par durable goods orders in June, reported Thursday, could bode badly for\par Japanese exporters.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against that backdrop, equities investors may be starting to fret that\par Japanese stocks are heading for a fall, after a short-lived period of\par stability.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "As U.S. fund managers look for profits to offset their losses" at\par home "Japan is bound to catch their attention," says David Mozina,\par director of global fixed-income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research with Banc\par of America Securities in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Year-to-date, the benchmark Nikkei 225 stock average is down around 9%\par for the year, and more modestly in dollar terms. While still a poor\par performance, it compares well with the Dow Jones Industrial Average's\par slide of around 18%, Mozina notes. But the Nikkei's 3.4% fall earlier\par Friday could mark the start of a relapse on Japan's capital markets that\par will hurt major domestic financial institutions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The slide in stock prices raises questions about the capital adequacy\par ratios at Japanese banks," wrote Marc Chandler, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist with HSBC in New York. Japan's banks have extensive equity\par portfolios, which makes them highly vulnerable to a slide of Japanese\par stocks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Burdened with a mountain of bad and non-performing loans - estimated\par by Japanese regulator the Financial Services Agency last year at some\par Y150 trillion - Japanese banks are already fragile.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And the Nikkei 225 index's fall to 9591.03 points Friday leaves it\par below the key 9600 mark, getting close to a "danger zone" for Japanese\par banks because it depresses their capital adequacy ratios below 10%, says\par Mozina.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Falling Stocks Hurt Banks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's major banks would feel yet more pressure if the Nikkei 225\par fell below 8100 points. That would send their capital ratios below the\par 8% recommended by the Bank for International Settlements, by which time\par banks would sell part of their extensive holdings in Japanese government\par bonds to cover themselves against slipping equities prices, Mozina said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Such a scenario would likely send JGB yields - which move inversely to\par their prices - jumping higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The possibility of cratering equities prices occurring at the same\par time as a slide in Japanese sovereign debt prices could recreate a\par vicious circle.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Because the banks would be among those institutions most jeopardized\par by this scenario, that would make bank failures more likely and step up\par pressure on the government to bailout creditors, should banks collapse.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's government debt-to-gross domestic product is currently around\par 140% and was a key consideration behind credit rating agency downgrades\par for the sovereign earlier this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet the dark cloud that is again shadowing Japan's government finances\par may soon offer a silver lining in the form of the weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It's\par what Japanese exporters so desperately need.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , acting via the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , spent more than\par $30 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  designed to weaken the yen\par between late May and late June. While these actions failed, the market's\par own perception of Japan's economy - returning to a more somber reality -\par may now have the desired effect.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires; john.parry@dowjones.com;\par 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4083 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020726dy7q00bf5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
