\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ to keep banks' current account targets - Jiji. \b0\par\par\uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2002\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 25 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Policy Board member Hidehiko Haru said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably maintain the present target for the amount of liquidity it supplies to the financial system, Jiji news agency reported on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Under its "quantitative easing" policy, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  targets the amount of funds banks keep in their current accounts at the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  Since mid-December, the target has been 10-15 trillion yen ($85.68-128.5 billion), raised to that level at a time when there were widespread worries of a financial system failure towards the end of the 2001/02 fiscal year in March. \par \par \uc2  "Our measures played a major role in securing stability in financial markets and preventing the economy from deteriorating further, and we must continue to supply ample funds," Haru said in an interview with Jiji. \par \par \uc2  "Our basic principle is that we will maintain the present current account deposit balance for now," he added. \par \par \uc2  Haru said inflation-targeting - a policy option advocated by some economists and policymakers - was worth studying but added that the time was not ripe to adopt such a measure. \par \par \uc2  "I think inflation-targeting is one option," he said. "I don't think we should make it an absolute taboo as long as we study the effects and the impact on society in depth." \par \par \uc2  "(But) if it becomes necessary, we must consider whether it will be decided by the BOJ alone or with the government, what kind of policy we can take once we adopt it," Haru said. \par \par \uc2  Another board member, Toshikatsu Fukuma, said in a separate interview with Jiji that he saw no need yet to embark on unconventional easing measures, such as buying foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  "An expansion of quantitative easing is possible without taking unorthodox measures," Fukuma said. \par \par \uc2  Turning to the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Fukuma said the yen's rapid strengthening was not favourable for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led economic recovery, adding that yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities had helped stabilise the market. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also had a positive impact on Europe because the dollar's broad-based weakness and its fall against the euro was not good for the European economies, Fukuma said. ($1=116.70 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020725dy7p00hav\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
