\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar struggles, sentiment bearish despite Wall St. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2002\par 05:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 25 (Reuters) - The dollar struggled against the yen and the euro in Tokyo morning trade on Thursday after failing to draw any benefit from a big overnight rally in U.S. share prices. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment towards the dollar was still bearish as there was no real proof that worries over accounting practices at U.S. firms had gone away and U.S. share prices could easily come under attack again, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average rallied from early five-year lows on Wednesday to finish at 8,191.29, up 488.95 points, or 6.35 percent. That rise was the second largest jump in points ever for the blue chip benchmark. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq Composite index rose 4.98 percent. \par \par \uc2  "For the time being, the market will be careful about selling dollars too aggressively after yesterday's steep rallies in the U.S. stock market," said Koichi Abe, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  section manager at Aozora Bank. \par \par \uc2  "But I'm sure the downside risk for the dollar is still there," Abe said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar moved in tight ranges throughout the morning as many traders were nervous about taking fresh positions after experiencing heavy volatility this week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar reached a 1-= week high of 117.93 yen and a high of $0.9844 per euro on Tuesday, helped to a large extent by U.S. banks and fund operators, collecting cash from overseas investments to cover losses on U.S. shares. \par \par \uc2  PARITY \par \par \uc2  At 0315 GMT, the euro had edged back up above parity against the dollar and was quoted at $1.0006/11 against $0.9940/45 in late New York trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was at 116.29/34 compared with 115.83 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 116.21/24 yen against the late New York level of 116.45/55 yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Without short-covering from U.S. operators, the dollar is extremely heavy. In this mood the dollar should be going down again," said a spot dealer at a European bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "After this week's volatility, I'm sure many are taking a wait-and-see stance. The market also wants to see whether the U.S. stock market can extend the gains made last night." \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's capital flow survey showed on Thursday that foreign investors sold a net 21.2 billion yen ($181.6 million) in Japanese stocks in the week ended July 19, a fifth straight week of net selling. \par \par \uc2  Selling of Japanese equities by foreign investors reflects a struggle by European and U.S. institutions to reduce risk exposure amid a tumble in global equity markets, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The total amount of net Japanese stock selling since mid-June has reached 343.3 billion yen, compared with net purchases of 931.7 billion yen in May. \par \par \uc2  Bargain-hunting orders are lined up below 116 yen but a drop below 115.80 yen may spur downward momentum. Major stop-loss orders were rumoured from a 1-= year low of 115.50 yen and below that level, the spot dealer said. \par \par \uc2  But the market was expected to be careful about selling the dollar heavily below 116 yen due to fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Thursday the ministry was always considering various steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, adding that excessive exchange rate movements were undesirable. \par \par \uc2  Asked about recent volatility in U.S. stock prices, Mizoguchi said: "Our view on the strength of the real economy of the United States is unchanged." He added that the fundamental strength of the U.S. economy was working to stabilise financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar slipped back against the Swiss franc reflecting its broad-based selling Thursday morning. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 1.4508/12 francs from Wednesday's late U.S. level of 1.4577/87. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0302 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 24 Jul.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9984   0.9951   +0.33    +5.14   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen               116.36   116.65   -0.25    +1.67   114.45\par  Euro/yen              116.21   116.11   +0.09    +6.90   108.71\par  Dlr/swiss             1.4508   1.4573   -0.45    -9.39   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr               1.5759   1.5726   +0.21    +5.06   1.5000\par  Can/dlr               1.5630   1.5625   +0.03    +4.80   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr               0.5437   0.5448   -0.20    -2.81   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss            1.4494   1.4509   -0.10    -4.78   1.5221\par  Euro/stg              0.6323   0.6328   -0.08    -0.03   0.6325.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020725dy7p006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
