\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar surges broadly, US repatriation cited. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2002\par 07:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 23 (Reuters) - The dollar staged a rare surge in Asia on Tuesday morning, pushing the euro below parity for the first time in a week, despite continued falls in U.S. share markets. \par \par \uc2  While many trader saw the dollar's rebound as a natural correction after its recent drop, some said sharp slides in global share prices may have forced U.S. investors to take profits in the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make up for losses in equities. \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be repatriation of funds to the United States," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Japanese trading house Itochu Corp. \par \par \uc2  Major stock indices for Britain, France and Germany all posted a loss of around 5 percent on Monday, while the U.S. Dow Jones average lost about three percent. \par \par \uc2  "With share prices having fallen this much, U.S. investors are selling some funds they are holding, which has forced fund operators to cash in their assets overseas," he said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0230 GMT, the euro had slipped to $0.9992/95, nearly a full cent down from $1.0075/78 in late U.S. trade and way below its multi-year high of $1.0220 hit on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Europe's common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also dipped to 116.79/88 yen from 117.19 yen. \par \par \uc2  The greenback rose to 116.89/92 yen from 116.31/39 in late U.S. trade, building gains from a 17-month low of 115.50 hit last week. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to curtail the yen's rise lent some support to the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven days between late May and late June. \par \par \uc2  POLICY COORDINATION? \par \par \uc2  Some traders speculate that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other countries could co-ordinate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the dollar from its downward trend, as waning confidence in U.S. equities and in the greenback appears to be affecting global equity markets. \par \par \uc2  "If world share prices fall further, there is a higher chance of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Akio Makabe, senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  However, many dealers are sceptical about the prospect of such action, given the apparent indifference of Washington to the dollar's recent fall and many European policymakers' satisfaction with the rise in the euro. \par \par \uc2  Still, even mere talk of joint action could help the dollar in a market nervous about whether world policymakers will take steps to soothe recent turmoil in financial markets, Makabe said. \par \par \uc2  Highlighting such nervousness, a rumour swirled in the market in the morning that Tokyo was to issue a statement to clarify its determination to stop the yen's rise, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also strengthened sharply against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The greenback rose to a 10-week high against the Canadian dollar, to C$1.5705 from C$1.5642 in late Canadian trade. \par \par \uc2  It jumped even more sharply - over one percent - against the Swiss franc, to 1.4588/95 franc from 1.4430/40. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar also sank about one percent to 54.10 U.S cents from 54.64/69. \par \par \uc2  Such broad-based strength made has caused traders to review their pessimism on the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar didn't fall during the last couple of days even when U.S. share prices fell. So personally, I am left with the impression that the dollar's rout might have came to a halt," said a dealer at Standard Chartered Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  In the last two business days, the Dow has shed about 7.5 percent while the Nasdaq composite index has dropped about five percent. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar could soon test 120 yen," the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  Others remained sceptical. \par \par \uc2  "This situation resembles the period after Black Monday (in 1987)," said Nakajima of Itochu Corp. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar held out for about a week after Black Monday because of repatriation, but then it crashed." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0305 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   22 Jul. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9977   1.0079   -1.01   +12.05   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        116.95   116.24   +0.61   -11.15   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    116.69   117.18   -0.42    -0.47   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.4585   1.4422   +1.13   -12.11   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.5721   1.5795   -0.47    +8.11   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5773   1.5640   +0.85    -0.90   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5419   0.5477   -1.06    +6.13   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4555   1.4544   +0.08    -1.52   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6342   0.6381   -0.61    +3.58   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...  Debt... Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par