\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Gains Against Yen, Euro In Defiance of Slumping Stocks \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2002\par 00:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- After a bruising few weeks, the dollar showed some mettle Monday, regaining ground against the euro, the yen and other widely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defy another shaky performance on U.S. stock markets. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar was at 116.32 yen, well above 115.69 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.0067, fairly close to its session lows and down from $1.0132 in late New York trading Friday. Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.4459 francs, up from 1.4407 francs late Friday. The pound was at $1.5772, down from $1.5781 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Some of the so-called commodity-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Canadian and Australian dollars, whose economies are linked to the commodities cycle and which had performed strongly earlier this year, slipped against the dollar Monday. \par \par \uc2  By midafternoon, the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had dropped over 2.3 cents against the U.S. dollar for its largest intraday collapse since August 1998. The Canadian unit then stabilized and retraced slightly after it hit a two-month low at 1.5643 Canadian dollars. \par \par \uc2  "People are realizing that there is a risk of a double-dip recession or a global slowdown, which is growing with this nosedive in global stock markets ... that can't be ignored," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics in Essex, Conn. \par \par \uc2  After last week's intense focus on the woes of WorldCom Inc., which filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy over the weekend and the accompanying savaging of U.S. stocks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants were turning their attention to worries that both U.S. and global consumer spending might start to suffer as a result of equity markets' descent. That could take the wind out of the global economic rebound's sails; a consideration which was hurting some of the higher yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday. \par \par \uc2  Canada and Australia's central banks have been among the most aggressive raising rates this year, helping drive their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. But now, "people are questioning the sustainability of raising rates in a softening global economy," Mr. Gilmore added. \par \par \uc2  On worries about the strength of the global economic recovery, oil producer Norway and neighbor Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell, most notably against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Yet the dollar was not merely capitalizing on these other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' weakness, but appeared to be gathering some momentum of its own. The greenback seemed to be finding its feet, fleetingly at least, in a repetition of a pattern that emerged late Friday when the greenback had decoupled from the stock-market descents that have been a key catalyst in its fall during previous months. \par \par \uc2  "It's going to be very difficult for us to see the dollar go weaker from current levels over the next couple of weeks, even if equity markets continue to erode," said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer in New York. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, few hailed the dollar's more robust performance as a change in direction. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's firmer tone still remains fairly fragile: it does not mean a new trend," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. Hedge funds may have been among those behind the profit-taking that helped give the dollar a lift Monday, Mr. Chandler wrote in his daily research note. \par \par \uc2  In the wake of WorldCom filing for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection Sunday -- the biggest in U.S. history with total assets of $107 billion -- \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said the dollar was showing some potential to shrug off the slew of such bad news surrounding corporate America that is already priced into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Coming into this week, with WorldCom out of the way, it feels there is room for dollar consolidation here," said Jim McCormick, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Lehman Brothers in New York. \par \par \uc2  Medium term, however, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts continue to watch the stock market's dismal performance as a factor that could stoke foreign disenchantment with this category of U.S. assets, hence steepening the dollar's decline. \par \par \uc2  "Households, pension funds, insurance companies, and foreign investors are simultaneously withdrawing their support of the stock market," wrote Tony Crescenzi, chief bond market strategist at Miller Tabak & Co. in New York Monday. "And the selling has become a self-feeding vicious cycle," he added. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors carry a lot of weight in the U.S., where they account for around 13% of the equity market, 24% of the corporate bond market and 40% of the Treasury bond market. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, persistent fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in an effort to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were given an edge by Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa's remarks earlier Monday. \par \par \uc2  "If there are excessive ups and downs, we are always prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mr. Shiokawa told the Lower House Budget Committee. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was firming against the euro partly because of some weak construction spending data in Germany, Europe's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  The common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also under some pressure because of increasing scrutiny of the accounting standards of European countries, said David Leaver, senior trader at Gain Capital, leading to buying of the Swiss franc, more of a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro could retreat toward parity with the dollar again before the end of this week, a level which the euro broke above last Monday for the first time since February 2000, Mr. Leaver said. \par \par \uc2  -- Kristyne E. Demske and Tyler Lifton contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020722dy7m006mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
