\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Defiant BOJ Hayami: It's Weak Dlr, Not Strong Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe and James Simms \par \uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2002\par 13:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Brushing aside claims he has caused confusion with irresponsible comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a defiant Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami stuck to his assessment Friday that the crux of recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements has been a weak dollar, not a strong yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is weak against the euro and the yen and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to problems on the U.S. side," Hayami told a committee of Parliament. \par \par \uc2  Although the Japanese government sold yen in record amounts through the BOJ in May and June \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to brake the yen's rise, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief has stubbornly focused on the weakening greenback, not the surging yen. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, he has publicly backed the government's efforts to tame the yen and later Friday he told reporters, "I don't think there's much reason for the dollar to continue slumping from here." \par \par \uc2  But even while doing so, Hayami has insisted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is weakening globally as investors pull out of dollar-denominated assets amid fears over the growing U.S. budget and current-account deficits. \par \par \uc2  His emphasis on the dollar and U.S. factors came under fire from MPs who said Hayami, an inveterate yen bull, appeared unconcerned about the yen's climb - which the government fears could derail Japan's nascent, export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry, which sets \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, almost daily threatens further yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the economy from the ravages of a too-strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    I'm A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Man\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the 77-year old Hayami, challenged by opposition Liberal Party\par lawmaker Masayuki Fujishima, denied he was being irresponsible or\par opening himself to misinterpretation, saying he knew exactly what he was\par talking about.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Before returning to the Bank of Japan as governor, I spent most of my\par 34 years (at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -related areas,"\par Hayami said. "I am a man who was brought up riding the waves of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It's with that experience, and a stint in the interim as head of\par Japanese trading firm Nissho Iwai Corp. (J.NIW or 8063), that he is now\par saying that the dollar is suffering from a global selloff.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Hayami also said excessive movements in the dollar/yen exchange\par rate would harm the economy and that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was watching the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stock markets with caution.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If anyone is confused on his position, he said, it's because his\par remarks were misrepresented in a Japanese newspaper - which he declined\par to name.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "On the other hand, the same comments have been accurately reported by\par The Asian Wall Street Journal," Hayami said. "It's sad that there is\par such a difference."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hayami didn't specify an article, but the Journal carried a Dow Jones\par Newswires story July 12 that said, "Mr. Hayami told a parliamentary\par committee that movements in dollar/yen rates seem to be stemming 'not\par from a strong yen, but from the dollar being weak across the board.'"\par The article also noted that Hayami expressed support for the Finance\par Ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as an excessive strengthening\par of the yen has negative effects for the Japanese economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop the dollar from falling is not a bad thing," the\par article quoted Hayami as saying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Several Trillion Yen On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The ministry hasn't sold yen in three weeks even though the dollar\par sank Tuesday to a 17-month low of Y115.50. But Finance Minister Masajuro\par Shiokawa reiterated Friday that the government is "worried" about the\par yen's rise "and that is why we have taken various measures."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A deputy finance minister, Takayoshi Taniguchi, said that a Y1 fall in\par the dollar from Y115 would knock 0.01 percentage point off Japan's gross\par domestic product.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Zembei Mizoguchi, a senior Shiokawa deputy appearing with the other\par ministry officials in a different committee from Hayami, also confirmed\par that Japan has spent several trillion yen in its seven rounds of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from May 22 to June 28.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's quite an amount," said Mizoguchi, the ministry's International\par Bureau chief. "Several trillion yen sounds about right."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In fact, ministry figures indicate the government spent close to a\par record Y3.3 trillion in just the first six of the seven rounds of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Receipts and payments of Treasury funds for May and June showed\par deficits totaling Y3.3 trillion, almost all of which likely reflected\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - more than all of September's record Y3.2 trillion worth\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A ministry official said other factors - such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's transactions\par with the International Monetary Fund - can sway the account, but\par nonintervention factors typically amount to only several tens of\par billions of yen a month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The ministry will release the exact dates, amounts and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par involved in its April-June \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in early August.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Miyako Takebe and James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com; james.simms@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020719dy7j000nj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
