\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Advances Against Yen, But Still Feels Equities Pressure \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,051 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2002\par 01:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar moved higher against the yen Thursday on persistent fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might revive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  designed to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but lost some ground against the euro late in the day as retreating U.S. equities weighed on the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday in New York, the dollar was at 116.69 yen, in the middle of its trading range for the global session and up from 116.30 yen in late New York trading Wednesday. The euro was at $1.0096, close to its session highs and up from $1.0076 late Wednesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.4509 francs, up from 1.4545 francs, while sterling was at $1.5705, little changed from $1.5704. \par \par \uc2  Compared with recent highly volatile sessions, the dollar stabilized within fairly narrow ranges. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has had an enormous selloff this year: we have run out of reasons for the time being to add to the weakness of the dollar, so people have taken some profits," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Barings Capital Markets in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro has rallied some 13% against the dollar so far this year, while the yen has advanced around 11%. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants said the wave of dollar selling was playing itself out, while strategists mused whether the next leg of the dollar's expected decline will be more gradual than the last. \par \par \uc2  "Particularly on the euro/dollar axis, you saw a good move to parity and slightly higher; now, traders are peering into the second half and looking at growth and interest rate differentials," said Joseph Quinlan, an international economist at Morgan Stanley in New York. \par \par \uc2  In order for the euro, which broke above one-to-one parity with the dollar on Monday for the first time since February 2000, to make further gains against the dollar, the European economy would have to "break away from the U.S. and outperform," Mr. Quinlan said. He is skeptical that will happen and expects the dollar's losses against the euro to be capped as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants start to focus more closely on the economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, the dollar still showed some vulnerability to some weaker-than-expected U.S. economic data Thursday and skittishness as it closely mimicked the ebbs and flows of a fickle stock market. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday in New York, after hovering in a fairly narrowly mixed range most of the day, major U.S. stock market averages took a late fall, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average down 106 points, or 1.25%, to 8436. \par \par \uc2  Aside from receiving an indirect impact from the flurry of earnings reports feeding into stock-price gyrations, the dollar gave a feeble, fleeting hurrah to weekly initial jobless claims data falling to a 17-month low and hinting at a stronger labor market than expected. \par \par \uc2  But later, the greenback was bruised when the Philadelphia Federal Reserve reported its index of factory business activity slipped to a much weaker-than-anticipated 6.6 reading after having surged to 22.2 in June. \par \par \uc2  That indication that the pace of manufacturing growth might be decelerating, albeit in one area of the U.S., started to erode the dollar's earlier session gains which had sent it trading above the 117.00 yen mark for the first time in four days earlier in the global session. \par \par \uc2  Nagging fears that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen -- exacerbated by Japanese policy-makers turning up the rhetorical heat Thursday -- helped the dollar advance against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Japan is always in contact with other countries regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and remains ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the need arises, senior Finance Ministry bureaucrat Zembei Mizoguchi said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained the central market theme," with Mr. Mizoguchi sticking to "the latest MOF tactic of hinting at possible joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," wrote Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with UBS Warburg in London, in a daily research note. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended a governing council meeting with no announcement. Amid Europe's generally sluggish economic recovery, recent data have shown price pressures easing, so the ECB hadn't been expected to change interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Among the so-called "commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " whose economies performance is perceived to be quite closely correlated to the commodities cycle, the Canadian dollar showed a weaker tone. Its recent swoon is a temporary consequence of the U.S. dollar's rebound and the decision to drop Canadian companies from the Standard & Poor's 500 Index, but it will soon give way to renewed strength in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  After climbing to its highest level in 17 months on June 28 when the U.S. dollar slumped to $1.5028 Canadian, the Canadian dollar has faltered in the last few weeks, enabling the U.S. dollar to reclaim significant lost ground, with the U.S. dollar trading around C$1.5440 midafternoon Thursday in North America. \par \par \uc2  Another factor weighing on the Canadian dollar is that "there is a question about the preemptive tightening the Bank of Canada is doing ... and whether the demand side [of the economy] can sustain the rising interest-rate environment" that is weighing on the Canadian dollar, said Marcel Kasumovich, an economist at Goldman Sachs in New York. The Bank of Canada raised its key interest rate earlier this week by a quarter percentage point. \par \par \uc2  Among emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Brazilian real got a major boost Thursday, recovering from Wednesday's worst ever close, to 2.851 reals to the dollar late Thursday from 2.897 reals to the dollar the prior day, lifted by reports that International Monetary Fund First Deputy Managing Director Anne Krueger will meet with Brazilian President Fernando Henrique Cardoso and Finance Minister Pedro Malan in Brasilia next week, consolidating the impression among market participants that the IMF may prove supportive in future loan agreements to the country. \par \par \uc2  -- Don Curren in Toronto, Adriana Arai in Sao Paulo and Nicholas Hastings in London contributed to this report \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020718dy7i008y9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
