\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar keeps handle on yen, loses grip on euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2002\par 23:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 18 (Reuters) - The dollar clung to small gains against the yen on Thursday, helped by lingering fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced lower against the euro as Wall Street stocks tumbled. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that as long as U.S. stocks keep sinking, the dollar - which slumped below parity with the euro and 17-month lows against the yen this week - will be hard-pressed to catch a break. \par \par \uc2  "Where Wall Street goes, so goes the dollar. For the rest of the week the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's going to be focused on earnings reports from various corporations and digesting the effect on stocks," said Patrick Collins, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Ruesch International. \par \par \uc2  The euro ended the New York session at $1.0110, up 0.39 percent from the prior U.S. close, after wiping out slight early gains by the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar held modestly firmer on the day against the yen at 116.54 yen , up 0.21 percent. \par \par \uc2  On Wall Street, blue-chip stocks erased early gains and moved into negative territory in afternoon dealings as dismal profit reports and forecasts injected fresh pessimism into an already dour market. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average closed down 1.56 percent and the Nasdaq Composite index finished off 2.88 percent. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE JAWBONING \par \par \uc2  Despite the stock losses, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to keep its chin up against the yen. Traders credited Japanese monetary authorities, who weighed in during Tokyo trading hours with comments aimed at stemming the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi reiterated that Japan would take action in the market, if needed, adding that the ministry was always in contact with overseas authorities on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters. Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa has made similar comments. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers puzzled over the remarks, which some interpreted as raising the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , worried that yen strength will strangle its export-led economy, has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen on at least seven days since late May, spending an estimated $30 billion in yen. \par \par \uc2  However, a number of U.S. traders said they were skeptical that U.S. or European monetary authorities would join Japan in a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to prop up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Many market players believe Washington tacitly approves a weaker dollar, which should help U.S. manufacturers ahead of mid-term elections in November. In addition, European officials have made clear they welcome the recent gains in the euro and do not see the dollar's recent fall as a threat to the economy. \par \par \uc2  "There's this lingering fear due to the barrage of remarks from Japanese officials, but I feel the U.S. administration would be reluctant to engage in joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen," said Collins of Ruesch International. "Of course, only time will tell. \par \par \uc2  U.S. DATA DISAPPOINTS \par \par \uc2  Disappointing economic data dragged on the dollar early in the U.S. session. The Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia said its business conditions index dropped to 6.6 in July - the lowest level since December 2001 - from 22.2 in June. \par \par \uc2  Economists had expected a drop to 17.0. A reading above zero indicates growth, and the index has shown expansion for seven straight months. \par \par \uc2  The dollar tumbled a quarter cent against the euro and a quarter yen shortly after the Philly Fed data was released. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020718dy7i0137g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
