\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar seesaws, still dogged by nervousness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2002\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 18 (Reuters) - The dollar seesawed in Asia on Thursday, still weighed by suspicions about accounting practices at U.S. firms even after U.S. stocks rebounded the previous day. \par \par \uc2  While wariness about further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept dollar bears sidelined, the greenback was at the mercy of concerns hanging over Wall Street share markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar did enjoy solace in morning trade after the Dow Jones industrial average ended its seven-day losing streak on Wednesday, ending up 0.82 percent. The Nasdaq composite index also ended up 1.60 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback slipped in Asian afternoon trade on a Washington Post report that America Online Inc had boosted its revenue by "unconventional" deals. \par \par \uc2  The fall highlighting the market's vulnerability to U.S. accounting woes, sparked by the demise of energy trader Enron Corp. \par \par \uc2  At 0530 GMT, the dollar stood at 116.43/49 yen, off the day's high of 116.66. But it maintained slim gains compared with 116.31 yen in late New York on Wednesday and a 17-month low of 115.50 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was generally solid versus the dollar. It firmed near $1.01 before easing back to $1.0078/80, still slightly higher than $1.0068 in late U.S. trade. On Wednesday, it hit a 2-= year peak of $1.0162. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 117.32/42 yen compared with 117.15 yen in late U.S. trading. \par \par \uc2  GREENSPAN BOOST \par \par \uc2  Earlier the dollar recovered some ground, as Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan gave traders pause for thought with unusually outspoken remarks on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his testimony to Congress on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan said: "A considerable volume of market commentary in recent weeks has suggested that concerns about earnings prospects and the proliferating revelations of serious governance and accounting issues have contributed not only to lower equity prices but also to a decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  value of the dollar." \par \par \uc2  But he said he wanted to raise a flag of caution regarding such commentary. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that Greenspan said much more than usual about the dollar suggested he was keen on the issue, which generated caution among traders about selling it down too far, at least for now," said Minori Takeuchi, vice president of research ini the global trading division of Chase Manhattan Bank. \par \par \uc2  Masamichi Koike, senior vice president at Sumitomo Mitsui Bank, said: "Greenspan's words raised speculation that he is concerned about the dollar's fall," said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  CAUTION \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also helped by wariness about possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Thursday the ministry was always in contact with overseas authorities on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters, adding that it would take action in the market if needed. \par \par \uc2  The reference to being in touch with overseas authorities, more specific than the usual jawboning by Japanese officials to curb the yen and coming after a similar comment on Wednesday, caught the market's attention. \par \par \uc2  Still, the warning rang hollow to many dealers, who think co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar is not on the cards given unwillingness of U.S. and European policymakers. \par \par \uc2  "Co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely. In the U.S, some House members are saying even the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wrong," said Hidehiko Inamura, vice president at Citibank N.A. in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Many market players think Washington tacitly approves a weaker dollar, which should help U.S. manufacturers ahead of mid-term elections in November, despite its official strong-dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  European officials have made clear they welcome the recent gains in the euro and do not see the dollar's recent fall as a threat to the economy. \par \par \uc2  "If Japan acts alone, it is unlikely to succeed," Inamura said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times from late May to the end of June but failed to change the dollar's bearish trend. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0554 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   17 Jul. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       1.0076   1.0071   +0.05   +13.16   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        116.49   116.30   +0.16   -11.50   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    117.38   117.13   +0.21    +0.12   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.4542   1.4540   +0.01   -12.37   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.5683   1.5703   -0.13    +7.85   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5390   1.5393   -0.02    -3.31   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5514   0.5512   +0.04    +7.99   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4654   1.4652   +0.01    -0.85   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6420   0.6410   +0.16    +4.85   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 