\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar crawls up, but confidence still fragile. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2002\par 09:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 17 (Reuters) - The dollar dragged itself up from 17-month lows against the yen in Tokyo on Wednesday, but dealers doubted the respite would last long given the mysterious lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  action from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  After diving to 115.50 yen in overseas trade, bargain-hunting by Japanese pension funds and repatriation demand from U.S. operators helped the dollar to a session high of 116.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  But it was back down to near 115.90 in late trade as the market proved unable to shake off the gloom from the U.S. corporate accounting scandals, despite a reassuring Senate testimony by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  As of 0618 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 115.92 yen against 115.63 yen in late U.S. trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "It has basically settled around 115.90, trapped from both sides," said Koichi Abe, manager at Aozora Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading section. \par \par \uc2  The euro firmed slightly to $1.0127 against $1.0115 in late U.S. trade. It rose to a fresh 2-= year high of $1.0148 on Tuesday, with many dealers forecasting the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would test $1.02 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Japanese retail investors were showing interest in putting their summer bonuses into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, helping the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Support also came from U.S. operators who were seen shifting their funds from overseas instruments back into dollars, either to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk or to take overseas profits to cover for U.S. equity losses. \par \par \uc2  But the rise was capped due to heavy Japanese exporter offers around 116.50 yen, with major sell orders said to be placed above 117 yen. \par \par \uc2  WHERE IS THE BOJ? \par \par \uc2  Despite verbal warnings from Japanese officials anxious that the yen's rise will derail the economy's export-based recovery, the Bank of Japan was again absent from the market. \par \par \uc2  "They would like to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Tokyo time because if they ask the ECB or the Fed, it would be difficult to act in a timely fashion," said Aozara's Abe. \par \par \uc2  "But since the fresh lows were reached in New York, it is making it difficult for them to step in." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven occasions between May 22 and June 28 and is estimated to have bought a total of about $30 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the Japanese authorities since kept out of the market for two weeks. \par \par \uc2  "In this mood, the dollar is likely to fall below the key support of 115 yen and once the level is broken, many would forecast it to drop towards 110 yen," said Michihisa Tanimoto, deputy general manager at Sumitomo Life Insurance. \par \par \uc2  "Japan could step in at any time, but I feel the key to the success of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  depends on whether the monetary authorities can present their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy clearly to the market," Tanimoto said. \par \par \uc2  Senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said the dollar's weakness was unlikely to be long lasting, given the strength of the U.S. economy, Japan's Jiji news agency said. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi, the head of the ministry's international bureau, also reiterated that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged - that it would take necessary action when needed. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was firmer at 117.38 yen against 117.04 yen in late U.S. trade. Many profit-takers and exporters were rumoured to be placing heavy offers between 117.50 and 118.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan will deliver a testimony to the House of Representatives later on Wednesday but market players are not expecting anything new to emerge from it. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan failed to help sentiment in U.S. asset markets, or in the dollar in testimony to the Senate on Tuesday in which he said the economy was improving. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan's remarks for a while seemed to reassure Wall Street, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average crawling back to mild losses from triple-digit declines in early activity. \par \par \uc2  But heavy selling reared its head again later driving the average to its seventh-straight decline. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0551 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   16 Jul. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       1.0128   1.0115   +0.13   +13.75   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        115.92   115.83   +0.08   -11.93   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    117.39   117.16   +0.20    +0.13   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.4481   1.4492   -0.08   -12.73   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.5744   1.5751   -0.04    +8.27   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5380   1.5375   +0.03    -3.37   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5560   0.5570   -0.18    +8.89   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4670   1.4662   +0.05    -0.74   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6428   0.6420   +0.12    +4.98   0.6123\par \f2