\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Falls Slows Its Fall as Stocks Mark Time \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,071 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2002\par 01:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar got a reprieve Wednesday, helped by a few signs of resilience on U.S. stock markets and consolidating what is likely to prove a technically-driven and fairly short-lived rebound, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said. \par \par \uc2  "People are broadly trading the dollar on the basis of stocks," said Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics in Valhalla, N.Y. Some stabilization of U.S. equities Wednesday after a string of dismal daily performances "has suggested the dollar take a breather," he added. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the dollar was at YEN 116.30, off its session highs, but up from YEN 115.89 in late New York dealings Tuesday. The euro was at $1.0076, down from $1.0109 late Tuesday. The dollar was at 1.4545 Swiss francs, up from 1.4509 francs late Tuesday, while sterling was at $1.5704, down from $1.5736, after minutes from a Bank of England monetary policy meeting hinted that the BOE is prepared to postpone rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was stronger against the yen amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and higher against the euro after stronger euro-zone economic data failed to give the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  much of a boost. \par \par \uc2  In the wake of the second leg of Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's two-day semiannual congressional testimony, the dollar held onto most of its gains from earlier in the global session. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Greenspan reiterated his view that the euro's reattainment of parity with the dollar is "an arbitrary issue" which holds "no economic or financial significance" and his remarks had little direct influence on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Among emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Brazil's real slumped to its weakest ever late-day levels Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates by half a percentage point to 18.00%. The dollar rose to 2.897 reals from 2.871 reals late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise move, reducing its reference lending rate for the first time in four months, renewed an effort to pursue long-term growth objectives as inflation appears to be under control, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Barring a continuing collapse in equities, which is certainly a risk, a dollar consolidation over the next week or two is reasonable" to expect, said Jim McCormick, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Lehman Brothers in New York. He anticipates that the euro could recede to just below parity with the dollar, while the yen could retreat between 1% and 2% to the dollar over that period, before the greenback re-embarks on its longer-term decline. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday, the dollar was around its lowest levels since February 2000 against the euro and still around 10-month lows against the yen. After having lost some 13% against the euro and 11% against the yen since the start of this year, the dollar was overdue for at least a fleeting rebound, strategists said. \par \par \uc2  JP Morgan's measures of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market positioning showed Wednesday that the dollar had become oversold for the first time in two months, giving added incentive to bid up the greenback somewhat, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at the bank said. \par \par \uc2  Another factor helping the dollar regain some ground specifically against the yen was traders' continuing fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's widely expected upgrade of its assessment of the domestic economy for the fifth month in a row failed to bolster the yen. Instead of focusing on signs of Japan's fledgling economic recovery, the market "still seems far more concerned by if and when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 " again, wrote Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with UBS Warburg in London in a daily research note. \par \par \uc2  There is a "growing view" among \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants, Mr. Jalinoos wrote that in the wake of comments by Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa earlier Wednesday, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  only stopped its unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  [believing they couldn't work in isolation] in order to let the dollar fall sharply, and force others to seriously consider concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Mr. Shiokawa helped to promote fears of some coordinated action by warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in an "extremely important phase" now and that his officials are in touch with other countries on the matter. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  acting through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times in efforts to weaken the yen since late May, the last of which was on June 28. Despite spending more than $30 billion, these efforts have proved fairly fruitless. \par \par \uc2  "They just couldn't be effective going against the falling dollar tide," but if the dollar were to fall to YEN 115.00, "that may be pushing their limit and they [the MOF] may try again," said Larry Brickman, interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar is emerging from under the heavy selling pressure it struggled with earlier this week, that helped the common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  break above the psychologically critical bar of dollar-parity Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro then moved above $1.01 Tuesday, to its highest levels since January 2000, but it has receded somewhat since. \par \par \uc2  A bigger-than-expected rise in euro-zone industrial production, which was up 0.1% in June after falling by 0.7% in May, didn't provide the euro with much of a lift. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, a rethink about the state of the world economic recovery as some fret that the dent to U.S. stock prices might rein back the U.S. economic rebound to some extent, was also taking its toll on some of the dollar's other widely-traded rivals. \par \par \uc2  In one "indication of a growing pessimism around global growth prospects," the Australian dollar's slippage during the past few days has been joined by the Canadian dollar weakening Wednesday, for the first time since early this year when both "had led the bearish trend" against the U.S. dollar, Mr. McCormick of Lehman Brothers said. Late Wednesday, the Australian dollar was at $0.5507, down from $0.5570. \par \par \uc2  -- Anthony Dovkants and Paula Lace in Sao Paulo, Gerald Jeffris in Brasilia and Nicholas Hastings in London contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021030dy7h005k4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
