\par\par \b \uc2  CHARTING ASIA: EUR/JPY Bullish After Trendline Holds  \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Torchia  \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2002\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.)  \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have focused for the last several months on the U.S. dollar's dramatic slide against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the charts say there's a good cross-trade opportunity in the second half of this year: the euro against the yen.  \par \par \uc2  EUR/JPY's bounce from its 115.08 low in the past couple of days has left it looking bullish on both short- and longer-term charts.  \par \par \uc2  The bounce confirmed a multiple-point uptrend line from May 2001, confirmed support in the vicinity of the 200-day moving average (now 114.74), and returned EUR/JPY above its 100-day average (now at 116.25). All of these events are positive signals for coming weeks.  \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, daily stochastics staged a bullish crossover this morning, implying at least several days of strength are probably ahead; it appears to be the first clean bullish crossover since late May, when EUR/JPY began a month-long rally from around 115.50 to 119.00.  \par \par \uc2  EUR/JPY faces minor resistance at 117.28 (the trough in late June), from which it has shied away in Asian trade today, standing at 117.10 in the early Asian afternoon after an intra-day high of 117.23. But there's no major resistance until the 119 level.  \par \par \uc2  This is very strong resistance; EUR/JPY has tried but failed to make a sustained entry (more than a couple of days) into the 119 level seven times in the last seven months. However, the slope of the long-term uptrend line implies a sustained entry will finally occur by end-September at the latest.  \par \par \uc2  If the resistance in the 119 level is overcome, that will be a very bullish signal, at a minimum pointing EUR/JPY up to test 121.12 resistance (the low in July 1999) by the end of this year.  \par \par \uc2  A EUR/JPY rally would imply substantial outperformance of the euro against the dollar compared to the yen against the dollar in the second half of this year.  \par \par \uc2  One reason to expect this is the relative likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in each \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Action by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and perhaps ultimately other central banks, to restrain yen appreciation and protect the export competitiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's shaky economy seems a lot more likely than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrain euro strength, which European authorities are believed to welcome because it dampens inflation.  \par \par \uc2  In addition, the euro looks significantly more bullish against the dollar on the long-term charts than does the yen. EUR/USD broke its last long-term downtrend line in May, and is supported by a long-term uptrend line dating back to October 2000. USD/JPY, on the other hand, doesn't have a long-term downtrend line but remains above a long-term, four-point uptrend line dating back to its record low of 79.80 in April 1995.  \par \par \uc2  That line comes in at about 113.30 this week and 115.50 at the end of this year. It wouldn't be surprising if support at this trendline was confirmed in the next month or two, triggering some rebound of the dollar against the yen, even as the euro continued to appreciate against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones survey of 18 major \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks operating in New York, taken after EUR/USD hit parity last night, confirmed that this is roughly what the market is expecting.  \par \par \uc2  The banks' median EUR/USD forecast for end-September was 1.0225, and for end-2002 it was 1.0450, implying continued EUR/USD appreciation. By contrast, the median USD/JPY estimate was 118.00 at end-September and 120.50 at end-year, which would involve a USD/JPY rebound from current levels - showing the market consensus is for a major EUR/JPY rise in coming months.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -by Andrew Torchia; (65) 6415-4013; andrew.torchia@dowjones.com\par  \f28 \par \par \uc2  1181  \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020716dy7g00099\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
