\par\par \b \uc2  Japan officials fret as yen rises, BOJ stands pat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2002\par 14:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 16 (Reuters) - The dollar's drop to 17-month lows against the yen fuelled anxiety in Japan on Tuesday, sparking a chorus of complaints by government officials who fear the drop could unravel a fragile export-driven economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have been sensitive about the dollar's swift decline, which makes Japanese exports more expensive and threatens a prime source economic growth when fiscal and monetary policy stimulus has been virtually exhausted. \par \par \uc2  But in a reminder of the limited monetary policy options left, with interest rates already virtually zero, the Bank of Japan kept policy unchanged at a board meeting on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Had they had a lot of room to ease, they could have been more flexible and acted early," said Mamoru Yamazaki, chief economist at Barclays Capital Japan. \par \par \uc2  "But there is little left that they could do, and that's limiting their freedom to act swiftly to early signs of a worsening economy." \par \par \uc2  While many economists say the BOJ could even upgrade its economic assessment in its monthly report due out on Wednesday because of improving indicators up to May, forthcoming figures for June and beyond - following the yen's sharp rise - could paint a grimmer picture, posing a conundrum for policymakers. \par \par \uc2  In the absence of a boost from domestic policy, Japanese officials sought to talk the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka, who earlier urged the BOJ to bear in mind the fallout from the yen's rise, said Japan must take action on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  if its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  became too strong and diverged from economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Trade Minister Takeo Hiranuma was more vocal, saying a weaker yen was in Japan's interests because its economy relied on exports. \par \par \uc2  "Because our country thrives on exports, a weaker yen is desirable," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  ABSENCE FROM MARKET \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at least seven times in the past two months but has kept a low profile in the last two weeks, even as the dollar fell to 115.65 yen on Monday, its weakest since February 2001 and below levels hit in the aftermath of the September 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  Traders say Japanese authorities appeared to be taking a low-key approach for now, given the dollar's decline was broad based, fuelled by worries about falling Wall Street stocks, accounting scandals and a possible U.S. economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit one-for-one parity against the euro on Monday for the first time in more than two years. \par \par \uc2  "We have no choice but to monitor the situation closely," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, whose ministry is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "Our basic policy is unchanged," said Shiokawa, who has repeatedly said in recent weeks that Japan would take appropriate action against excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials' concerns about the yen stems from realisation that Japan's economic expansion in the January-March quarter - the first in four quarters - was heavily reliant on export growth and a subsequent pickup in output. \par \par \uc2  The jobless rate running near record levels above five percent and dwindling income kept domestic consumption weak. \par \par \uc2  Balance of payment data on Monday showed the surplus in Japan's current account, the broadest measure of trade in goods and services, rose for the eighth month in May, jumping 107.7 percent from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  The scenario for an export-led recovery was underscored by revised industrial production figures, also released on Monday, which showed factory output had grown 4.1 percent from the previous month, the fourth month of gains. \par \par \uc2  Over the past few decades, Japan's trade partners have warned that Tokyo could not export its troubles away, but there have been few signs of progress in structural economic reform to ensure sustainable growth based on domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami has said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's so-called "quantitative easing" policy had helped underpin the economy by preventing a banking crisis but has said it had had little effect on boosting economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has kept interest rates at virtually zero since March 2001 through that poilcy framework, in which it floods the money market with excess liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's target for that excess liquidity, as measured by the balance of banks' current account deposits at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was left unchanged at 10-15 trillion yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (With additional reporting by Yonggi Kang and George Nishiyama). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020716dy7g00o8b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
