\par\par \b \uc2  Japanese officials fret as yen climbs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang \par \uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2002\par 06:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 16 (Reuters) - The dollar's tumble against the yen to 17-month lows fuelled anxiety in Japan on Tuesday, sparking a chorus of complaints by government officials who fear the drop could unravel a fragile export-driven economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have been sensitive about the dollar's swift decline, which makes Japanese exports more expensive and threatens a main source economic growth at a time when fiscal and monetary policy stimulus has been virtually exhausted. \par \par \uc2  In a reminder of the limited monetary policy options left, the Bank of Japan was widely expected to keep policy unchanged when its board concludes a two-day monthly meeting on Tuesday - much to the chagrin of government officials. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are showing worrying moves, so I want the Bank of Japan to conduct policy with that in mind," Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka told reporters before joining the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's meeting as a government representative. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has kept interest rates at virtually zero for more than a year through its so-called "quantitative easing" policy, in which it floods the money market with excess liquidity. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami has said the policy had helped underpin the economy by preventing a banking crisis but has admitted it had had little effect on boosting economic growth. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Takenaka also said said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  must take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the yen became too strong and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates diverged from economic fundamentals. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at least seven time over the past two months but has kept a low profile in the last two weeks, even as the dollar fell to 115.65 yen on Monday, its weakest since February 2001 and below levels hit in the aftermath of the September 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's decline - including hitting one-for-one parity against the euro for the first time in more than two years - has come amid concern about falling Wall Street stocks, accounting scandals and worries about a U.S. economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  "We have no choice but to monitor the situation closely," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, whose ministry is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, told reporters on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Our basic policy is unchanged," said Shiokawa, who has repeatedly said in recent weeks that Japan would take appropriate action against excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  Trade Minister Takeo Hiranuma was more vocal, saying a weaker yen was in Japan's interests as its economy relied on exports. \par \par \uc2  "Because our country thrives on exports, a weaker yen is desirable," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  Balance of payment data on Monday had shown that surplus in Japan's current account, the broadest measure of trade in goods and services, rose for the eighth month in May, jumping 107.7 percent from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  The scenario for an export-led recovery was underscored by revised industrial production figures, also released on Monday, which showed that factory output had grown 4.1 percent from the previous month, the fourth month of gains. \par \par \uc2  While growth in exports and output helped Japan's economy expand in the January-March quarter for the first time in four quarters, near record joblessness and dwindling income have kept consumption weak. \par \par \uc2  Over the past few decades, Japan's trade partners have warned that Tokyo could not export its troubles away, but there have been few signs of progress in structural economic reform for sustainable growth based on domestic demand. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020716dy7g007kg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
