\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Regains Dollar Parity For First Time Since 2000 \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry and Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 1,128 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2002\par 00:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- While the euro took center stage Monday, reclaiming one-for-one status against the dollar for the first time since February 2000 on Monday, the dollar's generalized decline, beset by a tide of negative sentiment, was the primary force at work. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was lower against the yen and down by a full cent against the euro late Monday in New York compared with its late Friday levels. \par \par \uc2  In the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen also capitalized on the dollar's retrenchment, while the advances of sterling and the Swiss franc "were purely a reflection of dollar weakness," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with UBS Warburg in London. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar was trading at 116.34 yen, down from 116.86 yen late Friday, but well off its session lows around 115.78 yen. The euro was at $1.0025, up around a full cent from Friday's level of 9.905 U.S. cents, but having declined from an earlier session high of $1.0088. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss Franc, the dollar was at 1.4633 francs, down from 1.4826 francs Friday, while the pound was trading around $1.5692, sharply up from $1.5509. \par \par \uc2  Following a string of U.S. corporate-accounting scandals that have surfaced over the past few weeks, the dollar, along with the stock market, "is getting defensive sentiment developing before any announcements that might take place," about the financial situation of U.S. companies, said Robert Sinche, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Most of the decisive moves Monday had happened by early in the New York day, with the key catalyst being the intensified anxiety about the quality of U.S. corporate accounts as earnings season got under way. \par \par \uc2  In fact, a sharp selloff in U.S. stocks in the middle of the New York trading day failed to generate further moves lower in the dollar, suggesting its losses in Asia and Europe were as much as would occur for the time being. And when stocks rebounded later in the day, so did the dollar. The Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbled more than 3% at one point, but it pared its gains significantly after that, ending the day down 45 points, or 0.52%, and causing the euro to consolidate close to the $1.00 mark in late dealings. \par \par \uc2  The common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  initially pushed above $1.00 at 1110 GMT in London, reaching $1.0026 by 1118 GMT, in a move that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists have been signaling during the past few weeks, and marking the rupture of a key psychological level. \par \par \uc2  In so doing, the euro easily outperformed most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts' forecasts made earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  In retrospect, "the pace of the move [up above parity with the dollar] has caught everyone by surprise," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. In the absence of convincingly robust European economic data, "the fact that investor confidence in the U.S. has really fallen has definitely helped the euro's cause," he added. \par \par \uc2  Now, the median estimate of 15 out of 18 banks that responded to a Dow Jones Newswires survey Monday has the euro climbing to $1.0450 at the end of this year, up from a median estimate of $1.00 in a survey undertaken on June 24. \par \par \uc2  In the case of the dollar's performance against the yen, forecasts are trickier to make because of the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven occasions since May, spending more than $30 billion in an effort to check the yen's appreciation against the dollar and nurture Japan's fledgling export-led recovery. But in more recent weeks, the BOJ has stood back from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, letting the dollar fall rapidly against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Over the weekend, Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said the dollar ought to be trading around 125-130 yen, again breaking a cardinal rule of never discussing specific levels. Shiokawa also has mentioned the 115 yen level in public comments several times in recent weeks, prompting many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  professionals to assume the BOJ would defend that level via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Many people now believe the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by either the BOJ alone or in concert with other central banks has waned despite the dollar's dipping south of 116 yen Monday. \par \par \uc2  "It's not that [Japan] is defending yen strength, they're going against dollar weakness, which is a totally different thing," said Harvinder Kalirai, senior strategist for State Street Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  And while it would be more effective if other central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  using their own funds alongside \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that is unlikely to happen because other countries don't share Japan's strategic interest in a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro zone, in fact, is "happy to have to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen a little bit and keep inflation in check," Mr. Kalirai said. \par \par \uc2  And despite the officially recognized "strong dollar policy," the U.S. is "just as happy with a weaker dollar," because it increases demand for U.S.-produced goods, said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist for North America at the Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants are now watching for any clues as to how Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan views the dollar's bearish bent and the battering in U.S. stock markets. Mr. Greenspan delivers congressional testimony on Tuesday and Wednesday on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Non-U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the so-called dollar bloc, whose economies are regarded as closely tied to the commodities cycle, didn't perform strongly against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar weakened versus the greenback Monday, despite expectations that the Bank of Canada will hike short-term rates by at least 0.25 percentage point Tuesday from its current level of 2.5%. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday afternoon the U.S. dollar was fetching 1.5381 Canadian dollars, up from 1.5309 dollars Friday. "Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven't benefited" from the dollar's decline, noted Mr. Mohi-uddin of UBS Warburg, because fears that the U.S. economic recovery might be less vigorous than previously thought are causing a rethink about global growth projections. \par \par \uc2  "As stock markets are collapsing, people are revising their growth forecasts down," Mr. Mohi-uddin said. \par \par \uc2  -- Kristyne E. Demske contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com and Tyler Lifton at tyler.lifton@wsj.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020715dy7f006y2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
