\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  SURVEY -2:Yen Seen Firmer; MOF Reluctance To Act \b0\par\par\uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2002\par 21:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists consulted cited the escalating anxiety about the quality of corporate accounting, which has been detracting from appetite for U.S. assets, as a cause for concern about the outlook for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Worries about the performance of U.S. equities, as borne out later in global trading day by a plunge in U.S. stocks - the Dow Jones Industrial Average was down by more than 3% at one stage during the New York afternoon - weighed on the greenback Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has lost 11.4% against the euro so far this year. After the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest, psychologically critical move above parity during European trading time, the euro was trading at $1.0050 Monday afternoon in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will continue to fall this year against the euro as Euro area investors put less money to work in U.S. stocks and bonds than they have in the past few years... this at the same time the U.S. current account deficit is widening," said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. current account, presently around 4.3% of gross domestic product, is widening as foreign investment flows afford dollar-denominated assets with less support. \par \par \uc2  "The problem is because people are so heavily weighted toward U.S. assets that the greenback is feeling the pinch," as they downgrade their allocations to these assets, said David Mozina, Director of Global Fixed Income and \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research at Bank of America in New York. The dollar is getting "clobbered" as a result, he added. \par \par \uc2  In the case of the dollar's performance against the yen, forecasts are trickier to make because of the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven occasions since May, spending more than $30 billion in an effort to check the yen's appreciation against the dollar and nurture Japan's fledgling export-led recovery. But in more recent weeks, the BOJ has stood back from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, letting the dollar fall rapidly against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the MOF may be less eager to step into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since it last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 28 has caused some strategists to forecast that the dollar will slip further against the yen over the near term. \par \par \uc2  On July 2, for instance, UBS Warburg's economists changed their view on how aggressive BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be, Mohi-uddin said. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the median estimate is now for the dollar to trade against the yen at Y118.00 by the end of September, stronger than its Y116.09 level midafternoon Monday in New York but considerably weaker than the previous survey's Y125.00 estimate. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to firm more than previously thought not solely because the MOF appears to have lowered the bar in terms the levels at which it is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In addition, "the yen tends to do well in periods of very low risk appetite," as currently, "with stock markets around the world getting murdered," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Lehman Brothers in London. \par \par \uc2  "Worries over the health of the U.S. corporate sector will keep Japanese investors from putting large amounts of funds to work in the U.S.," Patterson said. While in May, foreign investment flows started to show more interest in Japanese equities, in the past few weeks, some economists have also started to note repatriation flows from Japanese accounts going back into Japanese assets. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Table Of Banks' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Forecasts For Euro/Dollar\par Euro/Dlr On:        End Sept  End Dec    End June 2003\par Bank Of America     1.02        1.00        1.05\par Barclays            1.03        0.95        0.85\par BNP Paribas         1.02        1.02        1.05\par Brown Brothers      0.98        1.05        1.10\par Citibank            1.030       0.9900      0.9600\par CSFB                1.05        N/A         1.00\par Daiwa               N/A         N/A         N/A\par Deutsche Bank       1.03        1.05        1.05\par FleetBoston         N/A         N/A         N/A\par Goldman Sachs       1.03        1.08        1.12\par HSBC                1.00        1.05        1.10\par ING Barings         1.0225      1.005       0.990\par JPMorgan Chase      1.020       1.040       1.00\par Lehman Brothers     1.030       1.100       1.15\par Merrill Lynch       N/A         N/A         N/A\par Morgan Stanley      1.00        0.95        1.00\par UBS Warburg         1.02        1.05        1.15\par UFJ                 1.05        1.07        1.10\par MEDIAN              1.0225      1.045       1.05\par Table Of Banks' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Forecasts For Dollar/Yen\par Dlr/Yen On:        End Sept  En