\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  To Bolster Weak Dollar Seen Less Likely \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2002\par 20:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Despite the dollar's tumble early Monday below key psychological levels against both the yen and the euro, the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by either the Japanese government alone or a consortium of governments is low at this point, market professionals believe. \par \par \uc2  They cite the broad-based investment portfolio flows working against the dollar as well as a disinclination by the Federal Reserve or the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the  slide in the dollar as reasons why concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely. \par \par \uc2  "There doesn't seem to be strong appetite for action right now," among the world's leading central banks, said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for UBS Warburg in London. \par \par \uc2  At this point, Japan has the greatest interest in stemming the dollar's decline as a strong yen hurts the country's exports, but even the Japanese government may be rethinking its proclivity to act by itself. \par \par \uc2  "It's not that (Japan) is defending yen strength, they're going against dollar weakness, which is a totally different thing,"said Harvinder Kalirai, senior strategist for State Street Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  According to analysts,  movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market lately aren't being driven by either yen or euro strength, but more by dollar weakness. The Dow Jones Industrial Average - the direction of which has largely defined the dollar's movements of late - was as low as 8352 earlier Monday, its weakest level since Sept. 24. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's break below Y116 early Monday brought it level with its lowest point since the weeks following the terrorist attacks of September 11, a level that only recently was seen as a "must defend" region for the Japanese  government, which is dependent on its exporting community to lead the country out of recession. \par \par \uc2  "We'd probably have to see a complete implosion of the dollar, a move that in itself is seen as a threat to global markets... and would (for instance) prompt a wholesale flight of capital from the U.S. Treasury market," before a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by multiple central banks using their  own respective funds would occur, said Jalinoos. \par \par \uc2  But he added, "We're still a long way away from that." \par \par \uc2  Signifying the threat to Japan posed by the stronger yen, the most recent survey of non-financial listed companies by Japan's Cabinet Office showed that a dollar level of Y115.32 or lower would make it impossible for Japan's exporters to be profitable, according to a Citibank report released Friday. \par \par \uc2  But on its own, the BOJ is incapable of making a significant impact on exchange rates, market professionals said. \par \par \uc2  With investor capital leaking out of the U.S. - particularly that of Japanese investors in a repatriation move - "the market-driven flows are just so large that any reasonable amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  does nothing," said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist for North America at the Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's seven \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  between late May and the end  of June to weaken the yen versus the dollar "totaled about  $30 billion and hardly made a dent in the exchange rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  "So if they really want to have a perceptible effect, the number (of dollars spent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has to be much, much larger, and the question is, do they want to do that?"  he said, noting that flooding the monetary system with that  many yen could have an unintended effect on Japan's domestic monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "Fundamental considerations also work against stemming the yen's rise," wrote Marc Chandler, HSBC Securities' chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, in a report Monday. "Today Japan  reported an upward revision to May industrial output to 4.1% from 3.9% and a 107% increase in its May current account surplus to $10.9 billion." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's seven most recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  became increasingly ineffective, and even asking the Fed and the ECB to conduct market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  using the BOJ's funds, as happened during \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had only a  temporary impact. \par \par \uc2  The next step in the effort to increase the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be for the Fed, the ECB  and possibly other central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  using their own funds, some analysts believe. \par \par \uc2  But that is unlikely at this point, they say, because other countries do not have a strategic interest in a weaker  dollar, as Japan does. \par \par \uc2  The euro zone, in fact, is "happy to have to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen a little bit and keep inflation in check," said State Street's Kalirai. \par \par \uc2  And despite the strong dollar policy that the U.S. government publicly maintains,  "they're just as happy with  a weaker dollar," because it increases demand for U.S.-produced goods, Royal Bank of Scotland's Bhagavatula believes. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, "A lack of inflation in the U.S. means a weaker dollar doesn't pose threat on that front," said Jalinoos of UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248 \par \par \uc2  -tyler.lifton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020715dy7f00asx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
