\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar skids below euro parity on US earnings angst. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2002\par 14:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 15 (Reuters) - The dollar plunged below parity with the euro on Monday and spiralled down towards last September's low on the yen as concern about U.S. assets ahead of a key earnings season battered sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which last saw the psychologically important one-to-one level in February 2000, powered as high as $1.0035 , bringing its gains this year to nearly 13 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is under pressure from everything from economic problems to asset reallocation away from the U.S. and corporate accounting problems. It's difficult to see any positive factor for the dollar at the moment," said Julian Jessop, chief European economist at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The root of the problem is the U.S. current account deficit. If the U.S. doesn't have to attract an enormous amount of foreign capital, people wouldn't have to worry about domestic problems. One solution to this is a weaker dollar." \par \par \uc2  Worries about the dollar have overridden wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least seven days since late May to curb export-harming yen strength. It last acted to sell yen on June 28 but has kept the market on edge ever since. \par \par \uc2  The greenback fell to its lowest level since late September at 115.83 yen . A drop below 115.76, the low on September 23, would take the dollar to its weakest since February 2001. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc also soared against the dollar, hitting 1.4643 francs per dollar , its highest since October 1999. \par \par \uc2  PARITY REGAINED \par \par \uc2  After weeks of expectation, the euro finally pushed through parity, marking a dramatic turnaround in its fortunes after spending most of its short life on a massive downtrend. \par \par \uc2  "The whole market's watching this," said one trader at a U.S. bank as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shot more than one percent up on the day against the dollar in a sudden dash higher. \par \par \uc2  European policymakers declined to comment on the move to parity but euro zone finance ministers had issued one of their strongest statement to date in support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gathering strength when they met late last week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted however that the euro is still below its celebrated January 1999 launch rate around $1.17 and faces a key technical barrier at $1.0080/90. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is still weak in the historical sweep of things, so we shouldn't get carried away," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  Doubts over U.S. corporate credibility and apprehension over second-quarter earnings have been the driving force for the dollar's weakness and the euro's gains. \par \par \uc2  All three major U.S. stock indices fell steeply last week, with news of a sudden drop in U.S. consumer sentiment on Friday adding to the damage. Stock index futures were indicating a flat to lower open on Wall Street on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We will have a round of corporate earnings this week but faith will not come back quickly," said Stacey Seltzer, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Brown Brothers Harriman. \par \par \uc2  "The market is concerned if there are more cover-up scandals out there, they are going to come out next month." \par \par \uc2  In June, the Securities and Exchange Commission told the top 945 U.S. publicly-traded companies that their chief executives and financial officers would have to certify the accuracy of financial books personally starting in mid August. \par \par \uc2  For clues on the U.S. Federal Reserve's stance on interest rates, market players are looking to congressional testimony by Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan on Tuesday and Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "It's important whether he can calm the market. On the interest rate outlook, he will be positive but he will use his usual creative ambiguity," Seltzer said. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, a Reuters survey of Wall Street primary dealers found 12 out of 22 expect the Fed to keep rates on hold at a four-decade low of 1.75 percent for the rest of the year. Two weeks ago, those expecting no change this year numbered eight. \par \par \uc2  Eurodollar futures, one of the best guides to market thinking on Fed monetary policy, have already slashed the odds of even a quarter point hike by year-end. \par \par \uc2  BOJ ABSENT \par \par \uc2  Dealers felt brave enough to sell the dollar on Monday following the Bank of Japan's absence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market despite the dollar's decisive fall below 117 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders say the authorities' pain threshold was probably lower now, with top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda merely repeating on Monday his rather mild warning that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged. \par \par \uc2  And with Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa's track record of verbal gaffes and cryptic remarks, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders paid little heed to his nationally televised comment on Sunday that a dollar range of 125-135 yen would be favourable for Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020715dy7f00p64\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
