\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar stays frail, hits new 10-month low vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2002\par 06:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 15 (Reuters) - The dollar stayed frail at the start of a fresh week in Asia on Monday, still weighed down by a crisis of confidence in U.S. share markets after a series of financial shenanigans. \par \par \uc2  It slipped to a 10-month low against the yen of 116.40 yen, closing in on its post-September 11 low of 115.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  As of 0245 GMT, the dollar traded at 116.47/52, down from 116.80/85 at the end of U.S. trade last week. \par \par \uc2  The euro also firmed slightly to 99.40/45 cents, compared to 99.12/17 cents in late Friday U.S. trade, although still some distance from a recent peak of 99.90 cents that took it just shy of symbolic parity with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was easier at 115.73/84 yen, compared to 116.10/24 in late Friday U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The greenback remained in thrall to woes in U.S. equity markets, where major indices including the Dow Jones industrial average saw their largest weekly drops last week since September on distrust over accounting practices in U.S. firms. \par \par \uc2  "What is going on in financial markets is a crisis of confidence in the U.S. So it's not something you can mend in just a few days," said Takehiro Sato, an economist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  A sudden drop in U.S. consumer sentiment added to the damage. \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan's preliminary consumer sentiment index fell to 86.5 in July - its lowest level since last November - from 92.4 in June, though a slight rise to 92.8 had been expected. \par \par \uc2  GREENSPAN TESTIMONY WATCHED \par \par \uc2  "I can't think of a factor that can turn around the current bearish sentiment other than monetary easing," said Seiya Nakajima, an economist at trading house Itochu Corp. \par \par \uc2  While few go so far as to expect a Fed easing in the immediate future, the market has recently sharply scaled back its expectations for future rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  Growing expectations that the Fed is unlikely to raise rates for the rest of this year and possibly until early next year pushed yields on two-year U.S. Treasury notes up by a massive 0.36 percentage point to 2.53 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  For clues on the Fed's stance, market players are looking to congressional testimony by Fed chairman Alan Greenspan on Tuesday and Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "I think the Fed will for now wait and see whether the latest nervous sentiment calms down," said Sato of Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, the market was little impressed by comments by Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa at the weekend that a dollar range of 125-135 yen would be favourable for Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  Nor was it impressed by Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda's statement on Monday morning that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The market is still generally betting that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the rise in the yen, which threatens to hammer the profits of exporters and stymie a fledgling recovery in the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  But Japanese authorities have stayed on the sidelines since late June, after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least seven days since late May, when their latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign began. \par \par \uc2  Dealers are wondering what their intentions are, since they have let the dollar slip to low after low in the last two weeks, despite jawboning against the yen's rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0300 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   12 Jul. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9938   0.9909   +0.29   +11.61   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        116.39   116.85   -0.39   -11.58   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    115.70   115.82   -0.10    -1.31   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.4776   1.4823   -0.32   -10.96   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.5539   1.5531   +0.05    +6.86   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5310   1.5333   -0.15    -3.81   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5606   0.5595   +0.20    +9.79   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4690   1.4700   -0.07    -0.61   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6393   0.6380   +0.20    +4.41   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...  Debt... Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Official rates...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......   Top events........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Diaries........... Diaries Index........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press Digests....  Polls on G7 economies..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba00