\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Yen casts shadow over robust Japan exports. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2002\par 08:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 15 (Reuters) - Robust export growth continued to drive Japan's recovery from recession in May, but trade figures released on Monday also served up a reminder that the economy remains precariously dependent on foreign demand. \par \par \uc2  Japan's trade surplus surged 183 percent to 728.8 billion yen ($6.23 billion) as exports rose 8.7 percent from a year earlier, the largest gain since November 2000, the Finance Ministry said. \par \par \uc2  The surplus in the current account, the broadest measure of trade in goods and services, rose 108 percent to 1.044 trillion yen in May for the eighth straight year-on-year rise. \par \par \uc2  Big exporters such as Toshiba Corp and Toyota Motor Co have been leading the economy back to growth in recent months, but the yen's recent surge against the dollar has economists worried that it could prove short-lived. \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar's fall to 10-month lows against the yen, a ministry official said the current account surplus was set to widen further in the months ahead. \par \par \uc2  "Since there is a time lag before changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates affect the data, the yen's recent rise is unlikely to affect June figures. Overall, there will not be any big change in the trend" the official said. \par \par \uc2  From the previous month, exports gained 3.3 percent while imports were up 3.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  An export-driven recovery in industrial output and signs of a pick-up in long-dormant capital spending prompted the government to upgrade its economic assessment for the fourth time in five months in a report last week. \par \par \uc2  Japan's GDP grew at a brisk annualised clip of 5.7 percent in the first three months of the year, ending three straight quarters of contraction. \par \par \uc2  Separate data on Monday showed May industrial production posted a revised gain of 4.1 percent from the previous month, up from a preliminary reading of a 3.9 percent rise, according to the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry. \par \par \uc2  But consumer spending, the biggest part of the economy, remains stalled and the economy is beset by a host of structural problems including a massive debt overhang at banks, raising doubt over whether the recovery can become self-sustaining. \par \par \uc2  Exports only make up around 10 percent of the economy. \par \par \uc2  "Economic indicators related to corporate activity, particularly manufacturers, are steadily improving," said Shuji Shirota, economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "But the scenario for a broader economic recovery based on such improvements could be seriously derailed by further yen strength." \par \par \uc2  YEN KEEPS RISING \par \par \uc2  The yen's rise against the faltering U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will inevitably make life more difficult for Japan's exporters in the coming months as their goods become less competitive abroad. \par \par \uc2  In afternoon Tokyo trade, the dollar had fallen to 116.20/30 yen, its lowest level since September 2001. \par \par \uc2  Shirota said the average break-even dollar/yen rate for all Japanese exporters was around 115.30 yen, while the rate for the more competitive industries like electric machinery, whose growth has been the main engine for Japan's recovery, was around 112. \par \par \uc2  "Because these industries are more competitive on the global markets, they can withstand some more yen strength," Shirota said. "But if the yen strengthens much further, the prospect of the economy stalling will become more real." \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said on Monday the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy had not changed, refraining from the kind of threats the ministry often uses ahead of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Worries about a slowdown in the U.S. economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's number-one export market, as well as market turmoil there are likely to be high on the Bank of Japan's agenda as it starts a two-day policy meeting on MOnday. \par \par \uc2  Still, few expect any change in its already ultra-loose policy. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's monthly report for July, due to get Policy Board approval during the two-day meeting for release later this week, is expected by economists to upgrade the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic assessment for the fifth straight month. ($1=116.95 Yen) (Additional reporting by Shinichi Kishima, George Nishiyama). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020715dy7f002mj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
