\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Fin Min: Dlr Too Weak; Joint  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  At Y100 \b0\par\par\uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2002\par 06:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Sunday the dollar has fallen too far and that the leading industrial powers would likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly  to prevent it from collapsing below Y100. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is "too weak," Shiokawa said on a TV Asahi talk show, adding that it ought to trade around Y125 to Y130. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. economy remains strong, but scandals at such companies as Enron Corp. (ENRNQ) and WorldCom Inc. (WCOME) have shattered the market's trust and laid the dollar low, he said. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether a dollar collapse was possible, Shiokawa said, "That will absolutely not happen." He then chided the questioner: "It's because all of you keep fanning the flames that people get worried." \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the dollar could fall below Y100, Shiokawa  hinted that such a drop would force the U.S. government to drop its laissez-faire stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and join Tokyo in defending the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I think if such a thing were to happen, it would slam the entire world economy," Shiokawa said. "So we would all  make efforts and cooperate, I think." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded late Friday in New York for Y116.86, down some 14% from its January high and about Y4 below where Japan began trying to defend it with a record-setting round of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Finance Ministry sold more than Y3 trillion for dollars through the Bank of Japan, the Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in seven rounds from late May to late June. \par \par \uc2  Japan's recent absence from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has caused many to believe the government has abandoned its policy of fighting the yen's rise, although Shiokawa's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy insisted Friday there was no change to the Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo fears the yen's rise to post-Sept. 11 highs could derail Japan's nascent economic recovery, which is heavily dependent on exports. \par \par \uc2  For a full-fledged recovery, Shiokawa said, a revival in  corporate capital spending, the biggest chunk of the economy after personal consumption, is necessary. He insisted that with planned tax incentives, business investment will pick up next year and he angrily denied speculation that the economy could relapse this fall due to a soaring yen or a bank panic. \par \par \uc2  "By around spring of next year, capital spending will start" improving, he said. "So please don't keep fanning the flames by saying, 'Things are bad, things are bad, things are going to get worse."' \par \par \uc2  The government upgraded its assessment of the economy Thursday, saying it is showing patchy improvement as exports  lift production and signs emerge that capital spending may hit bottom. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa also insisted the government won't let any banks fail in the run-up to the planned capping of deposit guarantees. \par \par \uc2  Referring to plan to cap regular deposit guarantees at Y10 million per account next April, Shiokawa said, "Until then, we absolutely can't let any financial institutions fail. Mr. (Prime Minister Junichiro) Koizumi is saying this as hard as he can and we (in the government) are backing him  up." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa refused to be drawn on whether tax revisions now being debated will result in a net tax cut for businesses. \par \par \uc2  The government wants to introduce a "pro forma" levy that would tax businesses based on some measure of size, such as number of employees. Some 70% of Japanese companies,  mostly small firms, declare losses to the tax authorities and thus pay no corporate income tax. \par \par \uc2  But Shiokawa said the pro forma tax isn't meant to be packaged with a cut in the corporate tax rate - an idea he said needs to be considered with caution. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, he insisted business tax revisions won't lead to net tax hikes or hurt the economy. \par \par \uc2  "We are going to undertake tax revisions that will enable corporations to be active and make business investments so that we can have an industrial turnaround," the minister said. \par \par \uc2  - By William Mallard, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2932; billy.mallard@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020714dy7e0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
