\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa says dlr at 125-135 yen good for Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2002\par 05:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 14 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Sunday a dollar value in the range of 125 to 135 yen would be good for Japan's economy, now showing signs of an export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  "A recent survey of corporations said a dollar range of 125-135 yen was favourable, and I think that is obvious. (The current rate) is moving a bit beyond that," Shiokawa said on a TV Asahi television programme. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar at 125-135 yen would be a good area for the Japanese economy." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa dismissed the possibility of further steep falls in the dollar. Asked whether the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would fall below 100 yen, he said: "That depends on movements in the global economy. \par \par \uc2  "But the global economy would fall apart if such a thing were to happen so I believe everyone would cooperate and coordinate," to prevent that, he added. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least seven times since late May to try to curb the yen's rise. On June 28, it enlisted the help of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the New York Federal Reserve to sell yen on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last traded around 116.85 yen. \par \par \uc2  The outspoken 80-year old minister's remarks have caused much confusion recently in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month, Shiokawa's remarks during a trip to Copenhagen were interpreted in the market as suggesting financial authorities believed a weakening of the dollar towards September lows near 115 yen was in progress. \par \par \uc2  Last week, he said his true intention had been to say the dollar must not be allowed to get that far. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said on Sunday it would be problematic if the dollar fell to around 115 yen, adding that since Washington supported a "strong dollar" policy, it should make efforts to stabilise stock prices and show that its economy was strong. \par \par \uc2  "(Treasury Secretary Paul) O'Neill told me the U.S. economy would definitely grow three percent, or even 3.5 percent," he said. \par \par \uc2  "MISBEHAVING" \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa attributed the continuing fall in U.S. share prices to concerns over accounting standards at U.S. firms rather than their actual earnings performance. \par \par \uc2  "Stock prices have been falling not because of concerns over earnings, but because firms were misbehaving," he said, adding that President George W. Bush's plan to punish individuals more strictly for misconduct would help support the market. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average slipped 7.4 percent last week, chalking up its worst weekly drop since the stock market reopened following the September 11 attacks. \par \par \uc2  On Japan's economy, Shiokawa said that he expected capital spending to emerge "from next spring" and that the economy would not worsen from the current situation. \par \par \uc2  The government upgraded its view of the economy for the fourth time in five months on Thursday, saying it detected brighter signs in some parts of the economy thanks to a rise in exports and improvements in production and capital spending. \par \par \uc2  But it warned that the recovery dependent on exports and vulnerable to the weakening dollar. \par \par \uc2  On the financial sector, Shiokawa said the Koizumi administration would not allow any more financial failures. \par \par \uc2  "We will not let any financial institutions fail before the end of full bank deposit protection" next April, he said. \par \par \uc2  The government began phasing out blanket bank deposit protection in April, and plans to limit protection to all deposits to up to 10 million yen from next April. \par \par \uc2  But some smaller banks and ruling coalition party members have voiced opposition to the move. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa also said tax reforms would focus on reviving the economy, including tax incentives for capital expenditure. Tax reforms are to be largely finalised in August. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020714dy7e0040i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
