\par\par \b \uc2  POINT OF VIEW: Shiokawa's Yen Rhetoric Sows Confusion \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,099 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2002\par 22:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts have a message for Japanese finance minister Masajuro Shiokawa: when in doubt about how to talk to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, it might be best to say nothing. \par \par \uc2  The more often he tries to clarify his statements about the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the deeper into a verbal quagmire Shiokawa has appeared to sink and the further the dollar has slipped against the yen - surely not what he intended. \par \par \uc2  To be fair, Shiokawa's faux pas - allowing some in the markets to think that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be less disposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to weaken the yen - reveals a communications tangle that not just Japanese finance ministers have become ensnared in when sending signals to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, it's remarkable that in the space of just a  few days Japan's finance minister - unwittingly perhaps - has blundered by repeatedly breaking several unwritten rules  that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders expect policymakers to follow. As a result, "following Shiokawa's comments, the credibility of the (finance ministry's) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was damaged somewhat," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with BNP Paribas in London. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has undertaken seven bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since late May designed to weaken the yen, spending more than $30 billion according to some estimates. But since the last occasion on June 28, the MOF has been conspicuously absent from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, even though the yen has continued to strengthen. Simultaneously, Shiokawa has stumbled several times over the past week. \par \par \uc2  First of all, the finance minister said on July 4 that Japan wants to avoid the dollar's fall to its post-Sept. 11 lows around Y115.80. Even though he may not have meant to, in mentioning a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate level, Shiokawa broke a cardinal rule among monetary policy makers.  Participants in the fast-moving \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were so struck by the mention of a number well below that of the dollar's current exchange rate against the yen that they translated his - admittedly qualified - remarks as tacit approval, pushing the dollar lower against the yen in reaction. \par \par \uc2  That "put a level in the market's mind and in this environment (of prevailing negative sentiment encircling the  dollar) the market tends to move pretty rapidly towards it," said Daragh Maher, senior economist at ING Financial Markets in London. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Muddying The Waters\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shiokawa's subsequent efforts to clarify his remarks have only muddied\par the waters, leaving traders with the impression there is more room to\par push the dollar lower. Since just over a week ago, the dollar has\par dropped more than  three yen or 2.7%, to Y116.74 Friday afternoon in New\par York,  from Y119.90 late in New York on July 4.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts reckon silence could have been more effective than the stream\par of `clarifications' from Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  about its views on\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. "We would have thought monetary officials\par would have learnt  over the years that they should avoid mentioning\par levels at all...perhaps it was a momentary lapse," Stannard said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet such lapses on the 82-year-old Shiokawa's part are more than\par momentary, some Japan-watchers argue, pointing to his repeated efforts\par this week alone to tone down his earlier remarks. At a weekend meeting\par of Asian and European finance ministers last week in Copenhagen,\par Shiokawa once again said that the majority of those gathered there\par believed the dollar was heading to the post-Sept. 11 lows. And virtually\par every day since then, his efforts to dilute the remarks have served only\par to reinforce them. On Thursday,  he said that what he had meant was that\par the yen had gone to  Y115 after Sept. 11 and "we must keep it from\par returning there."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But despite his efforts, the only thing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   traders\par heard was his repeated references to the Y115 level, which in turn\par prompted some to push the dollar towards that bar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Part of the confusion about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy stems from the\par fact that the ministry emits a cacophony of messages regarding the\par willingness to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . While Shiokawa "is more the wild card", by\par contrast, vice minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda, the\par architect of the ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, "has been far more\par consistent...in using verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effectively," said Alan\par Ruskin, research director at 4Cast in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This two-tongued approach to communication is a far cry from the days\par when Eisuke Sakakibara was vice finance minister for international\par affairs - a key post that enabled  him to emerge, as far as the markets\par were concerned, as the  sole spokesman on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Who Does The Talking?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now, the first question the ministry needs to resolve is  "who speaks\par for this thing?" says Ruskin. Furthermore, by  contrast with Kuroda who\par appears to stick to a more tightly  crafted script, the more loquacious\par Shiokawa's latest statements get him "into the game of prognostication,"\par representing "an obvious error on his part," Ruskin said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among Shiokawa's latest sound bites, his remark that "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets are in an adjustment phase," was  "a classic case of talking too\par much," according to Ruskin. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders may have taken the comments\par as somehow condoning the direction \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are moving in, in  this\par case, the dollar's retrenchment against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shiokawa also played down the dollar's skid in Asia on Friday to\par Y116.51 - later repeated in New York - its lowest level since the\par aftermath of the September attacks. "Recent  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements\par appear to be somewhat smaller than those seen some two weeks ago," he\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It's certainly possible, of course, that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has shifted its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  priorities largely due to the realization that it cannot\par single-handedly turn around the wave of sour sentiment weighing on the\par dollar. In this sense, perhaps Shiokawa's comments are intended more to\par signal that the finance ministry will only fight the battles  it can\par win, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only when the dollar is strengthening in any case.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But while Shiokawa continues to fall between these two stools, traders\par may instead choose to focus more closely on the man with a more\par consistent message: "There is no change  to our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par policy," Kuroda insisted Friday. "We are watching the markets closely\par and are ready to take bold action if necessary."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096 (Miyako Takebe and  Audrey\par McEvoy in Tokyo contributed to this report).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020712dy7c00dkz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
