\par\par \b \uc2  Foreign Central Banks Unlikely To Pull Trigger On Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 1,195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2002\par 19:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Foreign central banks may well have access to the trigger that could spark a much more swift slide in the dollar. But they're probably not going to pull it. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar's decline gains pace, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts are looking for key flashpoints that might speed the dollar's decline, which thus far has been relatively orderly. A major catalyst would be if foreign central banks - in particular the five North Asian banks that between them hold over $1 trillion in dollar-dominated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - begin to shift more of those holdings into euro. On the face of it, that prospect gains much more likelihood as the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  approaches the elusive bar of parity with the greenback. \par \par \uc2  But with the export competitiveness of their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  very much on their minds, analysts believe any shift by those central banks would be extremely gradual and only carried out in a manner that wouldn't spark a run on the dollar. "Their role will be redistributing and whether it begins now or later will depend on their judgment as to whether the market can withstand the sale," said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist and head of North American research at Bank of New York. "They will contribute to the secular decline of the dollar, they just won't contribute to market instability." \par \par \uc2  Between them, the central banks of Japan, China, Taiwan, Hong Kong and South Korea hold just over $1 trillion in reserves, with the bulk still held in the U.S. dollar. Since the launch of the euro in January 1999, those banks have indicated that there would come a time when they may need to rebalance their assets, but with the dollar remaining strong over the past few years, there's been little incentive to do so. Now, that picture is changing dramatically, with evidence mounting that the dollar's decline might this time be more than a cyclical - and therefore temporary - downturn. There are also key signals that the euro has emerged as a viable alternative major global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If you're a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managing reserves and you think the dollar is on a cyclical decline, you wouldn't do any adjustment, but if you think it's a secular decline, you want to think about it," said David Mozina, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income research at Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Some Juggling Already Going On\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There are already signs that some of these banks are doing precisely\par that. The People's Bank of China, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , somewhat\par unusually, has been the most transparent of the five. Late last year,\par the bank said it was raising the euro portion of its reserves portfolio,\par citing the "steady" nature of the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Analysts said the\par move seemed part of a plan to gradually diversify its massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings away from the dollar into the euro as China seeks to\par deepen trade ties with the European Union.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wu Xiaoling, deputy governor of the People's Bank of China, the\par country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview,\par however, that "the recent depreciation of the U.S. dollar doesn't have\par much impact on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in China."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There has also been market speculation that the central banks of South\par Korea and Taiwan have increased their euro reserves, although they\par haven't confirmed that. Chou A-ting, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of China, Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , told Dow Jones Newswires\par only that the bank will adjust its reserves based on market conditions.\par Taiwan's reserves hit a record high in June for the tenth straight\par month, partly due to the rise in the value of the euro. The Bank of\par Korea meanwhile, has said the weaker dollar is one of the key reasons\par for the increase in its  reserves - also because its assets denominated\par in euro and yen rise in value when converted into dollar terms, in which\par its reserves are compiled.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hong Kong's monetary authorities also defer questions regarding any\par planned changes to its reserves, which stand at about $112.4 billion.\par The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's chief executive Joseph Yam recently\par said that the strong euro and yen had led to a better-than-expected\par return from the quasi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's exchange fund in the first six\par months of the year, but he didn't give any outlook for the rest of the\par year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Not Profit Maximizers\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While subtle shifts in their foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings might certainly\par be already fueling general pressure on the dollar few market\par participants expect the Asian banks to embark on any radical reweighting\par of their portfolios now. For one, central banks are generally\par longer-term investors, with a reputation for sluggish responses to\par market conditions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par What's more, many often have competing motives to trade in the market\par - typically to strengthen or weaken their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - which means\par they're not generally seeking to maximize their profits. `Central banks\par aren't punters in markets," said Mozina, using to the British English\par term for players.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the case of the Asian central banks, current concerns about their\par own exports' competitiveness could easily trump their interests in\par profitable reserve management. "Korea, Japan and China are major trading\par partners with the U.S.," noted Jay Bryson, a global economist at\par Wachovia Securities in Charlotte, N.C. "If they thought the dollar was\par going to decline, they would be loath to add to even more selling\par pressure on the dollar by dumping their reserves."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There's also the strong possibility that the North Asian banks will\par give some backhanded support to the dollar, even aside from the more\par blatant efforts of Japan - the world's largest holder of dollar reserves\par - to prop up the greenback through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many of these central banks hold large amounts of U.S. government\par bonds - mostly Treasurys - in their portfolios and traders reckon they\par have already - led by the Bank of Japan - supported the dollar by buying\par even more shorter-dated bonds and hiking their holdings of agency\par securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Demand for Treasurys, at least from foreign central banks, could\par increase if downward pressure on the dollar continues," said Bryson, at\par Wachovia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seven times to buy dollars against\par yen since late May, fueling $26.5 billion leap in the country's reserves\par in June, to a total of $446.2 billion. Because of this, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par has probably seen little if any impact from the weaker dollar on its\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings, because the pace of amassing dollars is\par swamping the diminution in the value of its reserves due to the fall in\par the value of the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381;\par grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (J.R. Wu in Taipei, So-Eui Rhee in Seoul, Liz Rudall in Hong Kong,\par Billy Mallard in Tokyo, Charlene Lee in Frankfurt and Brian Blackstone\par in Paris contributed to this report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020712dy7c00bf5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
