\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Japanese buying of foreign bonds falls in June. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 857 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2002\par 04:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 12 (Reuters) - Cash-rich Japanese investors sharply reduced foreign bond purchases in June, reflecting the steady decline of the dollar against the yen and fading confidence in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said on Friday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) data showed that Japanese investors' net buying of foreign bonds, based on contracts, totalled 106.5 billion yen ($909.6 million) in June after net purchases of 995.8 billion yen in May. \par \par \uc2  The June figures were also sharply lower than April's 1.96 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  "Growing pessimism about the outlook for the dollar made investors nervous about piling fresh foreign bond holdings into their portfolios last month," said Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dropped steadily against the yen during the month despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  The dollar managed to rise near to 126 yen in mid-June, but by the end of the month it had weakened to around 118.50 yen, down about six percent from the high. On Friday it was even lower at 116.95 yen at 0215 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Concerned that a stronger yen will derail an export-led economic recovery, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven occasions since late May, including four times last month. \par \par \uc2  In total \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is estimated to have bought about $30 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the two months. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on June 28, but the dollar kept on sliding, falling to a 10-month low of 116.50 yen in overseas trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  RISE IN EARLY JULY \par \par \uc2  Even though net purchases slumped in June, Japanese banks and individual investors appear to have flocked into foreign bonds at the start of July. \par \par \uc2  The ministry's weekly capital flows data released on Thursday showed that Japanese investors' net buying of foreign bonds rose sharply to 949.1 billion yen last week, from July 1 to 5. \par \par \uc2  That was more than 14 times as much as the 66.2 billion yen bought during the week of June 24-28. \par \par \uc2  Fund managers and analysts said the huge net purchases were largely the result of buying by Japanese banks, who shifted their dollar-based proceeds into foreign bonds, which meant that there was little impact on the dollar/yen rate. \par \par \uc2  "Heavy purchases were not done by yen-based Japanese investors, but bought by dollar-based (Japanese) banks, seeking capital gains in the recently bullish U.S. Treasury market," the chief fund manager at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think yen-based Japanese investors, like pension funds or life insurers, flocked into U.S. bonds or European bonds at a time when the yen was appreciating sharply last week," the fund manager said. \par \par \uc2  "If they had done, they would have got burned very serverely." \par \par \uc2  In contrast to the slump in the U.S. stock market, where the S&P 500 and the Nasdaq index have dropped to five-year lows, aggressive fund flows into U.S. bonds have been pushing Treasury yields down sharply. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year T-notes stood at 2.59 percent on Thursday, approaching the all-time closing low yield of 2.31 percent struck in November last year. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year T-note yield was at 4.64 percent on Thursday. It was above five percent about a month ago. \par \par \uc2  Japanese retail investors have also been looking to buy foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds to lock in higher returns, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "It's true that we are seeing constant bids for dollars from (Japanese) retail accounts for the purpose of parking their summer bonuses in foreign bonds," a dealer at a securities firm said. \par \par \uc2  "Unlike institutional investors, they are relatively flexible in buying dollars on dips," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Net buying of foreign stocks grew slightly to 646.1 billion yen in June from 599.1 billion yen a month earlier. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Japanese pension funds had been steadily adding foreign stocks to their portfolios as they face difficulties raising high returns from domestic instruments when Japanese interest rates are locked at record low levels. \par \par \uc2  Net purchases of Japanese stocks by foreign investors, totalled 134.9 billion yen in June after net purchase of 931.7 billion yen in May, while their net buying of Japanese bonds totalled 481.9 billion yen, compared with net purchase of 894.9 billion yen in May. \par \par \uc2  Details in billions of yen are as follows (bonds include beneficiary certificates but exclude all bills): \par \par \uc2  FOREIGNERS INVESTMENT IN JAPAN (NET): \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        JUNE        MAY\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japanese stocks:     +134.9     +931.7\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japanese bonds:      +481.9     +894.9\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  JAPANESE INVESTMENT ABROAD (NET):\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        JUNE        MAY\par  Foreign stocks:      +646.1     +599.1\par  Foreign bonds:       +106.5     +995.8\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (breakdown by investors)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                 foreign stocks   foreign bonds\par  Life insurers      +18.9           -29.7\par  Banks              -20.5          +239.0\par  Trust banks       +629.7          -160.6\par  mutual funds       +11.6           +26.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Figures are rounded and do not include investment by brokerage firms.\par ($1=117.08 Yen) (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020712dy7c000bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
