\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Declines Against Yen and Other Major  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 900 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2002\par 00:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Absent action by Japanese finance authorities to prop it up, the dollar slid by almost a full yen Thursday and lost ground against its other major rivals, although a late surge by U.S. stocks helped haul the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off its session lows. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday in New York the dollar was lower against the yen, after breaking new 10-month lows in volatile trading and demolishing some key technical support levels. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also much lower against the pound and the Swiss franc, but little changed against the euro, which was hovering close to the 99-cents mark. \par \par \uc2  In what is becoming a familiar pattern, heightened worries about the quality of U.S. corporate accounts conspired to send the Dow Jones Industrial Average lower, although the session proved a roller-coaster ride, with the index sinking some 200 points at one point in the day only to pare its losses significantly by the close. \par \par \uc2  The stock market was "very choppy, with that unbelievable volatility characterizing the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as well," said Thomas Molloy, a trader at Bank Leumi in New York. \par \par \uc2  Early in the New York day, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  missed an opportunity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as the dollar was ticking higher against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , before the tide of dollar-negative sentiment returned, some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said. \par \par \uc2  In the wake of a disappointing weekly initial jobless claims report -- showing U.S. claims jumped above the psychologically important 400,000 mark to 403,000 -- and then a lower opening on equities markets, the dollar slid below 117 yen early in the day. At its low, the greenback was at 116.54 yen, its weakest point since Sept. 24, 2001. The euro also fell against the yen, hitting an intraday low of 115.21 yen as a wave of speculators tested whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although both Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Masaru Hayami and Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa warned that further yen strength would be inappropriate for the Japanese economy and that they would move to prevent a fall in the dollar to 115 yen, the market continued to press the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. \par \par \uc2  Some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said the pace of the dollar's slippage against the yen may not have been fast enough Thursday to galvanize the Finance Ministry into action. Nonetheless, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is becoming increasingly likely now, said David Mozina, director of global fixed-income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Banc of America Securities in New York. "Without a doubt this is a key level already," Mr. Mozina said. \par \par \uc2  Late in New York Thursday, the dollar was trading at 116.83 yen, down from 117.61 yen late Wednesday in New York. The euro was trading at 99.01 U.S. cents, up from 98.89 cents late Wednesday in New York. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.4803 francs, down from 1.4869 francs. Sterling was at $1.5556, up from $1.5462. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since May, spending roughly $30 billion in an effort to check the yen's appreciation and guard Japan's fledgling export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  Now that the dollar has slipped below a key technical level at 116.85 yen, that could pave the way for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slip toward 114.00 yen, some technical analysts said. Japanese policy makers and major Japanese exporters have consistently alluded to the 115 yen area as being a level where the profitability of manufacturers would be jeopardized. \par \par \uc2  While the early part of the day was dominated by the strong yen and an otherwise firmer dollar, sentiment once again gradually shifted to "more of a correction back to general dollar weakness driven by a crisis of confidence in investing in the U.S. in general," said Jeremy Fand, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Friedberg Mercantile Group in New York. \par \par \uc2  One mark of investor aversion to the greenback was the pound's jump to its strongest levels against the dollar since May 4, 2000, breaking resistance around $1.550 and attaining session highs around $1.5573. One sterling trader in New York said that the pound now has the potential to rise to $1.56. \par \par \uc2  Among peripheral \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Turkish lira was once again a focus of attention, amid continuing political turmoil. Turkish Economy Minister Kemal Dervis confirmed Thursday that he withdrew his resignation at the urging of Prime Minister Bulent Ecevit and President Ahmet Necdet Sezer. The lira sank back Thursday to the record lows it had touched the prior day, at around 1,706,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But after the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy lira for dollars, confirming the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time ever, the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recouped some losses, to levels around 1,680,000 in late New York dealings, but weaker than 1,663,037 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in ... trying to restore order to disorderly markets, without which the lira would have gone further [down]" said Stephen Leach, emerging markets strategist with Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020711dy7b005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
