\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Govt Upgrades Economic Assessment In July Report \b0\par\par\uc2 1,257 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2002\par 11:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's economy is showing patchy improvement as exports lift production and signs emerge that capital spending may hit bottom, the government said Thursday, upgrading its official assessment. \par \par \uc2  The improvement is expected to spread as recovering economies overseas, particularly in Asia, import more Japanese goods, although a rising yen and falling share prices pose risks for the vulnerable economy, the government said in a monthly report. \par \par \uc2  "There are signs the economy is picking up in some areas, though it remains in a severe state," the Cabinet Office said. \par \par \uc2  Last month's assessment, which was unchanged from May, said, "The economy has bottomed out, though it remains in a severe state." \par \par \uc2  Although the new assessment underscores relatively bright prospects for Japan as it emerges from its third recession in a decade, it also shows the economy is precariously dependent on overseas demand and susceptible to external shocks. \par \par \uc2  The report makes special note of risks, given the cloudy outlook for worldwide growth against a background of sharp declines in U.S. share prices and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The outlook for the global economy is increasingly uncertain due to recent declines in the U.S. dollar and stocks globally. There are concerns this will weigh on final demand in Japan," the report says. \par \par \uc2  The performance of the U.S. economy, the engine behind the current economic rebound in Asia, will remain a top concern for Japanese officials in the months ahead. \par \par \uc2  Falling U.S. share prices threaten to damp U.S. consumer sentiment and demand for goods from Japan. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen in theory hurts the Japanese economy by making its exports more expensive and deflating Japanese firms' overseas earnings when repatriated. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets seven times since late May to stem the rapid rise in the yen that has accompanied the dollar's fall. \par \par \uc2  But overwhelming downward pressure on the greenback has thwarted the MOF's efforts. So far it has only been able to slow the pace of the dollar's fall against the yen, not stop it. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has plummeted 13.2% against the yen from its year-high of Y135.19 in January. It has also plunged 16.5% from its high against the euro for the year, also marked in January. \par \par \uc2  Thursday afternoon in Tokyo, the dollar was trading around Y117.70. \par \par \uc2  Exports are leading Japan's mild recovery. \par \par \uc2  Reflecting this strength in exports, the Cabinet Office upgraded its assessment of the sector in July, adding the word "sharply" to its statement to read "exports are rising sharply, in particular to Asia." \par \par \uc2  The Cabinet Office also upgraded its view on production, saying improvements are more widespread than before, after the government reported industrial production soared 3.9% in May, the fourth straight increase and the biggest in almost 10 years. \par \par \uc2  "Improvement in production can be seen," Thursday's assessment says. Last month, the government saw only "improvement in some areas" of production. \par \par \uc2  Importantly, the government also Thursday upgraded its view of capital spending for the first time since March 2001. While acknowledging that capital investment is falling, the government added, "There are signs it will bottom out." \par \par \uc2  The government cited a slower rate of decline for machinery orders in the January-March period for its decision. Machinery orders generally serve as an indication of capital investment trends six months ahead. \par \par \uc2  A recovery in capital spending, the biggest component of gross domestic product after personal consumption, is considered essentially for a durable economy recovery. \par \par \uc2  The government left its assessments of consumer spending and housing investment unchanged at flat and weak, respectively. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Audrey McAvoy, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par audrey.mcavoy@dowjones.com\par   (This item originally ran around 0900 GMT Thursday.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's economy is showing patchy improvement as\par exports lift production and signs emerge that capital spending may hit\par bottom, the government said Thursday, upgrading its official assessment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The improvement is expected to spread as recovering economies\par overseas, particularly in Asia, import more Japanese goods, although a\par rising yen and falling share prices pose risks for the vulnerable\par economy, the government said in a monthly report.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are signs the economy is picking up in some areas, though it\par remains in a severe state," the Cabinet Office said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Last month's assessment, which was unchanged from May, said, "The\par economy has bottomed out, though it remains in a severe state."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the new assessment underscores relatively bright prospects\par for Japan as it emerges from its third recession in a decade, it also\par shows the economy is precariously dependent on overseas demand and\par susceptible to external shocks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The report makes special note of risks, given the cloudy outlook for\par worldwide growth against a background of sharp declines in U.S. share\par prices and the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The outlook for the global economy is increasingly uncertain due to\par recent declines in the U.S. dollar and stocks globally. There are\par concerns this will weigh on final demand in Japan," the report says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The performance of the U.S. economy, the engine behind the current\par economic rebound in Asia, will remain a top concern for Japanese\par officials in the months ahead.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Falling U.S. share prices threaten to damp U.S. consumer sentiment and\par demand for goods from Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A stronger yen in theory hurts the Japanese economy by making its\par exports more expensive and deflating Japanese firms' overseas earnings\par when repatriated.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets\par seven times since late May to stem the rapid rise in the yen that has\par accompanied the dollar's fall.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But overwhelming downward pressure on the greenback has thwarted the\par MOF's efforts. So far it has only been able to slow the pace of the\par dollar's fall against the yen, not stop it.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar has plummeted 13.2% against the yen from its year-high of\par Y135.19 in January. It has also plunged 16.5% from its high against the\par euro for the year, also marked in January.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thursday afternoon in Tokyo, the dollar was trading around Y117.70.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Fate Of Capex Key To Econ Recovery\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Exports are leading Japan's mild recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Reflecting this strength in exports, the Cabinet Office upgraded its\par assessment of the sector in July, adding the word "sharply" to its\par statement to read "exports are rising sharply, in particular to Asia."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Cabinet Office also upgraded its view on production, saying\par improvements are more widespread than before, after the government\par reported industrial production soared 3.9% in May, the fourth straight\par increase and the biggest in almost 10 years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Improvement in production can be seen," Thursday's assessment says.\par Last month, the government saw only "improvement in some areas" of\par production.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Importantly, the government also Thursday upgraded its view of capital\par spending for the first time since March 2001. While acknowledging that\par capital investment is falling, the government added, "There are signs it\par will bottom out."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government cited a slower rate of decline for machinery orders in\par the January-March period for its decision. Machinery orders generally\par serve as an indication of capital investment trends six months ahead.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A recovery in capital spending, the biggest component of gross\par domestic product after personal consumption, is considered essentially\par for a durable economy recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government left its assessments of consumer spending and housing\par investment unchanged at flat and weak, respectively.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Audrey McAvoy, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par audrey.mcavoy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020711dy7b000fh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
