\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Hayami:Rapid Yen Rise Not Good For Japan \b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2002\par 06:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami expressed support Thursday for the government's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a sharp surge in the yen may be harmful for Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  But Hayami told a parliamentary committee recent movements in dollar/yen rates appear to be stemming "not from a strong yen, but from the dollar being weak across the board." \par \par \uc2  But the yen has strengthened excessively regardless, and as that has negative effects for the Japanese economy, "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the dollar from falling is not a bad thing," he said. \par \par \uc2  Hayami said there was a chance the dollar would be sold on a global scale if investors flock out of U.S. assets on concerns over the U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  "There is a need to watch the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement on the (domestic) economy," Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, speaking at the same committee meeting, again clarified remarks he made last weekend regarding the possibility that the dollar may fall to Y115.00. \par \par \uc2  "I said that the dollar had fallen to around Y115-Y116 after the September attacks, but we must keep it from returning there," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister's remarks made in Copenhagen last weekend were initially interpreted by market players to mean that Tokyo would tolerate a fall in the dollar to Y115, sending the greenback sliding sharply lower against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa admitted however, that he has realized the difficulty of commenting on such issues as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and stock market, given the media attention it generates. \par \par \uc2  When asked about the scale of the ministry's most recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the New York Federal Reserve Bank on June 28, Shiokawa declined to answer. \par \par \uc2  On the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducting concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other Group of Seven industrialized nations in order to stem the dollar's fall, Senior Vice Minister for Financial Affairs Hidehisa Otsuji said that he couldn't comment given the sensitive nature of the issue. \par \par \uc2  But he said Japan is always in contact with other G7 nations with regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Hayami said that despite the yen's strength, he didn't see any immediate adverse impact on Japanese corporations. The BOJ, however, would have to watch very closely the possible effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates on the economy, he said. \par \par \uc2  A weaker dollar versus the yen reduces the repatriated earnings of Japanese firms, as well as making their exports less competitive overseas. \par \par \uc2  Noting that over 50% of the nation's exports were paid for in dollars, Hayami said Japan needed to get more of its exports denominated in yen, adding that some other nations have 70% to 80% of their exports based in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  BOJ Deputy Governor Sakuya Fujiwara, speaking at the same committee, added it is important to watch over the impact recent exchange movements will have on Japanese corporate sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Fujiwara said the BOJ's latest tankan survey of business sentiment released last week showed that corporate sentiment has been improving on the back of increased profits. But it also showed that firms remained cautious over business investment, Fujiwara said. \par \par \uc2  To gauge the outlook for corporate sentiment in coming months, the deputy BOJ governor said it would be necessary to monitor whether recent improvements in the manufacturing sector filter through to the non-manufacturing sector. \par \par \uc2  It will also be necessary to watch how \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other market movements affect corporate sentiment and the economy as a whole, he said. \par \par \uc2  -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020711dy7b0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
