\par\par \b \uc2  US Economic Outlook Good, But Bearishness Dwells:Barclays \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 784 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2002\par 22:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- U.S. economic prospects are solid even as a bearish investment environment remains, the Federal deficit grows and the dollar continues to weaken, according to senior analysts at Barclays Capital's second-half outlook presentation Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Growth in real disposable income has risen sharply during the first two months of 2002, giving a boost to consumer spending, said Henry Willmore, Barclays Capital's chief U.S. economist. \par \par \uc2  That's a good thing for the U.S. economy, as income growth "is more important (to spending) than job growth and, for the time being, the stock market," he said. \par \par \uc2  A combination of rising real wages, longer work hours being logged by employees - in lieu of new hires - and the federal tax cuts passed last year is leading to the higher disposable income, Willmore said. \par \par \uc2  As a result Willmore, whose bullish outlook for the U.S.  economy proved fairly accurate during the first half of the  year, is calling for gross domestic product to rebound to 5.0% this quarter from 3% in the second quarter, dipping again to 3% in the fourth quarter before rising again to 4.5% in each of the first two quarters next year. \par \par \uc2  Regarding the Federal Reserve's monetary policy, Willmore said the "Fed would like to see at least a couple of job reports where growth is at least 150,000 to stabilize  employment" before it hikes the Federal funds target rate,  which has remained 1.75% all year. Willmore therefore doesn't expect the Fed to raise rates until at least November. \par \par \uc2  Consumers, meanwhile, haven't been the only ones spending. Treasury borrowing will be up 72% in 2002 relative  to 2001, and up another 10% in 2003 from this year's level,  said Gemma Wright, Barclays' market strategist. \par \par \uc2  That will translate into monthly two-year note auctions at $27 billion a clip, $26 billion quarterly five-year note auctions and $25 billion of 10-year notes each quarter as well, she estimated. Issuance of Treasury Inflation Protected Securities will increase to $20 billion this year, 82% more than 2001's total, Wright said, adding that Treasury bond buybacks will also be suspended. \par \par \uc2  These increased borrowings will help lead to a $200 billion budget deficit this year, followed by a $170 billion  deficit next year, and a $50 billion shortfall in 2004, Wright said. \par \par \uc2  She predicted that after a 0.25 percentage- point rise in the Fed funds rates in the fourth quarter, the Fed will hike rates by a similar amount again in the first quarter of  next year. \par \par \uc2  But even with more Treasurys to purchase, foreign capital flows into the U.S. will continue to decline, said Jake Moore, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist. \par \par \uc2  "People seem to be losing confidence in the U.S. economic story," he said. \par \par \uc2  The slew of high profile corporate scandals this year combined with a huge funding requirement for the current account leaves the greenback vulnerable, he said. \par \par \uc2  As such, Moore said the euro could reach $1.05 by September, a level that reflects the enormously bearish outlook strategists have for the dollar. The euro currently fetches about $0.9888. \par \par \uc2  And the dollar will continue to drop versus the yen, with a move to Y115 appearing "more than likely" despite large scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, Moore said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's strength will put a crimp in Japan's exporting  - a linchpin for that country's attempted economic recovery  - shaving about 1% off of Japan's GDP, "which Japan cannot  afford to lose," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the emerging market sector, "The critical issue is when do we move to a new credit cycle?" according to Jose Barrionuevo, Barclays' head of Latin American research. \par \par \uc2  A new credit cycle for Latin America in particular will coincide with the U.S. economic recovery, when capital flows  into Latin America will increase, he said. \par \par \uc2  Right now, the key topic is Brazil, where the presidential election this fall has investors' attention. \par \par \uc2  A victory by either of the opposition candidates, Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva or Siro Gomes, "means a recession of 0.5% and a sharply weaker real" falling to BRL3.5 versus the dollar, Barrionuevo said. \par \par \uc2  But if the government's candidate, Jose Serra, should win, growth would be 1.2% and the real should close at BRL2.5 this year, Barrionuevo predicted. \par \par \uc2  With strong macro-economic progress in the region, "The  challenge for Latin America is the structural reforms that politicians have long avoided," he said. \par \par \uc2  -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248 \par \par \uc2  -tyler.lifton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020711dy7b0093t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
