\par\par \b \uc2  Yen Surges As  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Seen Reluctant To Order  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2002\par 21:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- In the face of strong dollar-negative headwinds, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be shifting, which could signal the yen has much more strength in store, U.S.-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2   strategists sense. \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar's slippage against the yen this week far below levels where the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  previously stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, there's been a hiatus in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 28. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday afternoon in New York, the dollar slid to its lows for the session and to its weakest point in ten months at around Y117.38, well below the Y118.30-Y118.70 range where most of the Japanese government's seven \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar since May have occurred. \par \par \uc2  The pause for breath since the most recent action on June 28, in spite of the dollar's continued erosion, may reflect a rethink among Japanese officials, reeling from the  sheer weight of the dollar's broad-based decline. Within weeks this tide has swept aside the effects of their recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  designed to weaken the yen in an effort to keep Japan's fledgling export-led recovery on track. \par \par \uc2  "After being run over by a locomotive several times, they have realized that maybe it is not a good idea to try to stop the train when it is running this fast," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Putnam Investments in Boston. In spite of the MOF's efforts, the dollar has slid about 9% against the yen since the start of May. \par \par \uc2  Hurt by a plentiful supply of U.S. corporate scandals, wilting stock markets and an exacerbation of worries about the burgeoning U.S. current account deficit, the dollar has wiped out any temporary gains against the yen driven by the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on which the Japanese government has now spent a total of more than $30 billion, according to rough independent estimates. \par \par \uc2  Upadhyaya expects that Japan's finance ministry, which acts in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets via the Bank of Japan, "will wait for a small pickup in the dollar - coupled with some signs of a weaker economy in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market's eyes." \par \par \uc2  That would follow a more textbook approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that central banks have often employed: bide your time until the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to be moving in the direction you want and jump on the bandwagon to give it added momentum. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Dollar Reprieve Not Guaranteed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It could be a long wait. Such a convenient reprieve for the dollar\par isn't an opportunity guaranteed to present itself, analysts reckon.\par Instead, against its own will, the MOF may be forced to once again defy\par the juggernaut of a sliding dollar, in spite of the growing recognition\par among market strategists that this tactic isn't proving effective.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "My feeling is that they will try to fight this (yen appreciation\par against the dollar) but in the short term are not going to be\par successful," said Paul Podolsky, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at\par FleetBoston Financial in Boston.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Contemplating that alternative scenario, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists are now\par openly musing whether Japan's tolerance bar of dollar weakness against\par the yen has moved lower, to an area somewhere between its post-Sept 11\par low of Y115.80 and Y117.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market "thinks there has been a change in\par policy," says Larry Greenberg, international economist at Ried Thunberg\par in Westport, Conn. Nevertheless, Greenberg said he remains  "unconvinced\par there has been any radical strategic change " by the MOF, maintaining\par his previously held view that for Japanese exporters, the "pain\par threshold" lies in the Y115.00 area to the dollar. "We may make a move\par toward that level," he said, but added that  "they won't wait until Y115\par to act."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the wake of some apparently confusing remarks by Finance Minister\par Masajuro Shiokawa over the past week, some expect that future\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will probably be at lower levels than before. If the\par dollar's steady decline persists,  the MOF might not step in before it\par sinks to the Y116.80 area, wrote Marc Chandler, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist at HSBC  in NY in a daily research note Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, Shiokawa said Japan wants to avoid the dollar's fall to\par around Y115.80. Then, in efforts to clarify  his remarks in Copenhagen\par on Saturday, he more or less repeated them, saying the dollar was in a\par downtrend and could indeed fall to that post-Sept. 11 low.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even though he hinted at further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by saying "we cannot\par afford to ignore a rapid change in exchange rates," Shiokawa's apparent\par flip-flop on official yen policy - and the very fact that he took the\par highly unusual step of mentioning a specific exchange rate level - has\par confused market participants about the Japanese government's  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Since these bewildering signals, some have started to take more note\par of remarks by other ministry officials, including Zembei Mizoguchi,\par director of finance in the ministry's International Bureau. Greenberg\par sees Mizoguchi as  a candidate to succeed Haruhiko Kuroda as the vice\par finance minister for international affairs, one of the key positions\par for Japan's statements about yen policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People feel that" Kuroda and Mizoguchi are  "more reliable...to\par listen to on policy" than Shiokawa, who "doesn't present a consistent\par message," said Greenberg.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4083 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020710dy7a00ct5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
