\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar nervously holds above 10-month low vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2002\par 09:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 10 (Reuters) - The dollar held just above its 10-month low against the yen in Asian trade on Wednesday amid wariness over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the damage caused by doubts over accounting practices in the U.S. weighing heavy. Most dealers were inclined to sell the dollar and only refrained from doing so because of a feeling that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to buy dollars at any minute. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar stays under strong downward pressure and without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it should be going down again," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  As of 0620 GMT, the dollar was at 117.90/96 yen against Tuesday's late U.S. level of 118.06 yen and the 10-month low of 117.72 hit earlier in New York. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment for the dollar remained bearish given weak U.S. stocks and worries over U.S. accounting, but the market lacked the energy to push it down further, especially as many traders were already well short of dollars, some dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some even suggested that any short-covering could push it up towards 119 yen because there were no major offers detected until that level. \par \par \uc2  Hanao, however, did not agree that short positions were mounting in the market. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign players, who have just started the second half of their business year, are in an early stage of building fresh short positions at the moment," Hanao said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been under renewed selling pressure since the start of the week after weekend comments by Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa that world financial authorities believed a weakening of the dollar was in progress. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least seven days since late May to counter the yen's rise, which it fears could derail a tentative export-led recovery in the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  Japan last interevened on June 28, when the dollar fell below 119 yen for the first time in nine months. \par \par \uc2  Because Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken place mostly when the dollar slipped to fresh lows, wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew after the dollar dipped to the new low on Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Back in Tokyo on Wednesday, senior Finance Ministry officials continued their jawboning to try to pep up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda said Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged while the ministry's international bureau chief, Zembei Mizoguchi, called market moves "not a favourable situation". \par \par \uc2  ACCOUNTING WOES ADD PRESSURE \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at 99.33/37 against 99.26 cents in late U.S. trade. Against the yen, it was at 117.09/14 yen against 117.20 in late U.S. \par \par \uc2  The euro is for now blocked by option-related sales around 99.50 in its attempt to reach parity with the dollar - a level not seen since February 2000. \par \par \uc2  The dollar took little comfort from an initiative unveiled by President George W. Bush on Tuesday to clean up corporate America, which proposed longer jail terms for corporate fraud and the enhancement of watchdog agencies. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said it was too early to judge the ramifications of the policy but that the announcement had failed to change investor sentiment on U.S. shares. \par \par \uc2  "As far as the dollar is concerned, it comes down to the fact that U.S. share prices fell on Tuesday," said Fumihiko Kawano, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks closed near the day's lows on Tuesday, with the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average off 1.93 percent at 9,096.09 and the Nasdaq composite down over 1.7 percent to 1,381.12, both near recent nadirs. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. share prices are threatening to fall to fresh lows. Investors' distrust is mounting," said Satoshi Tokuda, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Corp. \par \par \uc2  "There are worries that there could be more scandals, at least in the short term, if the clampdown gets serious," he said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Canadian dollar slipped after Standard and Poor's late on Tuesday eliminated five Canadian firms, along with two other foreign firms, from its benchmark S&P 500 index, replacing them with U.S. firms. \par \par \uc2  It was at C$1.5152/62 to the U.S. dollar compared with C$1.5122 in late Canadian trade. \par \par \uc2  Sterling backed off slightly but held firm at $1.5478/88 after rising to a 26-month high of $1.5491 on Tuesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bids prices at 0610 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   09 Jul. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9929   0.9926   +0.03    +4.56   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen        117.92   118.06   -0.12    +3.03   114.45\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    117.12   117.20   -0.07    +7.74   108.71\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.4830   1.4823   +0.05    -7.38   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr        1.5487   1.5481   