\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL YIELD: Bunds To Gain As Dlr Dims Foreign Tsy Bids \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2002\par 23:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar's demise is starting to  take the edge off foreigners' appetite for the world's highest quality fixed-income asset: U.S. Treasury bonds. \par \par \uc2  Among other data, proof that foreigners were net sellers  of Treasurys in April has alerted global bond fund managers  that the price premium Treasurys command over rival European sovereigns may soon evaporate. \par \par \uc2  A key element eroding their appeal is the declining dollar, which is not only detracting from the allure of U.S.  assets, but may also start to import inflationary pressures, eating away at the value of longer-dated bonds, global fixed-income strategists warn. \par \par \uc2  As a result, many are betting that the spread between U.S. and European bond yields will shift, with European sovereign debt standing to be a chief beneficiary. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark 10-year bunds, a proxy for European government  bonds, were yielding 4.99% late Tuesday, 14 basis points higher than the 4.75% for the equivalent 10-year U.S. government bond. At current prices, bunds are cheaper than Treasurys, but that could soon flip-flop, as global investors demand for non-U.S. fixed income continues to rise. \par \par \uc2  "Now, as the dollar gets clobbered, foreign investors in U.S. Treasurys are getting whacked," as the value of their portfolios decline along with the greenback, said David Mozina, director of global fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  Betting that Treasury yields will rise higher than those  of bunds is "a convincing trade," says Mary Ann Hurley, senior U.S. government bond trader in Seattle with brokerage  D.A. Davidson. Because Treasury yields have already fallen to such low levels and "given the problems with the dollar,  it will be difficult for longer-dated Treasurys to extend gains," now, said Hurley, which she expects will send more flows into European sovereigns. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Key Harbinger Of Sentiment Change\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar has shed about 10.4% versus the euro and 11.1% versus the\par yen since the start of the year, hurt by escalating worries about the\par burgeoning U.S. current account  deficit and rocked by a series of U.S.\par corporate accounting  scandals and concerns about the pace and vitality\par of the rebound in the U.S. economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some portfolio flow data since May has already indicated  that\par foreigners have begun to sell U.S. equities and nongovernment bonds,\par with the tide turning in favor of European and Japanese assets. A more\par important indicator that foreign investor interest in U.S. assets has\par really done an about-turn would be any kind of retreat, no matter how\par slight, from the Treasury market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. government bond market is a critical bastion of  foreign\par support for U.S. assets. Foreigners own about 40% of the Treasury\par market, 24% of U.S. corporate bonds and 13% of equities, with the total\par value amounting to about $8.4 trillion, equivalent to around 80% of U.S.\par gross domestic product, estimates Michael Woolfolk, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist\par with the Bank of New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Underscoring the relationship between foreign investment  in bonds and\par the dollar, net foreign investment flows out of U.S. fixed income\par securities are having a very direct impact on the dollar's descent. This\par "can explain 75% of the variation in the movement in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par (against the euro) over the last three months," said Woolfolk, citing\par Bank of New York proprietary data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To be sure, as of now, even though foreigners have been selling U.S.\par bonds, there's little sign of a major exodus out of the Treasury market,\par even though foreigners made net sales of Treasurys amounting to $11.8\par billion in April, Treasury Department data released last week showed,\par compared  with an inflow of $13.9 billion in March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's a drop in the ocean compared with total foreign ownership of\par Treasurys, noted Hurley. But she added that the   trend of net foreign\par outflows will continue and may accelerate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Helping Hands From Central Banks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One source of support for Treasurys is coming from central banks\par increasing their stock of U.S. government bonds. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , via \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and some other Asian central\par banks, in their efforts to brake their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains and keep\par domestic exporters competitive, have bought dollars and parked the newly\par acquired funds in shorter-dated Treasurys.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet while Asian central banks are "forced buyers of dollar assets,"\par and their purchases play in Treasurys favor, there's a hefty\par countervailing force that will increasingly hurt U.S. government bonds,\par said Tim Bond, chief global fixed income strategist with Barclays\par Capital in London. "Private sector institutions are downgrading their\par dollar exposure," in Treasurys, equities and nongovernment bonds, Bond\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As foreigners pare their weightings in U.S. assets, the dollar will\par continue to retreat, which in turn will tend to stoke higher\par inflationary pressures in the U.S. via imports,  driving bond yields\par higher, says Bond.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He expects the benchmark 10-year Treasury's yield - which moves\par inversely to its price - to jump to around 6.0% by the end of the year,\par from 4.75% Tuesday. Equivalent bund yields will rise proportionately\par much less, to 5.3% over the  same period, with European sovereign debt\par clearly outperforming the U.S., Bond forecasts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It looks compelling (that investors) should move out of the Treasury\par market" into other, currently higher yielding global fixed-income\par assets, notably U.K. government  bonds or gilts, and bunds, Mozina said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As global bond fund managers cast outside the U.S. for potentially\par better performing securities, that in turn "could have a snowballing\par effect and keep the dollar in the  downward trajectory it is already\par in," Hurley said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4083 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020709dy7900ete\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
