\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa says dollar fall to 115 yen unacceptable. \b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2002\par 15:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 9 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa sought on Tuesday to clarify his remarks at the weekend which had sent the dollar diving against yen, saying a big drop was unacceptable and hinted at action to curb the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa, whose unusual comments at the weekend referring to monetary authorities' expectations of a possible fall in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  towards 115 yen sparked turmoil in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week, said his intention had been to say the dollar's fall must be stopped. \par \par \uc2  "What I said was that there was such a rumour, such a trend, and we must stop it before it gets there," Shiokawa told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  "We have not said that we will tolerate it (a fall towards 115 yen). We are saying it would be problematic if such a thing happened." \par \par \uc2  Asked if a slow, gradual move towards the 115 level would be acceptable, Shiokawa raised his voice, telling a reporter: "Don't ask me such a stupid question." \par \par \uc2  HINTS OF ACTION \par \par \uc2  Worried that a higher yen could choke off \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led recovery, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at least seven times over the past two months, including one occasion in which it enlisted the help of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Federal Reserve. The dollar had appeared to be stabilising last week, but Shiokawa's comments wiped out the hard-won gains. The dollar hit a fresh nine-month low of 118.32 yen in early Sydney trade and was trading around 118.70 yen on Tuesday afternoon, more than one percent below levels late last week. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa later told a parliamentary committee the government may need to consider taking measures in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should the speed of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements become too rapid. \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentally, movements should be left to the markets," Shiokawa told a Lower House fiscal finance committee. "But rapid movements are problematic and we are closely monitoring the speed of such moves. In some cases we may need to consider some measures." \par \par \uc2  He also said that while the United States upheld a "strong dollar" policy, actual movements were not necessarily reflecting that. "We are going though an adjustment phase globally." \par \par \uc2  FOOT IN MOUTH \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa, 80, who has gained a reputation for putting his foot in his mouth, said on Saturday after a two-day Asia-Europe Meeting (ASEM) in Copenhagen that financial authorities believed a weakening of the dollar towards September lows was in progress. \par \par \uc2  "Currently, the generally shared view among financial authorities is that dollar weakness is indeed in progress. The majority think the dollar could go as far as levels hit immediately after September 11," Shiokawa told a news conference after the ASEM. \par \par \uc2  "At that time the dollar's level against the yen was around 115. I maintain that even if (the dollar) is heading to that level, a certain time needs to be spent and it should not be reaching that level abruptly." \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary authorities take the stance that exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  In parliament on Tuesday, the outspoken Shiokawa also gave his opinion on a recent firming the Chinese yuan. \par \par \uc2  "China is putting efforts into adapting itself to the WTO regime, and I think that is being reflected in the markets," he said in response to an opposition lawmaker who suggested that Tokyo call on Beijing to raise the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying that it was presently undervalued. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020709dy7900qut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
