\par\par \b \uc2  ODJ World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Skids On Corporate Jitters, Shiokawa \b0\par\par\uc2 1,012 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2002\par 23:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones Commodities Service \par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Repeating story from earlier)\par    By John Parry\par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- After enjoying a burst of relief late last week, the\par dollar resumed its descent Monday, sliding 1.4% against both the euro and the\par yen as the specter of corporate accounting doubts once again returned to plague\par U.S. equity markets.\par   "The sharpness of the move lower across the board confirms that last week's\par dollar bounce was corrective in nature and not the end of the downtrend," said\par Marc Chandler, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with HSBC in New York.\par   Beyond these domestic factors, the greenback was also sent tumbling by a top\par Japanese official's comments that pushed the dollar down nearly two yen during\par the global day.\par   After putting in a stronger performance Friday, the dollar was sinking back\par to 26-month lows against the euro and nine month troughs against the yen\par Monday.\par   Nervousness about corporate accounting festered in both the equity and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, with U.S. stock market averages in negative territory\par for much of the session, after revelations that pharmaceutical company Merck  &\par Co.'s recorded $12.4 billion in revenue over the past three years that a\par subsidiary never actually collected. The Dow Jones Industrial Average ended\par down 1.1%, with the Nasdaq Composite losing 2.9%.\par   Also weighing on U.S. stocks was news that Reliant Energy and Reliant\par Resources each restated their revenue for  1999 through 2001. "The tone without\par a doubt is very negative" in the U.S. stock market, said David Mozina, head  of\par global fixed income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with Banc of America Securities in New\par York. "The news on Merck is another nail in the coffin" for equities and has\par consolidated the view that "the weaker dollar trend is still in place," Mozina\par said.\par   Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa's comments over the weekend were\par also a major trigger for the yen's ascent, to levels where \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders are\par once again on high alert for possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par   On Thursday, Shiokawa said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wants to avoid the dollar's fall to its\par post-Sept. 11 lows, around Y115.80. Then, in efforts to clarify his remarks in\par Copenhagen on Saturday, he more or less repeated them, saying the "majority" of\par monetary authorities at a meeting of European and Asian finance ministers in\par Copenhagen believed the dollar will fall to the that level.\par   Even though he hinted at further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by saying "we cannot afford to\par ignore a rapid change in exchange rates," Shiokawa's apparent flip-flop on\par official yen policy - and the very fact that he took the highly unusual step of\par mentioning a specific exchange rate level - has confused market participants\par about the Japanese government's   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy. "For now, we stand\par aside on the yen, because Japan's policy is so inscrutable," said Paul\par Podolsky, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at FleetBoston Financial in Boston.\par   The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , acting via the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on\par seven occasions since late May in an effort to weaken the yen and nurture\par Japan's nascent export-led recovery. The yen was also reinforced by a report\par that core machinery orders in Japan rose 0.2% last month instead of falling\par 2.3% as expected. This contributed to hopes that the economy there is bottoming\par out after all.\par   The euro meanwhile, was charting a strong advance against the dollar, as the\par prevailing negative sentiment toward the greenback held sway, even in the face\par of fairly lackluster euro-zone economic data. Sluggish economic growth  in\par Germany, Europe's biggest economy, "has been insufficient to boost employment,"\par part of a flurry of euro-zone economic news that "is hardly the stuff that\par ought  to encourage investors," noted HSBC Chandler.\par   Fostering the impression that speculators have played quite a role in the\par dollar's demise, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said\par Monday that "some  of the recent weakness could be related to a build up in\par speculative accounts, with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders betting on a rise in the euro."\par   It noted that on a number of previous occasions, the dollar has bounced back\par from such speculative pressure, but that it may not do so this time. "Growing\par evidence indicates that foreign appetite for U.S. financial assets is waning,"\par the OECD said.\par   "Short term, there is more euro strength and dollar weakness to come," said\par Tim Stewart, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York, adding\par that European policy-makers would be comfortable if the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strengthens to between parity and $1.05, as he expects.\par   HSBC's Chandler said that the euro could make a run at parity with the dollar\par as early as this week, especially if the U.S. retail sales report for June were\par to come in weaker-than-expected.\par   Late Monday in New York, the dollar was at Y118.57, not far above its\par intraday low and down steeply from Y120.24 late Friday in New York. The euro\par was trading at $0.9867, sharply up from $0.9732 late Friday. Against the Swiss\par franc, the dollar was at CHF1.4885, down from CHF1.5062 late  Friday. Sterling\par was at $1.5373, up from $1.5216.\par   Among other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Polish zloty came under renewed pressure, falling\par to its lowest level since former finance minister Marek Belka abruptly\par announced he was quitting last week. On Saturday, Grzegorz Kolodko was sworn\par in. The dollar reached PLZ4.1875 late Monday in Europe, up from around\par PLZ4.1600 Friday afternoon.\par   Financial markets participants are worried about more expansive fiscal\par policies and a potential zloty devaluation as a result of the ministerial\par change.\par   Also tipping the scales against the zloty Monday, a Hungarian interest rate\par hike triggered a selloff of zloty-denominated assets, but left the forint\par trading in a fairly narrow range.\par    By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par    (Tyler Lifton in New York and David McQuaid in Warsaw contributed to this\par report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2123GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djcs000020020708dy7800tmp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
