\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa's Confusing Comments Wrong-Foot  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Market \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,052 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2002\par 18:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- He may not have meant to, but Japan's finance minister has given \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders a green light to try to push the dollar down to levels close to Y115.00. \par \par \uc2  Despite the ministry's subsequent efforts to turn the light back to red, there's now a stronger chance the dollar will accelerate its tumble against the yen, possibly triggering further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to halt its slide, U.S.-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said Monday. \par \par \uc2  Twice in the past week, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa has said the yen could firm to levels last seen immediately after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, he said Japan wants to avoid the dollar's fall to that level - around Y115.80. Then, in efforts to clarify his remarks in Copenhagen on Saturday, he more or less repeated them, saying the dollar was in a downtrend and  could fall to its post-Sept. 11 low. \par \par \uc2  Even though he hinted at further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by saying "we cannot afford to ignore a rapid change in exchange rates," Shiokawa's apparent flip-flop on official yen policy - and the very fact that he took the highly unusual step of mentioning a specific exchange rate level - has confused market participants about the Japanese government's  \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, leaving many hesitant to put on large positions in the dollar against the yen either way. \par \par \uc2  "For now, we stand aside on the yen, because Japan's policy is so inscrutable," said Paul Podolsky, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at FleetBoston Financial in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Partly as a result of the mixed signals from Japan's finance ministry, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants have tended to steer clear of making big volume trades of the dollar against the yen, preferring instead to focus on the euro against the dollar for larger trades, said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Morgan Stanley in New York. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on seven occasions since late May in an effort to weaken the yen and nurture Japan's nascent export-led recovery. Over most of that time period, a host of Japanese officials have sought to also talk the yen weaker, even though their efforts have reaped only minimal success. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Sending Mixed Signals\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Shiokawa, the top diplomat on Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, has\par on several occasions only sowed seeds of doubt in traders' minds about\par the Japanese government's official yen policy and the whereabouts of any\par perceived yen  line in the sand that might trigger more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par While repeatedly telling reporters that the ministry was watching\par exchange rates closely, he said late last month that the government\par shouldn't always \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. That comment pushed\par the yen stronger, convincing some traders that unable to turn round the\par tide of negative dollar sentiment, the finance ministry had toned  down\par its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plans.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the MOF then followed up Shiokawa's comments by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on\par June 28 via the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Federal Reserve. This has left\par traders even more reluctant to conclude that Shiokawa's latest comments\par might mean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is less likely.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's ironic that Shiokawa's comments," by precipitating the sharp\par fall in the dollar against the yen in Monday's global session "could be\par the catalyst for (another) round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Shahab Jalinoos,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with UBS Warburg in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late morning in New York, the dollar was trading at Y118.61, close to\par its global session low and sharply down from Y120.24 late Friday in New\par York. If the dollar were to fall later Monday well below Y118.50 to\par levels close to Y118.00 and the Japanese authorities don't react "you\par could  make a pretty clear case they are no longer interested in\par propping up the dollar," Jalinoos said. But at the same time, Jalinoos\par and others don't expect Japan to allow that to occur.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Paradoxically, most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants  believe\par recent remarks were "simply a mistake on Shiokawa's part," believing he\par didn't mean to push the dollar significantly lower against the yen even\par though that has been the effect, Jalinoos said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We do not believe that the MOF's policy has changed,"  wrote Robert\par Sinche, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with Citibank in New York in a\par research note Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Unusually Direct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par What's more, Shiokawa was also stating the obvious - governments can't\par control the massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. With the dollar in the\par midst of a broad decline, he also said "the majority" of monetary\par authorities at a meeting of European and Asian finance ministers in\par Copenhagen believed the dollar will fall to the post-Sept. 11 lows. But,\par it's still highly unusual for a finance minister to comment directly on\par a specific exchange rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This isn't the first time that Mr. Shiokawa has surprised the markets\par with an atypically direct statement,"  noted Anne Parker Mills, senior\par U.S. economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "The problem with\par this statement was that it seems to suggest a different `line in the\par sand' than the market had in mind."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For now, damage control attempts to rectify the impression of\par Shiokawa's remarks by Administrative Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Mutoh\par have led \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders to tone down their expectations on the extent\par of any decline in  the dollar against the yen before the BOJ would step\par in again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mutoh said that exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals,\par but added that if rates move too suddenly, Japan is prepared to take\par "appropriate action", taken by many to signal willingness to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par further in order to cap the yen's ascent against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the very fact that so many public officials in Japan  feel\par compelled to talk about the yen is only fueling market  confusion. "What\par exactly the Japanese are up to is a bit ambiguous, as Mutoh said they\par would take `decisive' action on the yen, which at this point, can only\par be thought to mean  joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," noted Podolsky.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Jonathan Soble in Tokyo contributed to this report)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020708dy78007vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
