\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar bears emboldened by Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  policy doubts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2002\par 16:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 8 (Reuters) - Bets against the dollar were back in vogue on Monday after Japanese finance minister Masajuro Shiokawa seemed to admit that even \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to halt the greenback's slide. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said at the weekend that Japan, which has been selling yen for dollars to maintain competitiveness, could not artificially manage exchange rates and that most global policy-makers expected the dollar to extend its decline. \par \par \uc2  "It's rich for Japan to say it cannot artificially manipulate exchange rates when that it exactly what it has been trying to do," said Tony Norfield, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  "Shiokawa should probably just have kept his mouth shut." Accusations of "own goal" swept trading floors as the dollar tumbled more than one percent on Monday to 118.60 yen, retesting recent nine-month lows. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a break of key chart support at 118.30 could send the dollar back to levels last seen in the wake of the September 11 attacks, and possibly beyond. \par \par \uc2  Concern that the dollar's slide against the yen could derail Japan's fragile export-led recovery has prompted Japanese authorities to wade into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on at least seven days since late May. \par \par \uc2  Japan stepped up its firepower just over a week ago when it asked both the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Federal Reserve to sell yen on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  DANGEROUS GROUND \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the near-term outlook for the dollar would depend on whether investors felt Shiokawa's comments were a slip of the tongue or whether Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy had shifted. \par \par \uc2  "Japan is on dangerous territory," said Neil MacKinnon, chief economist at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt management group ECU. \par \par \uc2  "Shiokawa's comments have pulled the rug from under the dollar and given bears a green light to sell." \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed than the dollar could fall sharply if Japanese authorities were no longer perceived to be prepared to pick a fight with the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  While the euro, Swiss franc and sterling have recently powered to their highest levels against the dollar in more than two years, the yen has so far not risen above nine-month highs against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "What Shiokawa has done is to highlight that global policy-makers are biased to see the dollar lower," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  "With the exception of Japan, global policy-makers seem to be relatively happy with the dollar's decline." \par \par \uc2  DOWNWARD SPIRAL? \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg predicts the dollar will fall to 117.5 yen in the next three months and 115 yen by the end of the year, but it has not changed its forecasts in the wake of Shiokawa's comments. \par \par \uc2  "We were bearish on the dollar in any case," said Jalinoos. \par \par \uc2  ABN Amro's Norfield said the risk of further losses for the dollar in the near-term had increased and predicted that the dollar could easily fall to 117 yen in the next few days. "The dollar is already below the level at which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it may be more difficult to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now," he said. \par \par \uc2  He added that the prospect of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar - an idea brought up recently by Horst Koehler, managing director of the International Monetary Fund - was extremely unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "We would have to see another 10 percent drop in the dollar in a matter of weeks and complete mayhem in financial markets before central banks would cooperate to buy dollars." \par \par \uc2  The dollar has already fallen roughly 10 percent against the euro and the yen since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020708dy7800t63\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
