\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar sinks after Japan's Shiokawa sows doubt. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 973 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2002\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 8 (Reuters) - The dollar sank across the board in Asia on Monday, hurt by surprise comments by Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa that world financial authorities believed a weakening of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in progress. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa, speaking on Saturday after an Asia-Europe Meeting (ASEM) in Copenhagen, also said Japan cannot artificially manipulate exchange rates, although it has repeatedly stepped in to prop up the fading dollar in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Back in Tokyo, Japanese authorities were quick to play down Shiokawa's comments on Monday, suggesting a lack of policy coordination, but the damage had already been done. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell more than a yen to 119.00 yen - its lowest level in 10 days, after rising from multi-year lows during the last week. \par \par \uc2  The euro firmed against the dollar to 98.56/61 cents, well up from 97.18 in late U.S. trade. Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was slightly firmer at 117.29 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dollar bulls were also disappointed after it failed to extend a mini-rally after Friday's jumps in U.S. share prices. \par \par \uc2  Despite a surge of nearly five percent in the Nasdaq and a 3.58 percent gain in the Dow Jones industrial average, its biggest percentage gain this year, the dollar peaked at 120.40 yen in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "I think some investors who had built long positions on the good performance of U.S. stocks are selling after seeing that the dollar's rise was limited," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  In comments that seemed to work against his government's recent attempts to keep the yen competitive, Shiokawa said world financial authorities mostly believed the dollar could fall to around 115 yen, a level hit immediately after the September 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  But he added: "I maintain that even if (the dollar) is heading to that level, a certain amount of time needs to be spent and it should not reach that level abruptly." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen on seven occasions since late May to stem the yen's rise, which has been threatening to undermine Japan's export-driven recovery from recession. \par \par \uc2  "Shiokawa's comments seem to suggest that Japanese government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to smooth the dollar's decline, rather than to boost the dollar's value," said Masamichi Koike, senior vice president at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the ministry's international bureau, told reporters on Monday that Shiokawa's comments simply stated the market's views - a remark seen as an attempt to dilute the finance minister's statement. \par \par \uc2  ACCOUNTING WOES MOUNT \par \par \uc2  Many dealers also cited yet another accounting discrepancy at a U.S. firm, this time at Houston-based energy trader and electricity generator Reliant Resources Inc as weighing on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Reliant Resources said last Friday it had erased $7.9 billion in revenues from its accounts for the last three years, eliminating the effects of so-called "round-trip" transactions that were used to inflate revenues. \par \par \uc2  As the company had already been under investigation by the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, the news was hardly a surprise. But it was another sign of how widespread accounting could be, fraying already nervous sentiment ahead of a slew of U.S. corporate earnings announcements in the coming weeks. \par \par \uc2  The market reacted little on Monday to a slightly better-than-expected reading for Japan's machinery orders. \par \par \uc2  Private-sector core machinery orders - a key gauge of corporate capital spending - rose 0.2 percent in May from previous month, beating expectations for a 2.8 percent fall. \par \par \uc2  Many traders expect the dollar to face more pressure in European and U.S. markets. \par \par \uc2  Wariness of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to grow as the dollar approaches its recent nine-month low of 118.36, despite Shiokawa's remarks. \par \par \uc2  "It think there will be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar breaks its recent low of 118.36 yen. I'm not sure it will reach 115. Mr Shiokawa may have made the comments without liaising with (the ministry's) \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  division," said Koichi Abe, manager at Aozora Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading section. \par \par \uc2  Others think the Finance Ministry may take a more hands-off stance. \par \par \uc2  "Financial authorities approve a gradual decline in the dollar and I think it could fall to around 115 yen by the end of September," said Umemoto of Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  "But a sharp fall, such as going under 118 yen today, would prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. (With additional reporting by Kazunori Takada) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0556 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   05 Jul. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9859   0.9718   +1.45   +10.73   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        118.93